window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Low Back Pain I","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what do the TART criteria for diagnosing somatic dysfunction stand for?","choices":{"A":"Tension, Atrophy, Restriction of motion, and Trigger points found on palpation of the region","B":"Tenderness, Atrophy, Rotation, and Tone changes found on segmental testing","C":"Tissue texture changes, positional Asymmetry, Restriction of motion, and Tenderness","D":"Tissue tension, Asymmetry, Rigidity, and Temperature changes of the region","E":"Texture, Alignment, Range of motion, and Tone changes of the paraspinals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists the TART criteria as Tissue texture changes, positional Asymmetry, Restriction of motion, and local Tenderness, and says somatic dysfunction is diagnosed by identifying at least some of them.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: somatic dysfunction is diagnosed by identifying at least some of the TART criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the physician's primary role in supporting the body's inherent capacity to heal?","choices":{"A":"To eliminate mechanical and physiological barriers to homeostatic recovery","B":"To suppress symptoms with scheduled medication until the patient recovers","C":"To replace the body's own self-regulatory mechanisms with external control","D":"To restrict activity and prescribe rest until the pain fully resolves","E":"To correct structural findings on the spine without regard to function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that the body possesses self-regulatory and self-healing mechanisms, and that the physician's primary role is to eliminate mechanical and physiological barriers to support the patient's homeostatic recovery.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the osteopathic tenet that the body is capable of self-regulation and self-healing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is somatic dysfunction defined?","choices":{"A":"A structural abnormality that is visible on plain radiographs of the lumbar spine, pelvis, and sacrum, with scoliosis","B":"Weakness confined to the myotome of one lumbar nerve root, with a matching dermatomal sensory loss","C":"Pain that persists beyond the expected healing time of twelve weeks, with depression and anxiety","D":"A bony deformity that does not respond to manual treatment of the region, with loss of motion","E":"Impaired or altered function of the body framework and its related vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course defines somatic dysfunction as impaired or altered function of the body framework, meaning skeletal, arthrodial, myofascial, and related vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the definition extends beyond the skeleton to the vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements of the body framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which laboratory tests are indicated to evaluate infection, systemic inflammatory conditions, or spondyloarthropathies?","choices":{"A":"Serum calcium and alkaline phosphatase levels with phosphorus","B":"Serum uric acid and creatinine levels with urea nitrogen","C":"Serum vitamin D and parathyroid hormone levels together","D":"Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","E":"Serum lipid panel and fasting glucose with hemoglobin A1c"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists the inflammatory markers ESR and CRP as indicated to evaluate infection, systemic inflammatory conditions, or spondyloarthropathies.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: routine laboratory testing is not recommended for initial mechanical back pain without red flags.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is associated with early, non-indicated MRI within 4 to 6 weeks of onset of acute low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Faster recovery, better outcomes, and lower costs of care","B":"Longer disability, worse outcomes, and unnecessary costs","C":"Shorter disability, fewer surgeries, and earlier return to work","D":"Unchanged disability, unchanged outcomes, and unchanged costs","E":"Improved function, earlier detection, and reduced medication use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that early, non-indicated imaging such as MRI within 4 to 6 weeks is associated with increased length of disability, worse outcomes, and unnecessary medical costs or surgeries.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: guidelines recommend against routine or early imaging for acute low back pain without focal deficits or red flags.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does a radiolucent line on the 45-degree oblique view of the lumbar spine indicate?","choices":{"A":"Metastatic disease shown as a missing pedicle on the film","B":"Spondylolisthesis shown as anterior vertebral slippage on it","C":"Spondylolysis shown as a collar on the Scotty dog's neck","D":"Reduced lumbar lordosis measured on the Cobb angle view","E":"Psoas muscle border loss shown on the AP view of the pelvis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On the 45-degree oblique view, a radiolucent line through the pars interarticularis forms a collar on the neck of the Scotty dog and indicates spondylolysis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lateral view evaluates spondylolisthesis, and the AP view can show scoliosis and loss of a pedicle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what makes the nucleus pulposus incompressible?","choices":{"A":"Dense concentric lamellae of collagen fibers","B":"A high collagen content cross-linked by elastin fibers","C":"Calcified cartilaginous endplates above and below","D":"A high water content bound by proteoglycans","E":"Elastic fibers of the adjacent ligamentum flavum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The nucleus pulposus is highly hydrated and incompressible because of its high water content, which is bound by a matrix of proteoglycans synthesized by a sparse population of cells in the nucleus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the annulus fibrosus is the tough outer ring of concentric lamellae that contains the nucleus's hydrostatic pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does the intervertebral disc obtain its nutrients?","choices":{"A":"By direct arterial branches that enter the annulus fibrosus from outside","B":"By diffusion from the capillaries of the adjacent vertebral endplates","C":"By lymphatic drainage that moves through the nucleus pulposus itself","D":"By perfusion from segmental arteries that run within the nucleus matrix","E":"By absorption from the surrounding cerebrospinal fluid of the thecal sac"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The disc is entirely avascular and relies on diffusion of nutrients from the capillaries of the adjacent vertebral endplates. It also lacks lymphatic scavenger cells, so metabolic waste accumulates over time.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: insufficient diffusion starts the sequence of nutritional failure and disc degeneration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which part of a healthy intervertebral disc is innervated?","choices":{"A":"The central nucleus pulposus","B":"The inner third of the annulus fibrosus","C":"The full thickness of the annulus fibrosus","D":"The cartilaginous vertebral endplates","E":"The outer third of the annulus fibrosus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that, in a healthy disc, the outer third of the annulus fibrosus is the innervated portion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the outer third of the healthy annulus is the innervated portion of the disc.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which key muscle group is tested to assess the L4 nerve root?","choices":{"A":"Hip adductors for adduction of the thigh","B":"Tibialis anterior for ankle dorsiflexion","C":"Hamstring muscles for flexion of the knee","D":"Quadriceps femoris for extension of the knee","E":"Iliopsoas for flexion of the hip joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the course's table, the L4 root has the tibialis anterior as its key muscle, assessed by ankle dorsiflexion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's table pairs each lumbar root with a dermatome, a key muscle, a reflex, and a functional test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which deep tendon reflex is used to localize the L4 nerve root?","choices":{"A":"The Achilles reflex","B":"The cremasteric reflex","C":"The tibialis posterior reflex","D":"The patellar reflex","E":"The bulbocavernosus reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists the patellar reflex for the L4 root, and notes that the L3 patellar reflex is variable.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reflexes localize nerve root compression alongside dermatomal sensation and myotomal strength.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which areas show sensory loss in saddle anesthesia?","choices":{"A":"The perianal and perineal regions","B":"The anterior mid-thigh region","C":"The anterior lower thigh to the knee","D":"The medial leg and inner calf","E":"The lateral edge of the foot and heel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course defines saddle anesthesia as sensory loss in the perianal and perineal regions. The course also lists a digital rectal examination, bulbocavernosus and anal wink reflexes, and postvoid residual volume for cauda equina syndrome.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency requiring immediate MRI and decompression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the effect of incorporating abdominal diaphragm myofascial release in nonspecific low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Resolution of a herniated disk protrusion on repeat imaging","B":"Correction of the lumbar lordosis measured on radiographs","C":"Statistically significant improvements in pain and disability","D":"Elimination of a leg length discrepancy at the hip level","E":"Improvement in lumbar range of motion with no change in pain scores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that myofascial release of the abdominal diaphragm yields statistically significant improvements in pain and disability in patients with nonspecific low back pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the abdominal and pelvic diaphragms are part of the respiratory-circulatory model.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which techniques treat iliolumbar ligament syndrome highly effectively?","choices":{"A":"Side-lying thrust, rib raising, or lumbar traction therapy","B":"Muscle energy, rib raising, or the splenic pump technique","C":"Still technique, mesenteric lift, or percussion vibrator therapy","D":"Cranial vault hold, lymphatic pump, or soft tissue articulation with stretch","E":"Counterstrain, direct inhibition, or ligamentous articular strain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that iliolumbar ligament syndrome is treated highly effectively with counterstrain using an indirect approach, direct inhibition, or ligamentous articular strain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the tender point lies at the iliac insertion, just superior and lateral to the PSIS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does osteopathy in the cranial field help patients with chronic low back pain and central sensitization?","choices":{"A":"It down-regulates sympathetic activity, improves sleep latency, and addresses dural tension to the sacrum","B":"It lengthens the iliolumbar ligaments, restores lumbar lordosis, and reduces the paraspinal hypertonicity","C":"It reduces disk herniation, relieves nerve root compression, and restores the normal disk height","D":"It increases hamstring flexibility, corrects the leg length difference, and normalizes the gait pattern","E":"It strengthens the abdominal core, improves postural endurance, and reduces the spinal loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic back pain with central sensitization, sleep disturbances, and depression, osteopathy in the cranial field targets the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and primary respiratory mechanism to down-regulate sympathetic activity, improve sleep latency, and address dural tensions transmitted to the sacrum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the cranial approach targets the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and the primary respiratory mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with low back pain has fear-avoidance beliefs, anxiety, and poor social support. According to the course, which osteopathic model addresses these findings?","choices":{"A":"The metabolic model controlling inflammation through diet and nutrition","B":"The behavioral model integrating coping strategies and psychosocial yellow flags","C":"The neurological model normalizing autonomic tone and facilitation","D":"The respiratory-circulatory model optimizing diaphragmatic excursion and lymph flow","E":"The biomechanical model correcting posture, gait, and joint mechanics"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The behavioral model integrates coping strategies, screens for psychosocial yellow flags such as fear-avoidance beliefs, anxiety, and depression, and leverages social support systems.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the biomechanical, neurological, metabolic, and respiratory-circulatory models complete the five models of osteopathic patient care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a pelvic dysfunction and low back pain, and the DO also examines her thoracic spine, ribs, and lower extremities. According to the course, why?","choices":{"A":"Local lumbosacral dysfunction can trigger compensatory strain through the myofascial kinetic chain","B":"The lumbar spine moves independently of the pelvis and rib cage during gait and posture","C":"Strain stays at the segment where the dysfunction began and does not spread to the other regions of the body","D":"Thoracic findings arise from the lumbosacral dysfunction in a fixed reverse sequence of events","E":"Extremity findings are examined after the imaging is negative and the diagnosis is still unclear"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a biologic tensegrity model in which local pelvic or lumbosacral dysfunction can trigger compensatory strains through the myofascial kinetic chain, so regions above and below the lumbosacral junction are evaluated.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: tensegrity explains why regional interconnectedness matters in evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has low back pain, and the DO finds a somatic dysfunction alongside a mechanical disk problem. According to the course, how are these related?","choices":{"A":"Somatic dysfunction rules out an orthopedic diagnosis in an adult patient","B":"Somatic dysfunction appears after the disk problem has resolved and the pain has ended","C":"Somatic dysfunction is a visceral reflex arising from the abdominal organs and the retroperitoneum","D":"Somatic dysfunction can produce the symptoms or coexist with orthopedic and visceral pathologies","E":"Somatic dysfunction is a radiographic finding of a mechanical disk problem at the level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that somatic dysfunction can directly cause low back pain or exist in association with other orthopedic, visceral, and mechanical pathologies.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: somatic dysfunction can directly cause low back pain or coexist with orthopedic, visceral, and mechanical pathologies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with low back pain has unexplained elevated globulins on routine bloodwork. According to the course, which laboratory test is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Urinalysis with renal function studies","B":"Serum protein electrophoresis testing","C":"An antinuclear antibody titer panel","D":"A fasting lipid panel with glucose","E":"A lumbar puncture with cell counts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists serum protein electrophoresis as the multiple myeloma screen when there is an unexplained increase in total protein or elevated globulins.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: labs are reserved for patients who fail to respond to therapy or have a sudden change in pain character.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman who has had lumbar surgery has recurrent leg pain. According to the course, which imaging differentiates post-surgical scar tissue from a recurrent disk herniation?","choices":{"A":"Plain radiographs with oblique views of the lumbar spine","B":"CT of the lumbar spine without contrast","C":"Electromyography of the affected leg with nerve conduction","D":"A lateral radiograph with a Cobb lordosis angle measurement","E":"MRI with and without gadolinium contrast"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that MRI with and without gadolinium contrast is indicated to diagnose malignancies or to differentiate post-surgical scar tissue from a recurrent disk herniation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: MRI is the gold standard for spinal imaging because of its soft-tissue detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has leg pain 10 days after an injury, and his EMG is normal. According to the course, how should this result be interpreted?","choices":{"A":"A normal EMG excludes nerve root compression at any time in the course of care","B":"EMG changes appear within the first 24 hours of the initial injury to the root","C":"Electrical signs of nerve damage often do not appear until 21 days post-injury","D":"EMG should be repeated each day until it becomes abnormal on the tracing","E":"EMG cannot be used to separate radiculopathy from a peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course notes that electrical signs of nerve damage often do not appear on EMG until 21 days after the initial injury.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: EMG and nerve conduction studies help distinguish radiculopathy from distal peripheral neuropathies, myopathies, or entrapments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has searing leg pain that follows a disk annular tear, and imaging shows no direct nerve root compression. According to the course, what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Chemical radiculitis from leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines","B":"Mechanical radiculopathy from the nucleus pulposus compressing the root","C":"Microinstability from loss of segmental motion at the involved level","D":"Referred pain from sacroiliac joint dysfunction on the same side","E":"Referred visceral pain from a retroperitoneal organ near the root"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines through the annular tear irritates the adjacent nerve root and causes sharp, searing radicular pain even without direct physical compression.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mechanical radiculopathy instead produces focal dermatomal sensory loss, reduced reflexes, and eventual weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A degenerating disk has lost proteoglycan synthesis, and its hydrostatic pressure and water content have fallen. According to the course, what happens next?","choices":{"A":"The cells of the nucleus pulposus increase their output of proteoglycans and rehydrate the nucleus","B":"The vertebral endplates grow new capillaries into the nucleus and restore the diffusion of nutrients","C":"The segment becomes more stable as the nucleus stiffens and the annulus bears less of the load","D":"The outer annulus loses its innervation and becomes painless as the tears of the lamellae heal","E":"The annulus fibrosus tears under overload because the nucleus cannot distribute vertical loads"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After dehydration, the nucleus can no longer distribute vertical loads, so compressive forces transfer to the annulus fibrosus and cause tears or fissures.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the sequence runs from nutritional failure through dehydration and annular fissures to nociceptor activation, radiculopathy, and microinstability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a disk annular tear has localized axial back pain. According to the course, what generates this pain?","choices":{"A":"Compression of the nerve root by the nucleus pulposus at the level of the foramen","B":"New nerve endings in ingrown granulation tissue releasing nerve growth factor","C":"Leakage of acidic cytokines through the tear onto the dorsal root ganglion","D":"Loss of the proteoglycan matrix in the vertebral endplates above and below","E":"Hypertonicity of the psoas muscle on the same side as the symptoms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"As tears develop, well-vascularized, highly innervated granulation tissue grows into the defect. These new nerve endings release nerve growth factor, sensitizing local nociceptors and generating axial back pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: axial pain is localized to the spine, whereas radicular pain follows a dermatome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has weak great toe extension against resistance and numbness over the lateral lower leg, the dorsum of the foot, and the first two toes. According to the course, which nerve root is involved?","choices":{"A":"The L4 root","B":"The S1 root","C":"The L3 root","D":"The L5 root","E":"The L2 root"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the course's table, the L5 root supplies sensation to the lateral lower leg, dorsum of the foot, and first two toes, and its key muscle is the extensor hallucis longus for great toe extension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the L5 reflex is the tibialis posterior reflex, which is variable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman cannot walk on her toes and has a diminished Achilles reflex. According to the course, which muscle group is weak?","choices":{"A":"Tibialis anterior muscle group","B":"Extensor hallucis longus muscle","C":"Quadriceps femoris muscle group","D":"Hip adductor and iliopsoas muscles","E":"Gastrocnemius and soleus muscles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the course's table, toe walking and the Achilles reflex assess the S1 root, whose key muscle group is the gastrocnemius and soleus for ankle plantarflexion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's table lists a dermatome, a key muscle, a reflex, and a functional test for each root.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A seated patient slumps with the neck flexed, and the DO extends the knee and dorsiflexes the ankle, reproducing the patient's leg pain. According to the course, what does this positive test indicate?","choices":{"A":"Tightness of the hamstring muscle group","B":"Arthropathy of the lumbar facet joints","C":"Tension of the neural and dural structures","D":"Dysfunction of the sacroiliac joint complex","E":"Spasm of the psoas muscle on that side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A positive slump test reproduces radicular pain and indicates neural or dural tension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the straight leg raise is the supine dural tension test and is highly sensitive for lumbar radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has pain at the posterior iliac crest and groin. According to the course, how does the DO confirm the diagnosis of Maigne syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Counterstrain of the iliolumbar insertion eases the referred pain","B":"A heel lift that levels the sacral base removes the referred pain","C":"Lumbosacral decompression of the paraspinals clears the referred pain","D":"Segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 resolves the referred pain","E":"Counterstrain of the posterior piriformis point calms the referred pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Altered facet mechanics between T12 and L2 irritate the posterior rami and refer pain to the iliac crest, groin, or inguinal region. Segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 often completely resolves the referred pain, which confirms the diagnosis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Maigne syndrome is the thoracolumbar junction syndrome, in which altered T12 to L2 facet mechanics irritate the posterior rami.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, where is gentle OMT directed relative to the slip?","choices":{"A":"Above and below the level of the slip","B":"At the level of the slip itself with a thrust","C":"At the cervical spine and the occiput","D":"At the sternum and the clavicles","E":"At the hip joint capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course directs gentle indirect OMT above and below the level of the slip, using counterstrain, myofascial release, and balanced ligamentous tension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: related thoracic, pelvic, rib, and lower extremity dysfunctions are also treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has acute psoas spasm and a hip side shift. According to the course, why must the upper lumbar key dysfunction be treated first?","choices":{"A":"It is the most tender finding in the lumbar region on palpation","B":"It holds the greatest rotation of any segment in the lumbar spine","C":"Otherwise pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunctions recur rapidly","D":"It is the segment where counterstrain is applied for the lumbar region","E":"It restores hip extension without any need to treat the pelvis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that the upper lumbar key segment, a Type II FRS dysfunction at L1 or L2, must be treated first to obtain lasting relief. Without it, pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunctions rapidly recur.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: HVLA or muscle energy resolves the key dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Low Back Pain III","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which group of lumbar spine elements most commonly gives rise to low back pain?","choices":{"A":"The anterior elements, such as the posterior longitudinal ligament and vertebral body","B":"The middle elements, such as the meninges, spinal cord, and nerves","C":"The visceral elements, such as the retroperitoneal organs and kidneys","D":"The posterior elements, such as the intervertebral joints and ligaments","E":"The lateral elements, such as the psoas and quadratus lumborum muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course classifies lumbar pain as arising from anterior, middle, or posterior elements and states that low back pain most commonly stems from the posterior elements: the spinous and transverse processes, lamina, pedicles, intervertebral joints, ligaments, and joint capsules.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the anterior elements include the intervertebral disk and the longitudinal ligaments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, approximately what percentage of low back pain is nonspecific, with an unknown exact etiology?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 90 percent","B":"Approximately 10 percent","C":"Approximately 40 percent","D":"Approximately 60 percent","E":"Approximately 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course divides low back pain into specific pain with a medically known etiology and nonspecific pain, which represents approximately 90 percent of cases.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: musculoskeletal syndromes and somatic dysfunctions can account for part or all of nonspecific low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which findings typically accompany iliolumbar ligament syndrome?","choices":{"A":"A contralateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor, and tight quadratus lumborum","B":"Bilateral hamstring tightness, decreased lumbar lordosis, and a flexed posture","C":"An ipsilateral foot drop, an absent patellar reflex, and loss of thigh sensation","D":"Saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, and decreased rectal sphincter tone","E":"An ipsilateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor, and tight quadratus lumborum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Iliolumbar ligament syndrome presents with tenderness at the iliac insertion of the ligament, typically with an ipsilateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor muscle, and tight quadratus lumborum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the tender point lies 1 inch superior and lateral to the medial edge of the PSIS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, acute low back pain is defined as pain lasting how long?","choices":{"A":"Less than 4 weeks","B":"Less than 8 weeks","C":"Less than 6 weeks","D":"Less than 10 weeks","E":"Less than 12 weeks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course classifies low back pain as acute when it lasts less than 6 weeks, subacute from 6 to 12 weeks, and chronic when it lasts more than 12 weeks.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronicity determines which diagnostic tests are ordered and guides management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which are the four main categories of red flags for serious underlying pathology in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Cauda equina syndrome, infection, fracture, and malignancy","B":"Cauda equina syndrome, infection, depression, and malignancy","C":"Fracture, malignancy, fear-avoidance beliefs, and infection","D":"Infection, fracture, osteoporosis, and sleep problems","E":"Malignancy, catastrophizing, cauda equina syndrome, and smoking"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists four red flag categories: cauda equina syndrome, infection, fracture, and malignancy. Depression, fear-avoidance beliefs, obesity, sleep problems, catastrophizing, and smoking are yellow flags.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a single red flag has low sensitivity and specificity, but clusters of red flags raise the probability of serious disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which two predictive instruments does the course name for identifying yellow flags?","choices":{"A":"The Mini-Mental State Examination and the Apgar scoring system","B":"The STarT Back tool and the Orebro Musculoskeletal Pain Screening Questionnaire","C":"The Glasgow Coma Scale and the Montreal Cognitive Assessment tool","D":"The Wells criteria for pulmonary embolism and the CURB-65 pneumonia severity score","E":"The Ottawa ankle rules and the Canadian C-spine imaging decision rule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course names the STarT Back tool and the Orebro Musculoskeletal Pain Screening Questionnaire as predictive screening instruments for psychosocial yellow flags.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: psychosocial yellow flags play a critical role in the transition from acute pain to chronic pain and disability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man stands flexed forward with a lean to the right, restricted hip extension, and a pelvic side shift to the left. According to the course, which condition fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Left-sided psoas syndrome from iliopsoas spasm","B":"Right-sided iliolumbar ligament syndrome","C":"Right-sided psoas syndrome from iliopsoas spasm","D":"Pregnancy-related low back pain with a bilaterally flexed sacrum","E":"Right-sided thoracolumbar junction syndrome of T12 to L2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Psoas spasm produces a flexed forward posture with a lean toward the side of the tight muscle, restricted hip extension, and a pelvic side shift away from the tight psoas. Here the lean is right and the shift is left, so the right psoas is tight.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: psoas syndrome typically carries a Type II FRS dysfunction of L1 or L2 rotated to the side of the tight psoas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old gymnast has mechanical low back pain worsened by repetitive flexion-extension and lumbar extension. She has a hyperlordotic posture and hamstring tightness, and she has tender points over the iliolumbar ligaments, sacral sulcus, and gluteus medius without a step-off of the spinous processes. According to the course, what is the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Anterior translation of one vertebral body on the segment below","B":"Eccentric bulging of a disk with an intact annulus fibrosus","C":"Posterior translation of one vertebral body on the segment below","D":"Impingement of the superior cluneal nerve at the iliac crest","E":"A stress fracture of the pars interarticularis primarily at L5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis, primarily at L5 in 95 percent of cases, presenting with mechanical pain worsened by repetitive flexion-extension and extension. Spondylolisthesis, the anterior translation of a vertebral body, is characterized instead by a tender step-off of the spinous processes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spondylolysis involves L5 in 95 percent of cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has leg and buttock pain that worsens with walking and spinal extension and improves with sitting or forward flexion. She has a positive Kemp sign. According to the course, what is this classic presentation called?","choices":{"A":"Neurogenic claudication","B":"Vascular claudication of the legs","C":"Failed back surgical syndrome","D":"Cauda equina syndrome","E":"Referred visceral pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes spinal stenosis as presenting classically with neurogenic claudication, in which extension narrows the spinal canal and sitting or forward flexion relieves the pain, with a positive Kemp sign on extension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: vascular claudication can mimic neurogenic claudication, so the DO checks leg circulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has right low back pain. According to the course, when should the DO examine the painful region during the osteopathic structural examination?","choices":{"A":"First, to locate the restrictive barrier","B":"After imaging confirms the affected level","C":"At the same time as the neurologic examination","D":"Last, to prevent guarding and compensation","E":"Before the standing postural examination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that the painful region should be examined last during the structural exam to prevent patient guarding and structural compensation during the rest of the exam.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: active motion is tested first by the patient and passive motion by the physician.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has an anterior pelvic tilt, increased hip flexion, and compensatory lumbar hyperlordosis. According to the course, which muscle pattern defines this lower crossed syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Tight iliopsoas and erector spinae with weak gluteus maximus and abdominals","B":"Tight gluteus maximus and abdominals with weak iliopsoas and erector spinae","C":"Tight hamstrings and gluteus medius with weak quadratus lumborum and psoas","D":"Tight rectus abdominis and obliques with weak tensor fascia lata and rectus femoris","E":"Tight piriformis and hip adductors with weak gluteus maximus and erector spinae"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Janda's lower crossed syndrome combines tightness of the iliopsoas, rectus femoris, erector spinae, and tensor fascia lata with weakness or inhibition of the gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, rectus abdominis, and obliques.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Zink's common compensatory pattern is a different framework, with somatic dysfunctions at the spinal transitional zones showing alternating fascial rotational preferences throughout the body.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A supine patient has sharp pain shooting below the knee when the DO passively raises the extended leg. According to the course, how does the DO distinguish neural pain from hamstring tightness?","choices":{"A":"Raise the leg higher and hold the position until the radiating pain eases","B":"Slightly lower the leg and add passive ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion","C":"Flex the knee and plantarflex the ankle before repeating the full raise","D":"Ask the patient to contract the hamstrings against the DO's resistance","E":"Rotate the hip internally and externally while the leg stays raised"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Structural differentiation maneuvers, such as slightly lowering the leg and adding passive ankle dorsiflexion, hip adduction, or neck flexion, tension the dural sac without moving musculoskeletal tissue and so separate neural irritation from hamstring tightness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a positive straight leg raise typically occurs between 30 and 70 degrees of flexion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Low Back Pain IV","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how long is the standard conservative trial for nonspecific mechanical back pain?","choices":{"A":"6 to 8 weeks","B":"2 to 4 weeks","C":"10 to 12 weeks","D":"3 to 4 months","E":"6 months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a standard multidisciplinary protocol for nonspecific mechanical back pain as a 6-to-8-week trial combining patient education, home exercises, core stabilization, physical or occupational therapy, and judicious OMT.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a written summary of the recommendations should be given to the patient at the end of the visit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, when is initial follow-up recommended for an acute outpatient presentation of low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Within 12 to 24 hours","B":"Within 1 to 2 weeks","C":"Within 3 to 4 weeks","D":"Within 2 to 3 months","E":"Within 2 to 3 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For an acute outpatient presentation the course recommends initial follow-up within 2 to 3 days.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the DO palpates at each visit to adjust the treatment dosage or modality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what must the physician determine first when stratifying immediacy and risk in a patient with low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Whether the patient can set realistic, measurable goals with the physician for recovery","B":"Whether the dysfunction is better treated with a direct or an indirect technique","C":"Whether an emergent or urgent medical condition needs referral or advanced imaging","D":"Whether the most tender segment is suitable for direct treatment at the first visit","E":"Whether scheduled anti-inflammatory medication would help control the acute pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that the physician must first determine whether an emergent or urgent condition requires immediate specialist referral, surgical consultation, or advanced imaging.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a conservative timeline is established if no immediate life-threatening process is present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique is indicated when localized muscle and joint pain with dominant tender points characterizes the presentation?","choices":{"A":"Strain-counterstrain","B":"Cranial osteopathic technique","C":"Articulatory technique","D":"Still technique","E":"HVLA thrust technique"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists strain-counterstrain as indicated when localized muscle and joint pain and tender points dominate, and as an ideal gentle indirect technique for acute flare-ups.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the Jones points treated include anterior and posterior lumbar and pelvic points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is side-lying lumbar articulation indicated for?","choices":{"A":"Severe paraspinal hypertonia with loss of fascial compliance","B":"Chronic facet stiffness with loss of passive range of motion","C":"Acute complex restrictions of the pelvis with an innominate upslip","D":"Hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles with limited motion","E":"Specific localized dysfunctions at L5-S1 without contraindications"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists side-lying lumbar joint mobilization as indicated for chronic zygapophysial facet stiffness and to gently restore passive range of motion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: articulation targets chronic zygapophysial stiffness in a side-lying position.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which approach is indicated for sacral torsions and for lengthening hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles?","choices":{"A":"Osteopathy in the cranial field","B":"Balanced ligamentous tension","C":"Diaphragmatic myofascial release","D":"Muscle energy technique","E":"Side-lying lumbar thrust"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists muscle energy technique for segmental Type II FRS and ERS dysfunctions and sacral torsions, and to lengthen hypertonic muscles such as the psoas and piriformis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: muscle energy uses active, patient-directed isometric contractions against a counterforce.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique class is specifically named as absolutely contraindicated in these scenarios?","choices":{"A":"Gentle indirect techniques such as balanced ligamentous tension","B":"Direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA","C":"Passive myofascial release of the paraspinal tissues","D":"Diaphragmatic lymphatic drainage techniques","E":"Cranial manipulation of the sphenobasilar synchondrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA are absolutely contraindicated in the absolute scenarios, and that in many cases all manual manipulation at the affected region must be withheld.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the absolute list covers conditions with a risk of catastrophic structural failure, cord injury, or hemorrhage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is an absolute contraindication to OMT in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Spondylolisthesis with segmental instability","B":"Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","C":"Active inflammatory joint disease","D":"Bony metastasis at the target segment","E":"Acute herniated disk with active radiculopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists bony metastasis at the target segment as an absolute contraindication because tumors weaken the vertebral architecture.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: primary or metastatic tumors from breast, lung, prostate, renal, or thyroid cancers weaken the vertebral architecture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does OMT look like for a patient with a relative contraindication?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity thrust applied directly at the involved spinal level","B":"Forceful direct articulation applied to the unstable spinal segment","C":"Direct inhibition applied at the involved spinal level","D":"Forced lumbar extension applied to the affected lumbar region","E":"Gentle indirect OMT at associated or distant somatic dysfunctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For relative contraindications the physician avoids high-force, direct-action techniques at the involved level, while gentle indirect OMT directed at associated or distant somatic dysfunctions remains safe and beneficial.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course names strain-counterstrain, myofascial release, and balanced ligamentous tension as the gentle options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what should be avoided in a patient with acute psoas spasm?","choices":{"A":"Applying cold therapy to the spasm","B":"Gentle self-directed home stretches","C":"Counterstrain of the anterior psoas points","D":"Applying local heat to the spasm","E":"Muscle energy of the lower lumbar spine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course advises avoiding local heat, which typically worsens acute psoas spasm, and using cold therapy and gentle home stretching instead.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cold therapy and gentle self-directed stretching are the course's self-care advice for acute psoas spasm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which device is used in the acute phase of spondylolisthesis to limit shear?","choices":{"A":"A lordotic hyperextension-type brace","B":"A TLSO spinal orthosis","C":"A rigid cervical collar","D":"A sacroiliac support belt","E":"A knee immobilizer brace"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course recommends a spinal orthosis, the TLSO brace, in the acute phase to limit shear, along with an antilordotic physical therapy program.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the antilordotic program stresses core strengthening, hamstring stretching, and avoiding extension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which OMT is provided for short leg syndrome before heel lift therapy is introduced?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing a rigid lumbosacral brace to hold the pelvis level during standing and walking","B":"Prescribing high-impact plyometric training to equalize strength in both legs","C":"Pan-corporeal OMT for pelvic shears and sacral torsions with extremity work","D":"Strengthening the psoas muscle to level the pelvis in standing and walking","E":"Resting in bed for several weeks until the leg discrepancy resolves on its own"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes the structural OMT for short leg syndrome as pan-corporeal treatment of pelvic shears, sacral torsions, lower extremity joint restrictions, and quadratus lumborum myofascial release, before heel lift therapy levels the sacral base.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: leg length discrepancy is a primary perpetuating factor for quadratus lumborum trigger points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case of a 52-year-old man with right-sided low back pain, which event started the compensatory chain?","choices":{"A":"A left knee meniscus injury that altered his gait","B":"A right bicipital tendonitis flare from playing tennis","C":"A lumbar disk protrusion at L5-S1 that irritated the root","D":"An exhalation restriction of the right first rib","E":"A fracture of the right ankle during a golf swing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The case begins with a left knee meniscus injury playing tennis that forced him to limp, which set off restricted ankle dorsiflexion, pelvic unleveling, a sacral torsion, piriformis hypertonicity, and an L5 somatic dysfunction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the case uses the biomechanical model to link the knee injury to the back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case, which model includes short-term scheduled NSAIDs, advice against bed rest beyond 3 days, and dietary guidance?","choices":{"A":"The biomechanical model targeting gait kinematics","B":"The neurological model targeting segmental facilitation","C":"The respiratory-circulatory model targeting venous return","D":"The behavioral model targeting fear-avoidance and coping beliefs","E":"The metabolic energy model targeting systemic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The metabolic energy model plan includes short-term scheduled NSAIDs, avoiding bed rest beyond 3 days, and dietary guidance to decrease systemic inflammation and protect cellular repair.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hypercholesterolemia and a systemic inflammatory state can delay recovery of the meniscus, fascia, and avascular disk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case, what did the MRI show?","choices":{"A":"A 2-mm broad-based L4-L5 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis at L4","B":"A 5-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis at L5","C":"A 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis","D":"A 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild left neuroforaminal stenosis at L5","E":"A normal study with a hypertonic piriformis muscle on the right"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The subsequent diagnostics showed normal EMG and nerve conduction studies and an MRI with a 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 disk protrusion with mild right-sided neuroforaminal stenosis at L5.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in the course's case the EMG and nerve conduction studies were normal, so the MRI supplies the structural findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with low back pain has fever, a history of IV drug use, and a recent indwelling catheter. According to the course, what comes first in his treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"Immediate referral or advanced imaging before OMT","B":"A conservative trial of home exercises and OMT","C":"Scheduled NSAIDs with reassessment at the next visit","D":"Treatment of thoracic and pelvic dysfunctions before further workup","E":"Reassurance and a written exercise handout"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, IV drug use, and indwelling catheters are infection red flags in the course's evaluation section. The treatment plan section says the physician must first determine whether an emergent condition, such as infection, requires immediate specialist referral, surgical consultation, or advanced imaging before a conservative plan.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a conservative timeline starts only after emergent processes are excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO treated an acute outpatient with low back pain, and the patient has improved at each visit. According to the course, how should the interval between treatment sessions change?","choices":{"A":"It shortens as the dysfunction resolves and the pain subsides","B":"It stays fixed at the initial interval regardless of response","C":"It lengthens once imaging has been repeated and reviewed","D":"It progressively lengthens as dysfunction and inflammation resolve","E":"It shortens once the patient reaches a functional recovery milestone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that as somatic dysfunctions and local inflammatory responses resolve, the intervals between treatment sessions progressively lengthen.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the DO adjusts dosage and modality at every visit based on palpated tissue changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic low back pain has a lumbar dysfunction that is influenced by forces above and below the lumbosacral region. According to the course, which regions should the treatment plan address?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spine, sphenoid, temporal bones, mandible, and hyoid bone","B":"Lumbar spine with the remaining regions deferred to later visits","C":"Thoracic spine, costal cage, pelvis, sacrum, and lower extremities","D":"Pelvis and sacrum, which hold the primary lumbosacral dysfunction","E":"Lumbar paraspinal muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and iliolumbar ligaments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lumbar mechanics are affected by altered forces above and below the lumbosacral region, so the physician must evaluate and plan treatment for the thoracic spine, costal cage, pelvis, sacrum, and lower extremities.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: restoring posture and mechanical efficiency requires a multi-regional plan.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic nonspecific low back pain has anxiety, depression, and poor sleep latency, with signs of central sensitization. According to the course, which technique is indicated?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity thrust at the L5-S1 segment of the lumbosacral spine region","B":"Side-lying articulation of the lumbar facet joints for stiffness","C":"Prone lumbar pressure with counter-leverage on the paraspinals","D":"Osteopathic cranial manipulation of the sphenobasilar synchondrosis","E":"Still technique to the innominate bone for a pelvic upslip"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course indicates osteopathy in the cranial field for chronic low back pain with central neuropathic sensitization, anxiety, depression, or sleep disturbances, targeting the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and primary respiratory mechanism.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OCMM down-regulates sympathetic nerve activity and improves sleep latency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has a localized, subacute Type II dysfunction at L5-S1 with no bony or neurologic contraindications. According to the course, which approach is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar prone pressure with counter-leverage","B":"Side-lying lumbar thrust technique","C":"Diaphragmatic myofascial release","D":"Balanced ligamentous tension of the iliolumbar ligaments","E":"Passive positioning to shorten tissues at Jones points"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists HVLA as indicated for localized, subacute or chronic segmental restrictions, especially at L5-S1, when no bony or neurologic contraindications exist.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: side-lying lumbar thrust is the course's HVLA technique for the lumbar region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has lumbar paraspinal hypertonia, thoracolumbar fascial stiffness, reduced fascial compliance, and coccydynia. According to the course, which technique is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Active isometric contractions against a physician counterforce","B":"Moving the dysfunction from ease through the restrictive barrier","C":"Decompression of the occipitoatlantal joint and cranial base","D":"Passive positioning toward the point of greatest ease and comfort","E":"Soft tissue and myofascial release with lumbosacral decompression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course indicates soft tissue and myofascial release to reduce passive resistance in the paraspinal tissues, address thoracolumbar fascial stiffness, and treat coccydynia, using techniques such as lumbosacral decompression and abdominal diaphragm release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: diaphragmatic myofascial release improves pain and disability scores in chronic nonspecific low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has an acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy. According to the course, how is this classified?","choices":{"A":"A relative contraindication requiring avoidance of direct thrusts at that level","B":"An absolute contraindication that withholds manipulation of the entire region","C":"A condition that is not a contraindication when the radicular pain is stable and mild","D":"A contraindication that applies to the cervical spine and upper thoracic spine","E":"A relative contraindication that permits direct thrusts one level above the herniation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy is a relative contraindication. Direct thrusts at the herniation level are avoided, while gentle indirect techniques relieve the surrounding protective spasm.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: avoiding thrusts protects the nerve root from further compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has sudden perianal numbness and urinary retention after lifting. According to the course, how should the DO proceed?","choices":{"A":"Perform gentle indirect OMT at distant regions and reassess at the next visit","B":"Withhold OMT and arrange emergent surgical referral for decompression","C":"Treat the affected segment with a side-lying thrust technique","D":"Begin conservative therapy with home exercises and reassess at follow-up","E":"Treat the lumbar spine with prone pressure and counter-leverage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Perianal numbness and urinary retention suggest cauda equina syndrome, a surgical emergency. The course lists it as an absolute contraindication because any delay in decompression or mechanical pressure on the canal can cause permanent paralysis or incontinence.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: any delay in decompression or mechanical pressure on the canal can cause permanent paralysis or incontinence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis has a flare of sacroiliitis. According to the course, which approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Direct articulation to restore normal sacroiliac joint motion","B":"A high-velocity thrust to release the restricted sacroiliac joint","C":"Muscle energy mobilization of the restricted sacroiliac joint","D":"Forceful rhythmic springing of the sacrum to mobilize the joint","E":"Gentle indirect techniques that do not move the inflamed joint"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Active inflammatory joint disease such as sacroiliitis in ankylosing spondylitis is a relative contraindication. Direct-action manipulation aggravates joint inflammation, so the course uses gentle lymphatic drainage, balanced ligamentous tension, or counterstrain without moving the inflamed joint.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the goal is to reduce local nociceptive afferent output.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has acute psoas spasm with a pelvic side shift and a Type II FRS dysfunction rotated to the side of the tight psoas. According to the course, which region must be treated first?","choices":{"A":"The sacral base on the side of the shift","B":"The ilium on the side of the tight psoas muscle","C":"The upper lumbar key level at L1 or L2","D":"The lumbosacral junction at L5-S1","E":"The pubic symphysis and the pubic rami"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course calls the upper lumbar L1 or L2 Type II FRS dysfunction the key dysfunction that must be resolved first. Failing to release it causes lower lumbosacral and sacroiliac dysfunctions to recur rapidly.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: HVLA or muscle energy resolves the L1 and L2 dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has spondylolisthesis with extension-sensitive pain. According to the course, which treatment approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"A high-velocity thrust at the unstable level to restore motion","B":"Spinal extension stretching to reduce the anterior slip","C":"Direct articulation of the unstable segment","D":"Gentle indirect OMT above and below the level of the slip","E":"Vigorous psoas strengthening to stabilize the slipped segment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HVLA is relatively contraindicated at the unstable level. The course directs gentle indirect OMT above and below the affected segment, and treats functionally related regions.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gentle psoas release decreases the compressive intradiscal load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman develops a new leg length discrepancy and pelvic unleveling after a total hip replacement. According to the course, which measure is added to OMT?","choices":{"A":"Bed rest for 1 to 2 weeks to let the pelvis settle after surgery","B":"Psoas strengthening exercise to correct the pelvic tilt","C":"Pelvic HVLA for each pelvic shear found on the exam","D":"Heavy lifting restrictions for 6 months after the surgery","E":"Customized shoe or heel orthotics to level the sacral base"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For post-surgical patients with a new acute leg length discrepancy, the course combines OMT with customized shoe or heel orthotics to restore sacral base levelness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the discrepancy places traction on the iliolumbar ligaments and perpetuates quadratus lumborum trigger points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's biomechanical analysis of the case, a 52-year-old man has intermittent sharp pain radiating along the back of the leg but not past the knee. According to the course, why does this mimic radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"The L5 disk extrudes and compresses the S1 nerve root directly","B":"Piriformis spasm refers pain down the right posterior thigh","C":"Sacral torsion compresses the sciatic nerve at the greater sciatic notch","D":"Left knee meniscus inflammation refers pain upward along the leg","E":"A hypertonic psoas compresses the femoral nerve in the pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the biomechanical analysis, the hypertonic right piriformis muscle directly refers pain down the right posterior thigh, mimicking radiculopathy.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: counterstrain is used to release the hypertonic piriformis in this case.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's case, the patient has chronic asthma. According to the course, which finding reflects its chronic viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"An FRS dysfunction of L5 with a positive lumbar spring test result","B":"An inhalation restriction of the right 12th rib and diaphragm","C":"Tissue texture changes and tenderness at the T4 to T5 paraspinals","D":"A left sacral torsion on a right oblique axis of the sacrum","E":"Tenderness of the left medial meniscus counterstrain point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The neurological analysis states that chronic asthma maintains localized autonomic facilitation that appears as a persistent viscerosomatic reflex, with tissue texture changes and tenderness in the T4 to T5 paraspinal region.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: treating the T4 to T5 reflex helps normalize sympathetic outflow to the lungs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's case, the patient has trouble finding a comfortable position in bed. According to the course, which sleep ergonomics does the behavioral plan teach?","choices":{"A":"Lying on his side with knees bent around a pillow","B":"Lying prone with a pillow under the abdomen","C":"Lying supine with a rolled towel under the neck and shoulders","D":"Sleeping upright in a reclining chair at night","E":"Lying supine with the legs straight and no pillow support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The behavioral plan teaches supportive sleep ergonomics, such as lying on his side with knees bent around a pillow, to reduce dural tension and improve sleep quality.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the plan also gives reassurance that most acute low back pain episodes resolve within 6 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Gerontologic Changes and Geriatric Disease","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is gerontology?","choices":{"A":"The clinical care of the medical issues of the elderly","B":"The treatment of age-related pathological states","C":"The study of normal child development","D":"The science of the aging process itself","E":"The pharmacology of age-related drug clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course defines gerontology as the field of science pertaining specifically to the study of the aging process itself. Geriatrics is the clinical field dedicated to the health, care, and unique medical issues of the elderly.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic change is normal aging, whereas geriatric disease is a pathological state.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is a gerontologic change rather than a geriatric disease?","choices":{"A":"Dementia","B":"Osteoarthritis","C":"Multiple myeloma","D":"Severe pneumonia","E":"Sarcopenia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists sarcopenia, bone demineralization, increased body fat, and decreased total body water as gerontologic changes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic changes are universal and not diseases, but they reduce physiologic reserve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which feature distinguishes dementia from the cognitive changes of normal aging?","choices":{"A":"Mild benign delay in recalling memories with preserved independence","B":"Subtle cerebral volume loss with slower recall that does not limit daily activities","C":"Severe short-term memory loss with disorientation and functional decline","D":"Slower processing speed with intact orientation and daily function","E":"Occasional word-finding trouble that improves with rest or cues"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course contrasts the mild, benign recall delays of normal aging with dementia, a progressive degenerative disease with severe short-term memory loss, disorientation, and significant functional decline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal aging includes subtle cerebral volume loss and slower recall, which are not dementia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is homeostenosis?","choices":{"A":"A fixed set point of the hypothalamic thermostat in older adults","B":"Sudden loss of homeostasis after a single minor stressor","C":"Compensatory expansion of reserves after chronic stress in aging","D":"Progressive constriction of homeostatic reserves with aging","E":"Narrowing of arteries from age-related calcification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Homeostenosis is the progressive constriction of the body's homeostatic reserves that occurs as a natural consequence of physiological aging.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: homeostenosis is a normal consequence of aging, not itself a disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which is a key feature of homeostenosis?","choices":{"A":"A wider margin for iatrogenic disruption","B":"A decreased ability to adapt to stimuli","C":"Enhanced recovery after stressors","D":"Organ systems working with a large unused capacity","E":"Expanded alveolar surface area"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because baseline reserves are depleted, the elderly person has impaired adaptability, a decreased ability to adapt to internal or external stimuli.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: impaired adaptability means a smaller stimulus can destabilize an older adult.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does the course advise starting low and going slow with medications and interventions in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Homeostenosis makes their effects unpredictable","B":"Older patients clear drugs faster than young adults","C":"Starting low avoids the need to monitor responses","D":"Slow advancement prevents the development of drug tolerance","E":"Frail patients tolerate larger stimuli but recover slowly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that homeostenosis makes the effects of medications and therapeutic interventions highly unpredictable, so clinicians prescribe at lower initial doses and advance them slowly.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same principle applies to OMT dose in frail patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the recommended starting heel lift in an older adult with a leg length discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"1/4 inch to correct the full radiographic discrepancy","B":"1/2 inch with upward adjustment at each weekly visit","C":"1/8 inch or half the radiographic discrepancy","D":"The full measured discrepancy in a single step","E":"3/4 inch followed by gradual reduction over months"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course advises a very small, gentle dose, typically starting at 1/8 inch or less, or half of the measured radiographic discrepancy, and adjusting upward in 1/8 inch increments every 2 to 4 weeks.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: balance the sacral base with OMT and core strengthening before attempting lift therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which dysfunctions should OMT target to restore equilibrium and reduce lower leg muscle tension in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Extension dysfunctions of the lumbar, sacral, and pelvic regions and the hips","B":"Flexion dysfunctions of the upper thoracic, cervical, and craniocervical regions","C":"Rotational dysfunctions of the hip, knee, and ankle joints of the lower limb and foot","D":"Inhalation dysfunctions of the lower, middle, and upper ribs and the diaphragm","E":"Sidebending dysfunctions of the clavicle, scapula, and humerus of the shoulder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OMT should target flexion dysfunctions of the upper thoracic, cervical, and cranio-cervical junctions. Resolving them restores normal head position, returns the visual field to the horizon, and shifts the vestibular apparatus back to a stable neutral position.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course places this under the Neurological Model.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how much water should an older adult consume daily, prescribed like a medication?","choices":{"A":"1,200 to 1,500 ml","B":"500 to 800 ml","C":"2,000 to 2,500 ml","D":"1,600 to 1,800 ml","E":"800 to 1,000 ml"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under the Metabolic Model the course directs 1,200 to 1,500 ml of water daily and protein intake of 0.8 g/kg of body weight to preserve muscle mass.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a senescent decline in thirst receptors leaves older adults vulnerable to rapid dehydration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has bilateral high-frequency hearing loss and reduced lens elasticity but lives independently and has intact cognition. According to the course, how are these findings classified?","choices":{"A":"Gerontologic changes of normal aging","B":"Geriatric diseases from failed homeostasis","C":"Early signs of dementia","D":"Complications of multiple myeloma","E":"Atypical presentations of infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists presbycusis, bilateral sensorineural high-frequency hearing loss, and presbyopia, loss of lens elasticity, among the universal, non-pathological gerontologic changes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic changes are not diseases, but they increase vulnerability to disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman with a chronic lower urinary tract infection presents with fatigue and confusion but no urinary complaints. According to the course, how is this best described?","choices":{"A":"A gerontologic change presenting as localized urinary symptoms","B":"Normal aging that does not require further evaluation","C":"A gerontologic change from reduced bladder compliance with age","D":"A homeostenosis state that resolves on its own without treatment","E":"A geriatric disease presenting as an atypical systemic illness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists severe infections such as pneumonia or chronic lower urinary tract infections as geriatric diseases that present as atypical, systemic illnesses rather than localized complaints.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: infections in older adults can present as atypical, systemic illnesses rather than localized complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has symptomatic knee degeneration with cartilage destruction. According to the course, how does this differ from normal aging?","choices":{"A":"A gerontologic change that appears universally in older adults as they age","B":"A pathological geriatric disease called osteoarthritis","C":"Sarcopenia from progressive loss of skeletal muscle mass with aging","D":"A clonal plasma cell disorder that infiltrates the bone marrow","E":"Demineralization of bone with brittleness and fracture risk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes osteoarthritis as symptomatic joint degeneration and cartilage destruction, as opposed to the minor, asymptomatic joint changes that may occur with age.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: normal gerontologic changes in the joints can be minor and asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — falls and decreased range of motion in geriatric patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 80-year-old man on stable diuretic therapy develops volume depletion and acute renal failure after a minor increase in diuresis. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis does this illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced tolerance of iatrogenic disruption","B":"Expanded physiologic reserve in advanced age","C":"Faster recovery after dehydration","D":"A narrow margin for clinical error","E":"Compensatory hormonal expansion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course gives this example: a minor overdiuresis in a stable frail patient can rapidly precipitate volume depletion and acute renal failure, and slightly rapid fluid administration can trigger congestive heart failure.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: frail older adults have very little tolerance for iatrogenic or environmental disruptions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 101-year-old man decompensates into multi-system organ failure after a minor stressor. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis explains his instability?","choices":{"A":"Excess physiologic reserve that delays recovery","B":"Increased adaptability to external stimuli","C":"Reduced forces of recovery in advanced age","D":"A wide margin that requires faster dosing","E":"Rapid restoration of baseline homeostasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course notes that in advanced age, particularly in centenarians, the inherent forces keeping them from decompensation are reduced, so a stressor can push the patient into instability and rapid multi-system failure.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: unaddressed decompensation can progress to organ failure and death.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman has reduced CD8 T-cell function, decreased alveolar surface area, and a lower GFR. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis do these changes represent?","choices":{"A":"Reduction of systemic reserves","B":"Expansion of reserves through compensation","C":"A wide margin for physiologic error","D":"Failure of a single organ system","E":"Progressive loss of thirst receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course cites reduced CD8 T-cell function, decreased alveolar surface area, and a drop in GFR as examples of reduced systemic reserves, leaving organ systems operating closer to maximum capacity under basal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GFR declines with normal aging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman is admitted with pneumonia and is a candidate for adjunctive OMT. According to the course, what did standardized twice-daily OMT protocols show in the MOPSE trial?","choices":{"A":"Shorter hospitalization with no change in mortality","B":"Lower mortality with no change in length of stay","C":"No change in stay or mortality","D":"Shorter hospitalization and lower mortality","E":"A higher rate of rib fracture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under the Respiratory-Circulatory Model the course states that standardized, twice-daily adjunctive OMT protocols, as in the MOPSE trial, significantly reduced the length of hospitalization and decreased mortality in elderly patients admitted with pneumonia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OMT focuses on restoring motion of the thoracic cage, ribs, and diaphragm to promote congestion clearance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — lymphatic and respiratory treatment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man is taught slow deep breathing to improve autonomic tone. According to the course, which rate and effect are targeted?","choices":{"A":"10 to 12 breaths per minute, raising sympathetic tone","B":"20 to 24 breaths per minute, lowering baroreflex sensitivity","C":"5 to 7 breaths per minute, raising heart rate variability","D":"30 to 40 breaths per minute, raising vital capacity","E":"3 to 4 breaths per minute, lowering vagal tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course prescribes slow, deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute, which stimulates Traube-Hering-wave oscillations in autonomic tone and enhances heart rate variability and baroreflex sensitivity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this prescription falls under the Respiratory-Circulatory Model.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 6 — integrative treatment plans that balance autonomic tone","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"A geriatric clinic wants a framework for structuring age-friendly care. According to the course, which four elements make up the 4Ms?","choices":{"A":"Mobility, Mood, Memory, and Muscle strength","B":"What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility","C":"Medication, Morbidity, Mortality, and Medical costs","D":"What Matters, Metabolism, Mentation, and Meal planning","E":"Mentation, Mood, Mobility, and Medical history"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the Behavioral Model the course uses the 4Ms framework of What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility to structure care and tailor plans to the patient's goals.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: talking about What Matters allows comfortable trade-offs between symptom control and treatment burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Gerontological Changes of Aging","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how are articular restrictions treated in a frail patient?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity, low-amplitude thrust with a rotary lever","B":"Rapid, high-force direct techniques at the barrier","C":"Low-velocity, moderate-to-high-amplitude articulation","D":"Forceful, sustained traction held at end range","E":"Repeated, rapid springing of the restricted segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course treats articular restrictions with low-velocity, moderate-to-high-amplitude articulation procedures rather than rapid, high-force direct techniques.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the technique is chosen by the structural component that predominates in the somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which techniques manage neural reflex and nociceptive irritability in a frail patient?","choices":{"A":"Strain-counterstrain and facilitated positional release","B":"Muscle energy and high-velocity thrust procedures at end range","C":"Myofascial release and deep soft tissue stretching","D":"Articulation and high-velocity thrust techniques","E":"Lymphatic pump and visceral manipulation methods"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neural reflex and nociceptive irritability are managed with indirect, non-threatening, passive techniques such as strain-counterstrain and facilitated positional release to reduce neural reflex activity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: myofascial and soft tissue tension is addressed with gentle myofascial release, soft tissue stretching, or modified muscle energy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how long can post-treatment rebound reactions last in elderly patients?","choices":{"A":"30 to 90 minutes","B":"12 to 48 hours","C":"5 to 7 days","D":"2 to 6 hours","E":"2 to 3 weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rebound reactions in the elderly, from mild fatigue to increased pain at the chief complaint site, are slower and can last 12 to 48 hours.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: warn the patient about potential post-treatment rebound reactions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, a Timed Up and Go test longer than how many seconds places an older adult at high risk for falling?","choices":{"A":"6 seconds","B":"8 seconds","C":"20 seconds","D":"12 seconds","E":"10 seconds"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An older adult taking more than 12 seconds to complete the Timed Up and Go test is categorized as at high risk for falling.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: benzodiazepine use is also highly associated with falls, delirium, and depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, weakness or inhibition of which muscle leaves older patients vulnerable to falling sideways?","choices":{"A":"Gluteus maximus","B":"Rectus femoris","C":"Gluteus medius","D":"Tibialis anterior","E":"Iliacus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weakness or inhibition of the gluteus medius impairs lateral pelvic stabilization in the coronal plane, leaving patients vulnerable to falling sideways, which carries the highest risk of hip fracture.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic spasm of the psoas major stresses the femoral neck and contributes to mechanical falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which four findings independently predict hip fracture from falls?","choices":{"A":"Slow gait speed, poor tandem walk, reduced visual acuity, and small calf circumference","B":"Slow gait speed, reduced grip strength, poor hearing, and a large calf circumference","C":"Fast gait speed, good tandem walk, reduced hearing, and a small calf circumference","D":"Reduced vibratory sense, short reaction time, poor dentition, and a low body weight","E":"Slow reaction time, reduced vibratory sense, low thirst drive, and a large calf circumference"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists slower gait speed, difficulty with tandem walk, reduced visual acuity, and small calf circumference, which reflects gastrocnemius atrophy, as independent predictors of hip fracture from falls.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: benzodiazepine use is also highly associated with falls, delirium, and depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is an absolute contraindication to osteopathic treatment in a geriatric patient?","choices":{"A":"Spondylolisthesis","B":"Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","C":"Acute herniated disc with active radiculopathy","D":"Cauda equina syndrome","E":"Severe osteoporosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists cauda equina syndrome as an absolute contraindication, a medical and surgical emergency in which any manual delay or direct pressure on the compromised canal is contraindicated. The other listed conditions are relative, not absolute, contraindications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: other absolute contraindications include acute vertebral fracture, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, bony metastasis at the target segment, spinal osteomyelitis, and medical instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how must direct cervical mobilization be performed in a geriatric patient?","choices":{"A":"Rapidly with a rotary thrust delivered through the barrier","B":"With maximal rotation applied before the barrier is engaged","C":"Briefly with a short lever thrust applied at end range","D":"With sustained traction and a forceful rotary stretch","E":"Slowly and gently with the barrier engaged before force"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Abrupt or rapid direct rotary forces on the cervical spine are contraindicated. Direct cervical mobilization must be slow and gentle, with the barrier clearly engaged before any force.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cervical spine rotary restrictions are listed as a relative contraindication to direct techniques.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which motion should be avoided in a patient with spinal stenosis and neurogenic claudication?","choices":{"A":"Forced lumbar flexion","B":"Forced lumbar extension","C":"Gentle cervical sidebending","D":"Passive hip abduction","E":"Diaphragmatic breathing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forced lumbar extension structurally narrows the exit foramina and central canal, so it is avoided in spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: forced lumbar extension narrows the exit foramina and central canal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old frail woman has lumbosacral, pelvic, and lower extremity somatic dysfunctions. According to the course, what is the logical treatment sequence?","choices":{"A":"Sacrum, then lumbar spine, then psoas major, with the ilia and pubes last","B":"Pubes, then sacrum, then ilia, with the lumbar spine and psoas major last","C":"Ilia, then sacrum, then psoas major, with the lumbar spine and pubes last","D":"Lumbar spine, then psoas major, then ilia and pubes, with the sacrum last","E":"Psoas major, then pubes, then sacrum, with the ilia and lumbar spine last"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course advises treating the lumbar spine, psoas major, ilia, and pubes before the sacrum because the sacrum must accommodate forces from both above and below.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: sacral work comes last in lumbosacral, pelvic, and lower extremity treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old frail man remains unresponsive after three OMT sessions. According to the course, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Increase the dose and frequency of treatment until the patient finally responds","B":"Switch to high-velocity thrust techniques at the restricted segments","C":"Continue the same plan for six more sessions before changing course","D":"Discharge the patient from OMT care and close the treatment plan","E":"Reevaluate the diagnosis for occult viscerosomatic or visceral pathology"},"correct":"E","explanation":"If a patient remains unresponsive after 2 or 3 treatments, the course says to reevaluate the diagnosis and investigate occult, unrecognized viscerosomatic etiologies or underlying visceral pathologies.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: viscerosomatic reflexes are addressed primarily by treating the underlying visceral pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman has increased pain at her chief complaint site 24 hours after soft tissue OMT. According to the course, how should the next visit be timed?","choices":{"A":"The next day at a higher dose to overcome the reaction","B":"Within 24 hours with repeated soft tissue work at higher force","C":"3 days to 2 weeks later to allow tissues to recover","D":"As soon as fatigue resolves using thrust techniques","E":"After 2 months with no treatment in the interim"},"correct":"C","explanation":"If rebound reactions occur, the course says patients should be given 3 days to 2 weeks between interventions to allow tissues to recover. Without rebound, acute conditions can be followed up in 2 to 3 days with a decreased initial dose.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: frail patients need a smaller stimulus because they are prone to exaggerated responses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man has decreased passive ankle dorsiflexion and an unsteady gait. According to the course, how does this contribute to falls?","choices":{"A":"It increases cervical extension during the stance phase","B":"It strengthens lateral pelvic stabilization while walking","C":"It shortens reaction time to unexpected perturbations","D":"It alters foot clearance and ground-reactive forces","E":"It lowers the visual field below the level of the horizon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of passive ankle dorsiflexion and restricted ankle mobility alter foot clearance and ground-reactive forces, directly contributing to gait instability.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: restoring ankle flexion and extension is essential for upright balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has increased upper thoracic flexion and decreased cervico-occipital extension. According to the course, why does this raise her fall risk?","choices":{"A":"The head-flexed position lowers the visual field and shifts the vestibular neutral position","B":"The flexed head raises the visual field above the horizon and overstimulates the vestibular system","C":"The posture increases ankle dorsiflexion and overloads ground-reactive force absorption at the foot","D":"The posture lengthens her Timed Up and Go time by weakening the gluteus medius","E":"The posture impairs the righting reflex by causing cervical radiculopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased upper thoracic flexion with decreased cervico-occipital extension forces a forward head-flexed position that lowers the visual field below the horizon, shifts the neutral position of the vestibular apparatus, and triggers postural instability.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a forward head-flexed position lowers the visual field below the horizon and shifts the vestibular neutral position.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man with cataracts loses visual cues for balance. According to the course, what does he do to compensate, and why does it fall short?","choices":{"A":"He relaxes the lower leg muscles, but cataracts impair that compensation","B":"He stiffens the lower leg muscles, but sarcopenia impairs that compensation","C":"He widens his gait and base of support, but presbycusis impairs that compensation","D":"He flexes the cervical spine downward, but sarcopenia impairs that compensation","E":"He leans backward at the hips, but reduced vibratory sense impairs that compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When visual cues are lost, patients stiffen the lower leg muscles to compensate, but age-related sarcopenia impairs this compensation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: progressive CNS decline also reduces vibratory sense and the righting reflex and prolongs reaction time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has severe osteoporosis. According to the course, which technique should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"High-force downward compression on the thoracic cage in prone or supine positions","B":"Gentle strain-counterstrain applied at regions distant from the thoracic cage","C":"Balanced ligamentous tension applied to the sacrum in a supine position","D":"Gentle myofascial release of the thoracic paraspinal muscles with the patient seated","E":"Respiratory-assisted articulation of the ribs using patient inhalation and exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In severe osteoporosis, high-force procedures that apply downward compression on the thoracic cage carry a high risk of costochondral, costovertebral, and midshaft rib fractures and must be avoided.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe osteoporosis raises the risk of rib fracture with high-force procedures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis has sacroiliac pain, warmth, and swelling from an acute flare. According to the course, what is contraindicated?","choices":{"A":"Strain-counterstrain at a distant region","B":"Balanced ligamentous tension to related tissues","C":"Direct-action OMT at the inflamed joint","D":"Myofascial release of related paraspinal tissue","E":"Gentle respiratory techniques"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Direct-action OMT must not be performed on acutely inflamed joints, such as sacroiliitis in ankylosing spondylitis or an acute rheumatoid arthritis flare, to avoid exacerbating joint inflammation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: active inflammatory joint disease is a relative contraindication to direct techniques.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has an acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy. According to the course, which approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Apply a high-velocity thrust at the level of the herniation to decompress the affected nerve root","B":"Avoid thrusts at the herniation level and use gentle indirect OMT at related or distant regions","C":"Apply forced rotation at the herniated segment to reduce the nucleus pulposus protrusion","D":"Defer OMT entirely until the radiculopathy has fully resolved and imaging is repeated","E":"Apply sustained high-force traction through the herniated segment to retract the nucleus pulposus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Direct thrusts at the level of herniation are contraindicated to avoid worsening nerve root compression. Safe management uses gentle indirect OMT such as strain-counterstrain, myofascial release, or balanced ligamentous tension at related or distant regions.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: acute herniation with active radiculopathy is a relative, not absolute, contraindication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — OMT principles in low back pain","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Osteopathic Considerations in Geriatrics","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does Still technique move a somatic dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"From its position of ease through its restrictive barrier with continuous force","B":"Directly into its restrictive barrier with a rapid, small-amplitude impulse","C":"Into its position of ease without crossing the restrictive barrier at any point","D":"Through its restrictive barrier using a patient isometric contraction against counterforce","E":"Toward the tender point of greatest comfort and then slowly back to neutral"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes Still technique as moving a somatic dysfunction from its position of ease (indirect) through its restrictive barrier (direct) while applying a continuous force vector.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this indirect-to-direct path is why the course calls Still a hybrid technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which dysfunctions does Still technique rapidly resolve in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Iliolumbar, posterior sacroiliac, and sacrotuberous ligament laxities","B":"Mesenteric, hepatic, and splenic visceral restrictions","C":"Pelvic, lumbar, and sacral dysfunctions","D":"Cervical, upper thoracic, and rib dysfunctions","E":"Lymphatic, venous, and fascial pump dysfunctions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that Still technique rapidly resolves pelvic, lumbar, and sacral dysfunctions and is highly effective for specific unilateral pelvic shears, the innominate upslips.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: an innominate upslip is a unilateral pelvic shear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is Still technique indicated to rapidly resolve in a low back pain treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"Chronic, recurrent facet stiffness with loss of passive range of motion","B":"Ligamentous laxity, with microinstability of the iliolumbar and sacrotuberous ligaments","C":"Localized, tender muscle and joint pain with dominant tender points","D":"Acute, complex joint restrictions in the pelvis and lumbar spine","E":"Central pain sensitization, with anxiety, depression, and poor sleep latency"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the course's low back pain table, Still technique is indicated to rapidly resolve acute, complex joint restrictions of the pelvic and lumbar regions using an indirect-to-direct hybrid path.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: match the technique to the dominant component of the dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which position does the DO exaggerate in FPR?","choices":{"A":"The restrictive barrier using a short lever","B":"The neutral position using a short lever","C":"The tender point using a rapid impulse","D":"The side opposite the dysfunction using a long lever","E":"The position of ease using a long lever"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric directs the DO to exaggerate the named dysfunction or the position of ease with a long lever after disengaging the spine with compression.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the phrase long lever is the rubric's wording for exaggerating the position of ease in FPR.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what does the DO flatten when performing FPR for an articular dysfunction of the neck?","choices":{"A":"The thoracic kyphosis","B":"The lumbar lordosis","C":"The cervical lordosis","D":"The sacral base","E":"The occipital condyles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric for cervical FPR has the DO flatten the cervical lordosis after diagnosing the somatic dysfunction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: FPR starts by removing the natural curve of the region being treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, in which position is the patient placed for FPR of the thoracic spine?","choices":{"A":"In the prone position","B":"In the seated position","C":"In the supine position","D":"In the standing position","E":"In the sidelying position"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient in the seated position, in contrast to the other body positions listed here.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric names the seated position for thoracic FPR.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which two types of somatic dysfunction are the common indications for Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Articular and myofascial somatic dysfunctions","B":"Visceral and lymphatic somatic dysfunctions","C":"Neurologic and vascular somatic dysfunctions","D":"Ligamentous and membranous somatic dysfunctions","E":"Cranial and sutural somatic dysfunctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric lists two common indications: articular somatic dysfunctions with intersegmental motion restriction, and myofascial somatic dysfunctions with muscle hypertonicity or fascial bind.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the two indications split into a joint component and a soft tissue component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which of these is an absolute contraindication to Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Mild to moderate joint instability in the area to be treated","B":"An unstable fracture affected by treatment positioning","C":"Moderate to severe intervertebral foraminal stenosis","D":"Severe sprains and strains of the area to be treated","E":"A congenital anomaly that limits the needed position"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists four absolute contraindications. The first is an unstable fracture affected by treatment positioning. The other options are on the relative list.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric lists four absolute contraindications and six relative ones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which of these is a relative contraindication to Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Treatment positioning that exacerbates neurologic or life-threatening symptoms","B":"Mild restriction of a single intersegmental motion","C":"A somatic dysfunction with a clear position of ease","D":"Vertebrobasilar insufficiency or carotid pathology","E":"Fascial bind in the thoracolumbar region"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric lists vertebrobasilar insufficiency and/or carotid pathology among its six relative contraindications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric's relative list has six items, and this one is vascular.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the DO stand for seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Seated at the side of the table","B":"Standing at the foot of the table","C":"Kneeling in front of the patient","D":"Seated facing the patient's knees","E":"Standing behind the patient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For the seated cervical technique the patient is seated and the DO is standing behind the patient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: for the supine cervical version the DO is instead seated behind the patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where do the index finger and thumb of the monitoring hand contact the spine in seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"The transverse processes of the affected segment","B":"The spinous process of the segment above the affected one","C":"The mastoid processes on both sides of the skull","D":"The occiput and the first rib on the affected side","E":"The angles of the mandible on both sides of the face"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The monitoring hand's index finger and thumb contact the transverse processes of the affected segment.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the monitoring finger stays on the transverse processes of the affected segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what does the DO's other hand do in seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Holds the patient's chin to guide rotation","B":"Contacts the top of the patient's head","C":"Cradles the occiput from below with the palm","D":"Rests on the contralateral shoulder of the patient","E":"Supports the patient's forehead from the front"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the seated cervical technique the DO's other hand is placed in contact with the top of the patient's head, as stated in the lab rubric.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same hand placement is used in the supine cervical version.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO has positioned a patient's neck toward the position of ease and introduced compression to the dysfunctional segment during Still technique. According to the lab rubric, what is the next movement?","choices":{"A":"Hold the position of ease and slowly release the compression","B":"Thrust through the restrictive barrier with a maximal rotary force","C":"Move through neutral into the previously restricted range","D":"Add traction to the head and return directly to the neutral position","E":"Repeat the exaggeration of the position of ease on the opposite side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After compression, the monitoring arm simultaneously reduces flexion or extension and sidebending and rotates the spine through neutral and then into the previously restricted range.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this passage through neutral into the restricted range is the direct phase of the hybrid technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has acute psoas spasm with a pelvic side shift, and the upper lumbar key dysfunction has been treated. According to the course, which approach releases the pelvic fascia?","choices":{"A":"Local heat followed by passive rest","B":"Still technique or myofascial release","C":"Counterstrain of the anterior psoas tender points","D":"Muscle energy for the upper lumbar key segment","E":"A cranial vault hold to balance the dura"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists HVLA or muscle energy for the L1 and L2 key dysfunction, counterstrain for the anterior psoas and iliacus tender points, and Still technique or myofascial release to release the pelvis. Local heat typically worsens acute psoas spasm.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: treating the key upper lumbar segment first prevents lumbosacral and sacroiliac dysfunctions from recurring.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 — osteopathic evaluation of the low back pain patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO compares the seated and supine cervical Still techniques in the lab rubric. According to the rubric, which step appears only in the seated version?","choices":{"A":"Stabilizing the neck below the affected segment with the monitoring hand's other fingers","B":"Placing the monitoring hand's index finger over the spinous process of the affected segment","C":"Positioning the head and spine toward the position of ease with flexion or extension before compression","D":"Rotating the spine through neutral with the monitoring arm while compression is held","E":"Reducing the compression and passively returning the patient to neutral at the end of the technique"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The seated cervical technique includes a step in which the other fingers of the monitoring hand stabilize the neck below the affected segment. The supine rubric has no such step, and its other technique steps match the seated version.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the supine cervical rubric has no step for stabilizing the neck below the affected segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO is performing FPR for an articular dysfunction of the thoracic spine and has just flattened the thoracic kyphosis. According to the lab rubric, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Exaggerate the dysfunction toward the barrier","B":"Return the patient to neutral","C":"Reassess the somatic dysfunction","D":"Diagnose the somatic dysfunction along the thoracic spine","E":"Disengage the spine with compression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After flattening the kyphosis, the rubric has the DO disengage the spine with compression.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: FPR is performed in an ordered sequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A student compares FPR with Still technique in the lab rubrics. According to the rubrics, how does the movement that follows compression differ?","choices":{"A":"FPR exaggerates the position of ease, whereas Still passes through neutral into the restricted range","B":"FPR passes through neutral into the restricted range, whereas Still exaggerates the position of ease","C":"FPR thrusts through the restrictive barrier, whereas Still holds the spine at neutral","D":"FPR holds the restrictive barrier, whereas Still stops at the position of ease with a long lever","E":"FPR stays in neutral throughout, whereas Still holds the spine at the position of ease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In FPR the DO exaggerates the position of ease with a long lever. In Still technique the DO moves through neutral into the previously restricted range, and only afterward reduces compression and returns to neutral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: only Still moves the segment through neutral into the previously restricted range.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO is reviewing the lab rubrics for FPR and Still technique. According to the rubrics, which element do both techniques include?","choices":{"A":"A high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust at the barrier","B":"A patient isometric contraction against counterforce","C":"Compression to the dysfunctional segment","D":"Flattening of the thoracic kyphosis at the start","E":"Sustained traction applied to the head throughout the technique"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both rubrics use compression to disengage the spine. FPR also flattens the lordosis or kyphosis, and neither technique uses a thrust or an isometric contraction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: both techniques apply compression to the dysfunctional segment, and FPR describes it as disengagement of the spine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has almost no intersegmental motion in the cervical segments to be treated. According to the lab rubric, how is Still technique classified for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Relatively contraindicated","B":"Indicated with a longer lever","C":"Indicated with compression omitted","D":"Absolutely contraindicated","E":"Safe when performed in the supine position"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Severe loss of intersegmental motion secondary to spondylosis, osteoarthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis in the area to be treated is on the rubric's absolute contraindication list.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe loss of intersegmental motion from spondylosis, osteoarthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis is an absolute contraindication, not a relative one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has marked ligamentous laxity with severe instability in the lumbar region a DO plans to treat with Still technique. According to the lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Do not perform Still technique at that segment","B":"Use Still technique with caution as a relative case","C":"Avoid Still technique if mild to moderate instability exists","D":"Modify Still technique by omitting the compression step","E":"Treat with Still technique after using a longer lever"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe joint instability in the area to be treated is an absolute contraindication on the rubric. Mild to moderate joint instability is only a relative contraindication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the severity of joint instability decides whether the contraindication is absolute or relative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with no neurologic deficit has a herniated cervical disc, and the DO recognizes that Still positioning could exacerbate it. According to the lab rubric, how is this classified?","choices":{"A":"An absolute contraindication to seated Still technique","B":"A relative contraindication needing positional caution","C":"An indication for the seated Still technique approach","D":"A contraindication limited to the supine position","E":"An incidental finding without relevance to positioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A herniated disc where the positioning could exacerbate the condition is one of the six relative contraindications to Still technique.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a herniated disc is a relative, not absolute, contraindication to Still technique when the positioning could exacerbate it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO treats a right-sided lumbar somatic dysfunction with seated Still technique. According to the lab rubric, where do the DO's monitoring finger and the hand of the other arm, which crosses over the opposite shoulder, end up?","choices":{"A":"Finger on the left prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's left shoulder","B":"Finger on the right prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's left shoulder","C":"Finger on the right spinous process, other hand on the patient's right iliac crest and the hip joint","D":"Finger on the left prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's right shoulder","E":"Finger on the right prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's right shoulder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The monitoring finger, on the same side as the somatic dysfunction, contacts the prominent transverse process. The DO's other arm crosses over the opposite shoulder around the superior chest wall, and its hand rests on the patient's shoulder on the side of the dysfunction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same positioning is used for the thoracic Still technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old frail woman has an articular dysfunction of the cervical spine, and the DO is performing FPR. According to the lab rubric, when does the DO return the patient to neutral?","choices":{"A":"After the patient reports relief of the tenderness","B":"After 30 to 60 seconds of sustained compression","C":"Immediately without exaggerating the position of ease","D":"After 3 to 5 seconds in the exaggerated position","E":"After the segment passes through the restrictive barrier"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric has the DO demonstrate and verbalize the return to neutral after 3 to 5 seconds, followed by reassessment of the somatic dysfunction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: know the hold time before the return to neutral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old frail woman has a thoracic dysfunction with neural reflex and nociceptive irritability. According to the course, which approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"A high-velocity thrust through the barrier","B":"FPR as an indirect, passive technique","C":"Forceful, sustained traction at end range","D":"Rapid direct articulation of the segment","E":"Repeated springing of the rib cage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course manages neural reflex and nociceptive irritability in the frail patient with indirect, non-threatening, passive techniques such as strain-counterstrain and facilitated positional release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: strain-counterstrain and FPR are both passive and indirect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for the frail patient","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Visceral I","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is visceral mobility defined?","choices":{"A":"Inherent, active organ motion that arises without external forces","B":"Slow rhythmic motion around the embryologic axes of origin","C":"Fluid exchange across the sheaths of the visceral fascia","D":"Reflex motor output to the segmentally related paraspinal muscles","E":"Passive organ movement in response to external mechanical forces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course defines visceral mobility as the passive movement of viscera in response to external mechanical forces such as gravity, digestion, physical activity, and diaphragmatic expansion. Motility is the inherent, active, independent motion of each organ.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is passive and tested with respiration. Motility is intrinsic and assessed along the organ's longitudinal axis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which two phases make up visceral motility?","choices":{"A":"Inhalation and exhalation","B":"Flexion and extension","C":"Expir and inspir","D":"Systole and diastole","E":"Abduction and adduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes motility as a slow rhythmic motion with two phases. Expir moves organs toward the midline, and inspir moves them away from the midline in a rotation around their embryologic axes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: motility runs at a different frequency than breathing and is described as associated with the craniosacral rhythm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, in how many dimensions must the thoracic and abdominal viscera move during respiration?","choices":{"A":"Three dimensions, inferior-superior, lateral, and anterior-posterior","B":"Two dimensions, inferior-superior and lateral, without anterior-posterior","C":"Three dimensions, medial-lateral, rotational, and torsional","D":"One dimension, inferior-superior, without lateral or anterior-posterior","E":"Two dimensions, lateral and rotational, around the embryologic axis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that during quiet and forceful respiration the viscera must move in three dimensions, inferior-superior, lateral, and anterior-posterior, as the diaphragm, pelvic floor, and viscera descend with inhalation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral mobility follows the diaphragm during inhalation. COMLEX: loss of this three-dimensional glide marks a mobility restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which situation is an absolute contraindication to a splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"Chronic constipation from slow transit","B":"Adhesions after pelvic or colon surgery","C":"Functional nausea with early satiety","D":"Stress urinary incontinence in women","E":"Splenomegaly from acute mononucleosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated in an enlarged spleen, such as acute mononucleosis or hematologic malignancy, because of the severe risk of splenic rupture.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: infectious mononucleosis is a listed contraindication in both the lecture and the Week 4 lab rubric.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which findings suggest an aortic aneurysm that needs medical evaluation?","choices":{"A":"Borborygmi with a lateral pulsation narrower than 1 inch","B":"Rebound tenderness with absent bowel sounds","C":"Saddle anesthesia with new urinary retention","D":"A bruit with a lateral pulsation wider than 1 inch","E":"Fever with localized spinal bony tenderness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course describes an abdominal aortic aneurysm as severe tearing mid-back or abdominal pain, an abdominal bruit, or a lateral aortic pulsation wider than 1 inch. The other findings point to surgical abdomen, cauda equina syndrome, and spinal infection.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: know the bruit and the 1-inch limit for a normal lateral aortic pulsation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique is contraindicated in a patient with acute hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal ganglion inhibition at the celiac level","B":"Mesenteric lift toward the midline","C":"Liver pump or forceful rib cage compression","D":"Colonic stimulation along the descending colon","E":"General listening with a hand on the head"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that forceful compression of the rib cage or a liver pump is contraindicated in active hepatitis to prevent parenchymal liver damage.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: match each contraindication to the structure that is at risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Reduce hypersympathetic outflow to the gut","B":"Raise sphincter tone to slow intestinal transit","C":"Clear portal venous congestion through rib compression","D":"Increase vagal output to the stomach","E":"Relieve traction on the stretch-sensitive mesenteric roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Abdominal ganglion inhibition reduces hypersympathetic outflow to the gastrointestinal and pelvic viscera.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ganglion inhibition targets the celiac and mesenteric ganglia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the main physiologic goal of the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"Reduce hypersympathetic outflow to the gastrointestinal viscera","B":"Free the lymphatic channels running through the mesentery","C":"Decongest the portal venous system of metabolic waste","D":"Mobilize stored red and white blood cells into circulation","E":"Raise vagal tone to speed gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spleen stores red and white blood cells. Compression changes applied to it mobilize those cells into active circulation, which helps patients who need heightened immune defense or have some anemias.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the splenic pump is used for immune support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course's visceral techniques matrix, what is the goal of colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Mobilize stored blood cells to fight systemic infection","B":"Restore sliding and distensibility of bowel segments","C":"Reduce sympathetic outflow at the celiac ganglion","D":"Promote lymph flow through the hepatic channels","E":"Free the lymphatic channels from mesenteric stretch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The matrix lists colonic stimulation as resolving mechanical restrictions of colonic segments to restore sliding and gliding, and the technique description adds distensibility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation mobilizes segments of the large intestine in several directions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, continuous afferent bombardment from a dysfunctional organ leaves spinal interneurons in what state?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition, a chronic hypoexcitable state","B":"Reciprocal inhibition, a transient relaxed state","C":"Autogenic inhibition, a stretch-induced silent state","D":"Facilitation, a chronic hyperexcitable state","E":"Spinal shock, a flaccid areflexic state"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent visceral afferent bombardment keeps spinal interneurons at that segment chronically hyperexcitable, called segmental facilitation or central sensitization. The threshold for motor and sympathetic output is lowered.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitation lowers firing threshold at the dorsal horn. COMLEX: facilitated segments show tissue texture change and restricted motion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what end-feel characterizes somatic dysfunction of visceral origin?","choices":{"A":"A hard bony feel, at an absolute articular barrier","B":"Reluctance to motion, a soft rubbery feel","C":"An empty feel, with severe guarding","D":"A springy feel, that is symmetric","E":"Free motion, with a sharp capsular stop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that somatic dysfunction of visceral origin shows a characteristic reluctance to motion, a soft, rubbery, or hesitant end-feel, rather than an absolute hard articular barrier.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reluctance to motion suggests a viscerosomatic reflex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where are Chapman's points typically found in patients with gastrointestinal or pelvic disease?","choices":{"A":"Around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus","B":"Over the occiput, posterior neck, and shoulder","C":"Along the medial forearm, wrist, and thenar eminence","D":"Over the sternum, clavicles, and upper ribs","E":"Around the knee, ankle, and plantar foot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes Chapman's points as localized, gangliform, edematous fascial congestion typically found around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus in patients with gastrointestinal or pelvic disease.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman's points are reflex zones of fascial congestion that reflect visceral stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, before any visceral OMT is applied, what must the physician do?","choices":{"A":"A direct mesenteric lift to find the restriction","B":"A splenic pump to mobilize immune cells","C":"Colonic stimulation along the course of the bowel","D":"Home stretching and hydration instructions","E":"A safety check for red flags and surgical abdomen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that the physician must first rule out red-flag conditions or a surgical abdomen before any visceral OMT.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for acute abdomen, aneurysm, and other red flags before treating.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which neurovascular roots are palpated and released before direct treatment of the organs they supply?","choices":{"A":"Femoral and popliteal arteries with the inguinal nodes","B":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia and arteries","C":"Carotid and subclavian arteries with the cervical nodes","D":"Inferior vena cava and azygos veins with thoracic nodes","E":"Renal and gonadal arteries with the lumbar plexus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that treatment begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes. Releasing these neurovascular roots must precede direct treatment of the organs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the celiac and mesenteric roots are the neurovascular roots of the abdominal organs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, when are related lower thoracic and lumbar spinal segments and the rib cage treated?","choices":{"A":"Before the safety check for red flags","B":"After direct organ treatment is complete","C":"Before the direct treatment of the organs","D":"In place of the arterial and ganglia releases","E":"Before the neurovascular root releases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course has the physician first treat the associated lower thoracic and lumbar segments and rib cage to reduce sympathetic outflow to the abdomen, then perform direct visceral work such as mesenteric lifts or colonic stimulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: normalize sympathetic outflow through the spine and ribs before direct organ work.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has persistent abdominal congestion after surgery, with palpable fascial tethering. According to the course, how does visceral manipulation improve her circulation?","choices":{"A":"It raises sympathetic outflow to increase peristaltic pressure","B":"It lengthens the lymphatic contraction cycle to one per minute","C":"It frees the attachments that carry vessels and lymphatics","D":"It fuses adhesions so the organ moves as one unit","E":"It increases afferent bombardment to reset facilitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteries, veins, and lymphatics supplying the organs travel through the visceral attachments and fascial sheaths. Visceral manipulation removes the mechanical constriction there, improving arterial perfusion and venous and lymphatic drainage.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: fascial tethering after surgery impairs fluid exchange. Releasing the attachments restores it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — mesenteric lift to free the lymphatic channels","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is explaining how lymph moves toward the heart in a patient with abdominal congestion. According to the course, at what rate do lymphatic channels contract at rest?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 times per minute","B":"12 to 16 times per minute","C":"20 to 30 times per minute","D":"60 to 80 times per minute","E":"5 to 8 times per minute"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that lymphatic channels contract rhythmically at 5 to 8 times per minute at rest to move fluid toward the heart. Visceral techniques alter abdominal pressure gradients to move congested lymph.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymph flow depends on intrinsic vessel contraction and pressure gradients. COMLEX: pumps and lifts change those gradients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — liver and splenic pump goals","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has chronic visceral irritation with sustained sympathetic overactivity. According to the course, what does visceral manipulation do for his autonomic balance?","choices":{"A":"It amplifies visceral afferent input to reset sympathetic tone","B":"It dampens nociceptive input and lowers cortisol output","C":"It raises circulating cortisol to restore autonomic balance","D":"It increases HPA axis activation to speed healing","E":"It removes the chemosensitivity of visceral afferent receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that chronic visceral irritation overstimulates afferent pathways and the sympathetic nervous system. Visceral manipulation dampens the nociceptive barrage, down-regulates HPA axis activation, lowers cortisol, and restores balanced sympathetic and parasympathetic tone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic stress raises cortisol and allostatic load. COMLEX: reducing nociceptive input lowers sympathetic drive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — HPA axis and cortisol","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student has fever, sore throat, fatigue, and an enlarged spleen from acute infectious mononucleosis. His physician wants to boost immune function. According to the course, what is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Avoid the splenic pump because of the risk of splenic rupture","B":"Perform a gentle splenic pump to mobilize white blood cells","C":"Defer the splenic pump until the fever has resolved","D":"Perform the splenic pump with the fingertips for precise contact","E":"Perform the splenic pump timed with exhalation to lower rupture risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated in infectious mononucleosis or splenomegaly because of the critical danger of splenic rupture. Fingertip contact is also contraindicated for abdominal organs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the stated goal of immune support does not override the contraindication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — recognizing contraindications in the lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has severe mid-back and abdominal pain, a midline abdominal bruit, and a widened lateral aortic pulsation. According to the course, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition to relieve sympathetic spasm","B":"Perform a mesenteric lift to decongest the intestines","C":"Withhold abdominal OMT and arrange emergent evaluation","D":"Perform colonic stimulation to restore bowel function","E":"Observe at home and recheck in one week"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that abdominal visceral OMT is absolutely contraindicated when an abdominal aortic aneurysm is suspected, which is a life-threatening emergency suspected from a bruit or a widened lateral pulsation of the aorta.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: suspect AAA with severe tearing back or abdominal pain, a bruit, or a pulsation wider than 1 inch.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — contraindications requiring emergent evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has distension, vomiting, and high-pitched tinkling sounds on auscultation. According to the course, which interpretation and decision fit?","choices":{"A":"Paralytic ileus that resolves with gentle abdominal OMT","B":"Functional constipation that is suitable for a mesenteric lift","C":"Parasympathetic hypoactivity that is suitable for ganglion inhibition","D":"Mechanical obstruction needing urgent surgical evaluation","E":"Normal peristalsis that is suitable for a liver pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds indicate an active obstruction, while a complete absence of bowel sounds indicates a paralytic ileus. Both contraindicate direct abdominal manipulation and require immediate medical or surgical evaluation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: tinkling sounds mean obstruction, and absent sounds mean ileus. Neither is treated with direct abdominal OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — obstruction and ileus as contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has chronic constipation. According to the course, restricted mobility of which colonic segment is classically associated with it?","choices":{"A":"The cecum and ileocecal valve","B":"Sigmoid colon or rectum","C":"The hepatic flexure","D":"The ascending colon","E":"The transverse colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that osteopathic literature links a lack of sigmoid colon or rectal mobility to constipation, whereas a lack of cecal mobility is linked to diarrhea.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation targets the restricted segment. Sigmoid or rectal work addresses constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has bloating and a heavy, congested feeling in the lower abdomen. According to the course, which technique and goal fit best?","choices":{"A":"Splenic pump to mobilize stored red cells into circulation","B":"Liver pump to clear portal venous congestion","C":"Celiac ganglion inhibition to mobilize stored white cells","D":"Colonic stimulation to compress the stretch-sensitive mesentery","E":"Mesenteric lift to decongest bowel and free lymphatics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The mesenteric lift decongests the intestines, improves arterial flow, and frees the lymphatic channels running through the stretch-sensitive mesentery. It relieves mechanical stress on the mesenteric roots.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the mesenteric lift treats mesenteric tension and congestion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man recovering from abdominal surgery has sluggish bowel function and spastic sphincters. According to the course, which technique and goal fit best?","choices":{"A":"Ganglion inhibition to lower sympathetic tone and restore transit","B":"Splenic pump to enhance immune surveillance and lymph return","C":"Liver pump to drive fluid through the hepatic lymphatic channels","D":"Mesenteric lift to mobilize stored blood cells into circulation","E":"Colonic stimulation to mobilize stored leukocytes into circulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypersympathetic stimulation restricts peristalsis and increases sphincter tone. Abdominal ganglion inhibition reduces that outflow, relaxes smooth muscle, decreases sphincter spasm, and restores peristalsis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic outflow slows gut motility. COMLEX: inhibit the celiac and mesenteric ganglia to restore motility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man passing a kidney stone has widespread back muscle tightness. According to the course, which somatic finding fits?","choices":{"A":"A single-segment Type II dysfunction at C2","B":"An isolated rib head dysfunction at T1","C":"Flaccid paraspinal muscles with a diffuse soft tissue deficit","D":"Rigid lumbar group curves with paraspinal spasm","E":"A hard-end-feel anterior innominate rotation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course gives the example that the passage of a kidney stone down the ureter can create diffuse paraspinal spasm and rigid lumbar group curves, a facilitated-segment presentation of visceral origin.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: visceral pathology produces segmentally related paraspinal hypertonicity and persistent group curves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — viscerosomatic reflex arcs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with a Crohn flare has a flexed, rotated lumbar spine and severe low back pain from a tight muscle. According to the course, what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic floor weakness compresses the L1 nerve root against the psoas","B":"Bowel inflammation irritates psoas fascia and drives chronic contraction","C":"Diaphragmatic paralysis tilts the lumbar spine into flexion","D":"Direct invasion of the vertebral body by the colon rotates the lumbar spine","E":"Loss of hip abductor tone rotates the sacrum and strains the psoas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes reflex psoas major spasm, in which local inflammation of the colon, appendix, or pelvic organs irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces the muscle into chronic spasm, causing a flexed, rotated lumbar spine with severe low back pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: inflammatory bowel disease, appendicitis, and pelvic inflammation can present as psoas spasm and low back pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — viscerosomatic relationships","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has active gastric inflammation with a viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which temperature finding is expected on exam?","choices":{"A":"Localized cooling over the related paraspinal tissues","B":"No temperature change at the involved segments","C":"Localized heat over the related paraspinal tissues","D":"Diffuse cooling across the entire back","E":"Heat confined to the distal extremities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that active viscerosomatic reflexes are often accompanied by localized cutaneous heat over the segmentally related paraspinal tissues, palpable on the physical exam.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: acute viscerosomatic reflexes show warmth, tissue texture change, and reluctance to motion in the related segments.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — somatic findings of visceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has severe visceral irritation, and her paraspinal dysfunction is maintained by a highly active viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which approach fits?","choices":{"A":"Deep direct mesenteric traction to break the reflex","B":"Gentle direct stretch over the inflamed organ","C":"High-velocity thrust at the facilitated segment","D":"Direct fingertip pressure over the inflamed organ","E":"Gentle indirect somatic OMT such as counterstrain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course advises avoiding direct, aggressive manipulation over the organ in severe visceral irritation. The physician should prioritize gentle indirect somatic OMT, such as strain-counterstrain, cranial, or balanced ligamentous tension, directed at segmentally related somatic structures with minimal stimulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: dose down when the reflex is highly active, and use the soft palmar surfaces rather than fingertips on abdominal organs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — treating the low-reserve patient with minimal stimulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with sluggish bowel function and a core rehabilitation need finishes his visceral treatment. According to the course, which self-care advice fits?","choices":{"A":"Bed rest, fluid restriction, and avoidance of core exercise","B":"Heavy lifting, high-intensity crunches, and caffeine loading","C":"Daily deep self-massage, laxative use, and fasting","D":"Customized hydration, diet changes, and abdominal bracing","E":"Prolonged sitting, low fiber intake, and abdominal binding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course pairs visceral OMT with supportive self-care, including customized hydration advice, nutritional modifications, and core rehabilitation exercises such as posterior pelvic tilts, abdominal bracing, or the drawing-in maneuver.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: self-care supports lumbosacral stability and lower gastrointestinal regulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — dosing and post-treatment care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has abdominal congestion and restricted breathing. According to the course, why is thoracic diaphragm release included in her plan?","choices":{"A":"Diaphragm motion drives passive visceral mobility and lymph drainage","B":"Diaphragm release replaces the need for ganglion and arterial releases","C":"Diaphragm release raises sympathetic outflow to the abdominal organs","D":"Diaphragm release fuses fascial adhesions between neighboring organs","E":"Diaphragm release increases cortisol output from the adrenal glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that release of the thoracic diaphragm must be included, because optimizing diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm drives visceral descent with inhalation. COMLEX: include diaphragm release alongside spinal and rib treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — thoracoabdominal diaphragm release","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Visceral II","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"A hand placed on top of a standing patient's head senses a pull toward the front of the body. According to the course, what does this suggest?","choices":{"A":"Spinal, dural, or cranial dysfunction","B":"Extremity or paired organ dysfunction","C":"Muscular, tendinous, or ligamentous strain","D":"Normal global fascial balance with no pull","E":"Visceral, rib, or sternal restriction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In general listening, an anterior pull points to a visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: general listening uses the global fascial continuity model to choose the region to examine next.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"In which position is local listening performed, using layer palpation?","choices":{"A":"Standing, with a hand resting on top of the head","B":"Prone, with lateral pressure over the flanks and back","C":"Supine, with slow compression through the abdomen","D":"Seated, with a hand over the sternum and upper ribs","E":"Side-lying, with a quick thrust over the viscera"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For local listening the patient is supine and the physician slowly compresses through the abdominal wall, evaluating the tissue pull and center of tension at each layer from skin to peritoneum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: general listening is done standing at the head, and local listening is done supine at the abdomen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is impaired visceral mobility reflected on examination?","choices":{"A":"Reduced respiratory motion with rubbery resistance","B":"Increased respiratory excursion with a springy end-feel","C":"Absent motility with normal respiratory motion","D":"A hard bony block at the articular surface","E":"A rapid intrinsic rhythm at the craniosacral rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Impaired mobility shows a reduced range of respiratory motion, altered distensibility of attachments, and a characteristic reluctance to motion or rubbery resistance.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is the passive motion tested with respiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, in a direct visceral treatment the organ or its attachments are guided where?","choices":{"A":"Toward the position of ease","B":"Along the organ's motility rhythm without engaging tension","C":"Into the direction of tension with a sudden thrust","D":"Into the direction of tension","E":"Away from the organ's attachments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In direct visceral treatment the physician engages the restrictive barrier, guiding the dysfunctional organ or its attachments gently but firmly into the direction of tension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct treatment works at the restrictive barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, toward what end point does the physician guide the tissues in an indirect visceral treatment?","choices":{"A":"A fascial balance point with minimal tension","B":"Into the restrictive barrier of the dysfunction","C":"To a point of maximal fascial stretch and tension","D":"Beyond the end of the normal physiologic range","E":"To the point of complete adhesion release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Indirect treatment moves the organ or attachments away from the restrictive barrier toward the position of ease, a point of fascial balance or pathologic neutral where tension is minimized.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: indirect treatment seeks the balance point, then releases as the tissues relax.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the aim of a direct visceral treatment?","choices":{"A":"Minimize tension at a pathologic neutral point","B":"Elongate shortened tissue and restore its stretch","C":"Use a respiratory hold to release toward ease","D":"Reduce sympathetic outflow through the ganglia","E":"Avoid any loading of the dysfunctional attachments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Direct stretch aims to elongate shortened, inelastic connective tissues, break down fascial adhesions, and restore normal physiologic distensibility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct technique suits shortened, inelastic fascia and adhesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where does the pylorus project on the surface?","choices":{"A":"At the umbilicus just left of midline","B":"Two fingers below the umbilicus at midline","C":"At the xiphoid process left of midline","D":"At the left costal margin at the midclavicular line","E":"Four to five fingers above the umbilicus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pylorus projects four to five fingers superior to the umbilicus, just to the right of the midline, and feels palpatorily like an almond in size and firmness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach lies in the left upper quadrant, and the pylorus crosses toward the right of midline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which ribs protect the spleen, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Right ribs 7 to 10","B":"Left ribs 4 to 7","C":"Right ribs 8 to 12","D":"Left ribs 8 to 12","E":"Left ribs 6 to 9"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spleen sits deep in the posterolateral left upper quadrant, protected by the lower left rib cage spanning ribs 8 to 12. The liver lies under the right ribs 7 to 10.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the splenic pump contacts the left costal margin, and the liver pump contacts the lower right ribs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where is the gallbladder located on the surface?","choices":{"A":"Below the liver edge at the right anterior axillary line","B":"At the right anterior superior iliac spine","C":"Below the liver edge at the right midnipple line","D":"Two fingers lateral to the xiphoid on the left","E":"Above the umbilicus at the left midclavicular line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The gallbladder lies just inferior to the lower edge of the liver on the right at the midnipple line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder disease can refer pain to the right shoulder through the diaphragm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the goal of a mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Decongest bowel and free lymphatic channels","B":"Increase biliary flow through sphincter relaxation","C":"Treat postsurgical ileus by paraspinal inhibition","D":"Reduce sympathetic outflow through celiac inhibition","E":"Restore cecal and sigmoid sliding and gliding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The mesenteric lift decongests the intestines, improves arterial blood flow, and frees the lymphatic channels in the stretch-sensitive mesentery, relieving mechanical stress.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pulling bowel loops toward their attachments breaks the cycle of stretch, pain, and sympathetic tone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the goal of the liver pump?","choices":{"A":"Raise gastric motility through vagal facilitation","B":"Decrease sympathetic hyperactivity of the gut","C":"Assist hepatic detoxification and mobilize lymph","D":"Free the lymphatic channels of the mesentery","E":"Treat postsurgical paralytic ileus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The liver pump assists hepatic detoxification and mobilizes lymph. The liver has a rich bed of lymphatic channels and portal venous vessels, and the compression gradient also enhances Kupffer cell interaction with toxins and antigens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are the liver's resident macrophages.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which change in blood counts has been shown with the splenic pump, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Decreased reticulocyte counts with unchanged leukocytes","B":"Increased reticulocyte and leukocyte counts","C":"Increased platelet and eosinophil counts","D":"Decreased red cell and platelet counts","E":"Increased hematocrit with decreased leukocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Compression-gradient changes applied to the spleen mobilize stored red and white blood cells into circulation, which has been shown to increase reticulocyte and leukocyte counts.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course ties the splenic pump to blood cell counts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, visceral OMT is highly indicated for which problem?","choices":{"A":"Acute appendicitis with rebound tenderness","B":"A suspected abdominal aortic aneurysm","C":"Acute bowel obstruction with tinkling sounds","D":"Active internal hemorrhage with guarding","E":"Functional gut discomfort or constipation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists functional gastrointestinal discomfort, diarrhea, and constipation as indications for visceral OMT.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the Week 4 lab rubric lists a known diagnosis with a functional visceral component, or a viscerosomatic reflex, as indications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated in which infection?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcal pharyngitis","B":"Influenza without splenomegaly","C":"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection","D":"Infectious mononucleosis","E":"Acute otitis media"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated in infectious mononucleosis or splenomegaly because of the critical danger of splenic rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mononucleosis commonly causes splenomegaly, and splenic rupture is a recognized complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hand contact does the course require when performing visceral OMT?","choices":{"A":"The fingertips for precise localization of organs","B":"The soft palmar surfaces of the fingers and hands","C":"The tips of the thumbs for deep penetration","D":"The knuckles for broad even pressure over the organ","E":"The point of the elbow for deep contact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that clinicians must use the soft, flat palmar surfaces of the fingers and hands, never the fingertips, to prevent tissue injury or laceration of delicate internal organs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: fingertip contact on abdominal organs is a safety error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man stands while a physician rests a hand on top of his head and senses a posterior pull. According to the course, which region is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"Visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction","B":"Paired bilateral visceral dysfunction","C":"Extremity restriction","D":"Gastric fascial adhesion","E":"Spinal or dural dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A posterior pull in general listening points to a spinal or dural dysfunction. An anterior pull indicates visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction, and a lateral pull suggests the extremities or paired bilateral viscera.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: read the direction of global pull first, then localize with layer palpation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — general listening in the treatment plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has epigastric discomfort. According to the course, which palpatory finding supports localized inflammation of her stomach?","choices":{"A":"Tissue that feels softer than normal with no thermal change","B":"Tissue that feels normal with no thermal change","C":"Tissue that feels harder than normal with a thermal change","D":"Tissue that feels softer than normal with a thermal change","E":"Tissue that feels harder than normal with no thermal change"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that localized inflammation and density changes are diagnosed by palpating tissue consistency, such as an irritated stomach feeling harder than normal, and by noting manual thermal changes over the organ.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: inflammation changes density and temperature, both of which can be felt over the organ.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — somatic and tissue findings of visceral disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician wants to assess the inherent motility of a patient's organ. According to the course, how is the hand placed?","choices":{"A":"Firmly across the organ's short axis","B":"Gently along the organ's longitudinal axis","C":"On top of the head to feel the global pull","D":"Deeply into each tissue layer in turn","E":"Over the rib cage during forced respiration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that motility, the organ's inherent, active, independent motion, is assessed by gently placing a hand along the organ's longitudinal axis. Mobility is assessed by motion-testing the attachments.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is the passive respiratory motion, and motility is the intrinsic rhythm felt along the longitudinal axis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — mobility versus motility","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician guides a restricted organ attachment to a point of minimal tension, has the patient hold the breath in inhalation, and then works with the exhalation. Which approach is this, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Direct treatment with respiratory cooperation","B":"Indirect treatment with a high-velocity thrust","C":"Direct treatment with a thrust at end-exhalation","D":"Indirect treatment with respiratory cooperation","E":"Layer palpation without any treatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indirect treatment moves tissues away from the restrictive barrier toward the position of ease. The physician can add respiratory cooperation, having the patient hold the breath in inhalation to build tension and then engaging a new barrier during exhalation as the tissues release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: indirect visceral technique goes toward ease and can use a breath hold to enhance release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — indirect functional technique","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"During an indirect visceral treatment the patient holds the breath in inhalation. According to the course, what does the physician do during the following exhalation?","choices":{"A":"Engages a new barrier as the tissues relax","B":"Applies a high-velocity thrust through the barrier","C":"Returns the organ to the original restriction","D":"Maintains the breath hold until the patient tires","E":"Compresses the organ against the diaphragm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes the patient holding the breath in inhalation to build tension. The physician then engages a new barrier during exhalation as the tissues relax and release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: respiratory cooperation uses the patient's breathing phases as the treatment force.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — respiratory cooperation in the lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has fixed fascial adhesions after abdominal surgery. Which approach does the course describe for breaking down these restrictions?","choices":{"A":"Indirect treatment toward the position of ease","B":"Observation without manual treatment","C":"Direct treatment into the restrictive barrier","D":"Gentle cranial balancing at the occiput","E":"Counterstrain at an anterior thoracic tender point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Direct treatment guides the tissue firmly into the direction of tension, aiming to elongate shortened, inelastic connective tissue, break down fascial adhesions, and restore normal distensibility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct stretch is described for adhesions and shortened fascia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — direct versus indirect functional approaches","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician palpates the right lower quadrant, medial to the right anterior superior iliac spine. According to the course, which structure lies there?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon, near the pelvic brim","B":"Cecum, near the ileocecal junction","C":"Descending colon, along the left flank","D":"Hepatic flexure, under the right ribs","E":"Splenic flexure, under the left ribs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course places the cecum in the right lower quadrant medial to the right ASIS. The sigmoid colon lies in the left lower quadrant medial to the left ASIS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecum is the first part of the large intestine and sits low on the right.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — hand placement along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, which colon segments lie retroperitoneally?","choices":{"A":"Ascending and descending colon","B":"Transverse and sigmoid colon","C":"Cecum and transverse colon","D":"Sigmoid colon and splenic flexure","E":"Hepatic flexure and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that the ascending colon sits retroperitoneally along the right flank to the hepatic flexure, and the descending colon runs retroperitoneally along the left flank to the left pelvic brim.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal with mesenteries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colon anatomy for hand placement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is localizing the biliary outflow tract on a patient. According to the course, where does the sphincter of Oddi project on the surface?","choices":{"A":"About 2 cm below and to the left of the umbilicus","B":"At the xiphoid process on the midline","C":"About 5 cm lateral to the right iliac spine","D":"About 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus","E":"At the right costal margin on the midclavicular line"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that the sphincter of Oddi projects approximately 2 cm superior and to the right of the umbilicus. The gallbladder lies below the liver edge at the right midnipple line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sphincter of Oddi controls bile and pancreatic secretions into the duodenum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — surface landmarks for abdominal techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician applies inhibitory pressure over the superior mesenteric ganglion. According to the course, at what level is it located?","choices":{"A":"The L1 to L2 disk or the superior border of L2","B":"The superior border of L1 at the celiac level","C":"The level of L3 where the inferior ganglion lies","D":"The T10 to T11 disk behind the lower sternum","E":"The superior border of L5 above the sacral base"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course places the celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1, the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or superior border of L2, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion at the level of L3.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lab rubric uses surface landmarks instead, with the superior mesenteric ganglion midway between the xiphoid and umbilicus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — ganglion inhibition hand placement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"According to this lecture's description of colonic stimulation, also called paraspinal inhibition, what is its goal?","choices":{"A":"Mobilize stored white cells and reticulocytes into circulation","B":"Detoxify the liver and move hepatic lymph through the portal bed","C":"Decongest the intestines and free the mesenteric lymph channels","D":"Lower sympathetic hyperactivity and restore bowel function","E":"Raise sympathetic tone to speed peristalsis and gut transit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This section of the course describes colonic stimulation, also called paraspinal inhibition, as down-regulating sympathetic hyperactivity and restoring normal bowel function.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: another section of the course describes colonic stimulation as restoring sliding and gliding of colonic segments, so read which description a stem names.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man has an active systemic infection and no splenomegaly. Which technique and goal fit his need for immune support, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Liver pump to clear portal venous congestion","B":"Mesenteric lift to free the lymphatic channels","C":"Ganglion inhibition to lower sympathetic outflow","D":"Colonic stimulation to restore bowel function","E":"Splenic pump to mobilize stored blood cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The splenic pump assists patients who need heightened immune function during systemic infection. Compression-gradient changes mobilize stored red and white blood cells from the spleen into circulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: confirm there is no splenomegaly or mononucleosis before a splenic pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — splenic pump technique","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man with acute hepatitis asks for a liver pump to help detoxification. According to the course, what is the appropriate response?","choices":{"A":"Perform it because the liver has a rich lymphatic bed","B":"Decline it because compression can injure the liver","C":"Perform it because it enhances Kupffer cell function","D":"Perform it with the fingertips for gentle contact","E":"Substitute a splenic pump on the left ribs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that compression of the rib cage or a hepatic pump is contraindicated in acute hepatitis because direct mechanical forces can cause liver parenchymal damage.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the stated goal of detoxification does not override the contraindication in active hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — hepatitis and the liver pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has chronic constipation and functional abdominal discomfort with no red flags. According to the course, which plan fits?","choices":{"A":"Withhold OMT because functional symptoms signal organic disease","B":"Visceral OMT to raise sympathetic tone and speed transit","C":"Visceral OMT to improve peristalsis and lymphatic drainage","D":"Visceral OMT to elevate cortisol and loosen adhesions","E":"Defer OMT until imaging excludes any possible cause"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists functional gastrointestinal discomfort, diarrhea, and constipation as indications for visceral OMT, which improves peristaltic coordination, resolves fascial restrictions, and optimizes lymphatic drainage.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for red flags first, then treat functional complaints with visceral OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — indications in the lab rubric","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has severe abdominal pain, distension, and a complete absence of bowel sounds. According to the course, which interpretation and decision fit?","choices":{"A":"Functional constipation that is suitable for direct abdominal OMT","B":"Obstruction suggested by high-pitched tinkling sounds","C":"Normal bowel function with sympathetic hyperactivity","D":"Diarrheal illness that is suitable for colonic stimulation","E":"Paralytic ileus that needs immediate medical evaluation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that a complete absence of bowel sounds indicates a paralytic ileus, while high-pitched tinkling sounds indicate an active obstruction. Both contraindicate direct abdominal manipulation and require immediate medical or surgical evaluation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: absent bowel sounds with pain and distension is a surgical or medical emergency, not a visceral OMT case.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — surgical abdomen and red flags","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Visceral Techniques Lab","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 4 lab rubric, which finding is an indication for visceral manipulation?","choices":{"A":"A suspected abdominal aortic aneurysm","B":"A potential internal bleed","C":"Rebound tenderness with rigidity","D":"An acute abdomen from appendicitis","E":"A viscerosomatic reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric lists a known medical diagnosis with a functional visceral component, and a viscerosomatic reflex, as indications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: viscerosomatic reflexes link visceral dysfunction to segmentally related somatic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 4 lab rubric, which condition is a contraindication to visceral manipulation?","choices":{"A":"Irritable bowel syndrome","B":"Chronic constipation","C":"Nausea with vomiting","D":"Infectious mononucleosis","E":"Functional abdominal pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric lists infectious mononucleosis as a contraindication to visceral manipulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mononucleosis can enlarge the spleen and raise the risk of rupture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 4 lab rubric, how is severe pain with palpation of the abdomen classified?","choices":{"A":"An indication for deeper pressure","B":"A contraindication to treatment","C":"A sign of a viscerosomatic reflex to treat","D":"A reason to switch to the splenic pump","E":"A normal finding in a tense patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists severe pain with palpation as a contraindication to visceral manipulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe tenderness may signal an acute abdomen, which needs medical evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which patient and physician positions are used for the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Patient supine, physician seated to the side","B":"Patient prone, physician standing at the head","C":"Patient seated, physician standing behind","D":"Patient side-lying, physician kneeling at the foot","E":"Patient supine, physician standing at the head"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric places the patient supine with the physician seated to the side of the patient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the mesenteric lift is performed from the side with the patient supine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed for the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Over the iliac crests on both sides","B":"On the pubic symphysis at the pelvic brim","C":"Over the anterior superior iliac spines","D":"On the lateral border of the mesentery","E":"On the lumbar paraspinal muscles bilaterally"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the lateral border of the mesentery, with the patient supine and the physician seated to the side.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hand placement differs for each visceral technique, so learn it with the technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how do the fingers move during the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Push gently toward the diaphragm, then deepen with each exhalation","B":"Push toward the back, then draw to the midline and right upper quadrant","C":"Push gently toward the pelvis, then draw toward the left lower quadrant","D":"Compress the lower ribs, then release at the end of exhalation","E":"Pull laterally toward the flank with a quick thrust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric has the fingers gently push toward the patient's back and then draw toward the midline and right upper quadrant.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's lecture text describes traction medially and cephalad toward the umbilicus, so rely on the rubric's wording for the lab.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the physician stand or sit for the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"On the patient's right side","B":"At the head of the table","C":"At the foot of the table","D":"On the patient's left side","E":"Behind the seated patient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric places the physician seated or standing on the left side of the supine patient for the splenic pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen lies in the left upper quadrant and the liver in the right upper quadrant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what is the hand position for the liver pump and the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"One hand under the lower ribs and one below the costal margin","B":"Both hands on the xiphoid process with the fingertips pressed in","C":"One hand on the sacrum and the other over the sternum","D":"Both palms over the lateral borders of the mesentery and colon","E":"Hands along the course of the descending colon and sigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For both pumps the rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other hand on the abdomen below the costal margin.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: both pumps use the same hand position.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how is the contact adjusted during the liver and splenic pumps?","choices":{"A":"Released suddenly at the end of exhalation","B":"Deepened with each inhalation until release","C":"Deepened with each exhalation until release","D":"Held at the same depth throughout the technique","E":"Thrust quickly at the end of inhalation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position until release while deepening the contact with each exhalation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lecture text describes the liver pump as compression followed by a sudden release at the end of exhalation, so rely on the rubric wording for the lab.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where are the hands placed for colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"On the lateral border of the mesentery","B":"One hand under the lower ribs","C":"Fingers at the midline near the xiphoid","D":"Fingers just above the umbilicus","E":"Along the course of the colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric places the hands along the course of the colon while the patient lies supine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal, and the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"With respiratory cooperation during colonic stimulation, toward what does the hand gently push, according to the lab rubric?","choices":{"A":"The patient's diaphragm","B":"The pelvic floor","C":"The right upper quadrant","D":"The umbilicus at the midline","E":"The lateral flank"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric has the hand gently push toward the patient's diaphragm until the restrictive barrier is met.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation uses the same diaphragm-directed push as the liver and splenic pumps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the physician position for colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Standing at the head of the table facing caudad","B":"Kneeling at the patient's feet at the table end","C":"Seated or standing at the patient's side","D":"Standing behind the seated patient at the table","E":"Seated at the head of the table facing the feet"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric positions the physician seated or standing on the side of the supine patient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: most visceral techniques in the lab are performed from the patient's side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed to treat the celiac ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","B":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","C":"Just superior to the umbilicus","D":"Just inferior to the umbilicus","E":"Over the lateral border of the mesentery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the midline near the xiphoid for the celiac ganglion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric places the ganglia by surface landmarks, while the lecture text gives vertebral levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how do the fingers move during abdominal ganglion inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Push gently toward the diaphragm, then deepen with each exhalation","B":"Push gently toward the pelvis, then draw toward the flank","C":"Push gently toward the back, then draw toward the midline","D":"Press firmly toward the spine, then release suddenly","E":"Pull toward the flank, then apply a short thrust"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric has the fingers gently push toward the patient's back and then draw toward the midline.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ganglion inhibition is a sustained inhibitory pressure, not a thrust.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed to treat the inferior mesenteric ganglion?","choices":{"A":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","B":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","C":"Just inferior to the umbilicus","D":"Over the left iliac fossa","E":"Just superior to the umbilicus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric places the hand just superior to the umbilicus for the inferior mesenteric ganglion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric places the three ganglia by surface landmarks down the midline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has diagnosed irritable bowel syndrome with chronic constipation alternating with diarrhea and no red flags. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Withhold because irritable bowel syndrome is an emergent condition","B":"Withhold because constipation signals an internal bleed","C":"Proceed because a functional visceral diagnosis is an indication","D":"Withhold because viscerosomatic reflexes contraindicate treatment","E":"Withhold because a functional diagnosis is a contraindication"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric lists a known medical diagnosis with a functional visceral component, such as constipation, diarrhea, or irritable bowel syndrome, as an indication. Emergent conditions and severe palpation pain are the contraindications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for red flags first, and then treat functional complaints with visceral OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — functional indications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has severe abdominal pain, a pulsatile midline mass, and a bruit. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Withhold OMT and arrange emergent evaluation","B":"Begin a mesenteric lift to reduce tension","C":"Begin ganglion inhibition to calm sympathetic tone","D":"Begin colonic stimulation to relieve the pain","E":"Schedule routine follow-up and treat next week"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric lists any condition requiring emergent evaluation, such as an abdominal aortic aneurysm or potential internal bleed, as a contraindication to visceral manipulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a pulsatile midline mass with a bruit and severe pain suggests an aneurysm and needs emergent referral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — aortic aneurysm as a red flag","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with abdominal discomfort winces and guards at a light touch of the abdomen. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Proceed with deeper pressure to overcome the guarding","B":"Proceed with respiratory cooperation to distract the patient","C":"Proceed with a splenic pump to relieve the pain","D":"Proceed after confirming a viscerosomatic reflex","E":"Withhold treatment because the pain is a contraindication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric lists severe pain with palpation as a contraindication to visceral manipulation. Severe tenderness may reflect an acute abdomen that needs evaluation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: do not push through severe tenderness to complete a technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — situations requiring medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"During a mesenteric lift, a student's fingers have reached the restrictive barrier. According to the lab rubric, what comes next?","choices":{"A":"Release at once and reassess the patient","B":"Hold until release and repeat as needed","C":"Add a high-velocity thrust at the barrier","D":"Deepen contact with each exhalation until release","E":"Reverse to the position of ease for 90 seconds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position at the restrictive barrier until release and repeat into a new barrier if needed, then reassess.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lifts and ganglion inhibition are held until release. Respiratory deepening belongs to the colonic and pump techniques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — mesenteric lift goals","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has bloating from a congested small bowel. According to the lab rubric, which hand placement and direction describe her mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Lateral mesentery border, drawn toward the pelvis and left lower quadrant","B":"Midline near the xiphoid, drawn toward the midline and right upper quadrant","C":"Lower ribs, pushed toward the diaphragm with each exhalation","D":"Lateral mesentery border, drawn to the midline and right upper quadrant","E":"Along the colon, pushed toward the diaphragm with each exhalation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mesenteric lift places the hand on the lateral border of the mesentery and draws the tissue toward the midline and right upper quadrant after a gentle push toward the back.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lift decongests the intestines and frees the lymphatic channels in the mesentery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — mesenteric lift goals","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is seated at the side of a supine patient with a hand on the lateral border of the mesentery. According to the lab rubric, what is the next movement?","choices":{"A":"Gently push the fingers toward the patient's back","B":"Draw the fingers toward the right upper quadrant","C":"Hold the fingers still at the restrictive barrier","D":"Deepen the contact with each exhalation of the patient","E":"Repeat the hold into a new restrictive barrier"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After positioning, the rubric has the fingers gently push toward the patient's back and then draw toward the midline and right upper quadrant until the restrictive barrier is met.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lift starts with a gentle push posteriorly before the draw.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — mesenteric lift technique","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman needs a liver pump. According to the lab rubric, where does the physician position and toward what does the hand push?","choices":{"A":"On the left, pushing toward the diaphragm","B":"On the right, pushing to the diaphragm","C":"On the right, pushing toward the pelvis","D":"At the head, pushing toward the feet","E":"On the left, pushing toward the midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric places the physician on the right side of the supine patient for the liver pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver lies in the right upper quadrant under the right ribs 7 to 10.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — liver surface anatomy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-year-old man has fever, exudative pharyngitis, and a positive Monospot test, and a student plans a splenic pump. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Perform it gently since fever raises immune demand","B":"Delay it until the fever resolves and then perform it","C":"Perform it from the patient's right side this time","D":"Withhold it because mononucleosis bars the pump","E":"Perform it timed to the patient's deep inhalation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric lists infectious mononucleosis as a contraindication to visceral manipulation. The lecture text adds that a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated because of the danger of splenic rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mononucleosis causes splenomegaly, and the enlarged spleen is fragile.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — mononucleosis and splenic rupture","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is performing a liver pump. Which sequence matches the Week 4 lab rubric?","choices":{"A":"Supine patient, physician on the left, push toward the diaphragm, deepen with each exhalation","B":"Supine patient, physician on the right, push toward the pelvis, deepen with each inhalation","C":"Supine patient, physician on the right, push toward the diaphragm, deepen with each exhalation","D":"Prone patient, physician on the right, push toward the diaphragm, release suddenly after exhalation","E":"Supine patient, physician at the head, push toward the pelvis, deepen with each exhalation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric positions the supine patient with the physician on the right, one hand under the lower ribs and one below the costal margin. The hand pushes toward the diaphragm with respiratory cooperation, holds until release, and deepens with each exhalation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: left side for the spleen, right side for the liver, and both push toward the diaphragm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — liver pump goals","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"During colonic stimulation the hand has reached the restrictive barrier. According to the lab rubric, what does the physician do?","choices":{"A":"Hold until release, deepening with each inhalation","B":"Add a rapid thrust at the restrictive barrier","C":"Release at once and ask the patient to inhale","D":"Reverse to the position of ease and hold there","E":"Hold until release, deepening with each exhalation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position until release while deepening the contact with each exhalation, then reassess.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric ties the deepening to exhalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation goal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea with a viscerosomatic reflex and no red flags. Which colonic stimulation set-up matches the Week 4 lab rubric?","choices":{"A":"Prone, hands along the colon, push toward the diaphragm with respiration","B":"Supine, hands along the colon, thrust toward the pelvis at end-exhalation","C":"Supine, hands on the xiphoid, push toward the diaphragm with respiration","D":"Supine, hands along the colon, push toward the diaphragm with respiration","E":"Patient seated, hands along the colon, push toward the pelvis with respiration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric places the patient supine with the physician seated or standing at the side and the hands along the course of the colon. With respiratory cooperation the hand pushes gently toward the diaphragm until the restrictive barrier is met.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: functional diarrhea and viscerosomatic reflexes are indications for visceral OMT after red flags are excluded.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — functional bowel indications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course's lecture text, in which directions are individual colonic segments mobilized during colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Medial and lateral, without rotational motion","B":"Superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational","C":"Superior and inferior, against a fixed anterior lock","D":"Rotational and torsional, around a fixed fulcrum","E":"Anterior and posterior, with a sudden release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes motion-testing and mobilizing individual segments of the large intestine in superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational directions to restore sliding, gliding, and distensibility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic work is multidirectional and segment specific, from the cecum to the rectum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — colonic stimulation goal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student treats the superior mesenteric ganglion. According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed?","choices":{"A":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","B":"Just superior to the umbilicus at the midline","C":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","D":"Just inferior to the umbilicus at the midline","E":"Over the right costal margin near the nipple line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the midline near the xiphoid for the celiac ganglion, midway between the xiphoid and umbilicus for the superior mesenteric ganglion, and just superior to the umbilicus for the inferior mesenteric ganglion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lecture text gives vertebral levels instead, with the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or the superior border of L2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — ganglion inhibition goal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician wants to lower hypersympathetic outflow to the pelvic viscera and treats the inferior mesenteric ganglion. According to the course, at which vertebra does it lie?","choices":{"A":"The level of L3","B":"The superior border of L1","C":"The L1 to L2 disk","D":"The T10 to T11 disk","E":"The superior border of S2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course places the celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1, the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or superior border of L2, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion at the level of L3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the inferior mesenteric ganglion supplies sympathetic fibers to the hindgut and pelvic viscera.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — ganglion inhibition levels","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman recovering from surgery has sluggish bowel function. Which sequence matches the lab rubric for abdominal ganglion inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Supine, push toward the diaphragm, draw toward the midline, hold until release, reassess","B":"Supine, push toward the back, draw toward the flank, hold until release, reassess","C":"Prone, push toward the back, draw toward the midline, hold until release, reassess","D":"Supine, push toward the back, draw toward the midline, thrust at the barrier, reassess","E":"Supine, push toward the back, draw toward the midline, hold until release, reassess"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric has the patient supine with the physician seated or standing at the side. The fingers gently push toward the back and draw toward the midline until the restrictive barrier, hold until release, repeat into a new barrier if needed, and reassess.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ganglion inhibition is sustained pressure until release, not a thrust.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ganglion inhibition technique","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Neuroendocrine/Immune I","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, preganglionic sympathetic fibers exit the spinal cord at which levels?","choices":{"A":"C1 to T4","B":"T5 to L5","C":"T1 to L2","D":"S2 to S4","E":"C3 to C5"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The sympathetic division is thoracolumbar. Preganglionic fibers leave the spinal cord through the ventral roots at T1 to L2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic outflow is thoracolumbar and the parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — viscerosomatic reflex arcs and autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where are the parasympathetic ganglia located?","choices":{"A":"Within the paravertebral sympathetic trunk","B":"Close to or within the target organs","C":"Beside the spinal cord at each segment","D":"Inside the dorsal root ganglia","E":"Along the aorta in the prevertebral plexuses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that the parasympathetic ganglia are close to or within the target organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: paravertebral sympathetic ganglia lie beside the cord, whereas parasympathetic ganglia lie near or in the organ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — viscerosomatic reflex arcs and autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which body region has no parasympathetic innervation?","choices":{"A":"The arms and legs","B":"The stomach and pancreas","C":"The heart and lungs","D":"The bladder and rectum","E":"The liver and spleen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course notes that there is no parasympathetic innervation of the extremities.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: sympathetic fibers reach the limbs through gray rami to the spinal nerves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — viscerosomatic reflex arcs and autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which gastrointestinal changes result from sustained sympathetic tone?","choices":{"A":"Increased motility, relaxed sphincters, and increased gland secretions","B":"Reduced motility, contracted sphincters, and visceral vasoconstriction","C":"Reduced motility, relaxed sphincters, and marked splanchnic vasodilation","D":"Increased motility, contracted sphincters, and decreased gland secretions","E":"Reduced motility, increased gland secretions, and splanchnic vasodilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic sympathetic hyperactivity inhibits smooth muscle peristalsis and motility, contracts sphincters, decreases digestive gland secretions, and induces splanchnic vasoconstriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic tone slows the gut and tightens the sphincters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences in cardiovascular disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which respiratory changes result from sustained sympathetic tone?","choices":{"A":"Bronchoconstriction with increased mucus volume and thinner secretions","B":"Bronchodilation with increased mucus volume and thinner secretions","C":"Bronchoconstriction with decreased mucus volume and thicker secretions","D":"Bronchodilation with increased mucus volume and thicker secretions","E":"Bronchodilation with decreased mucus volume but thicker secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathicotonia causes bronchodilation and decreases overall mucus volume, but it increases the viscosity of respiratory secretions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta-2 stimulation dilates the airways.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences in cardiovascular disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does sustained sympathetic tone affect urinary function?","choices":{"A":"It predisposes to retention from trigone contraction","B":"It causes urgency from detrusor contraction","C":"It causes diuresis from lower sodium reabsorption","D":"It causes incontinence from a relaxed trigone","E":"It lowers renal vascular resistance and raises urine output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sympathetic tone raises renal vascular resistance, enhances tubular sodium and water reabsorption, contracts the bladder trigone through alpha receptors, and relaxes the detrusor through beta receptors, which predisposes to urinary retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha stimulation tightens the trigone and beta stimulation relaxes the detrusor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences in cardiovascular disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what pathway does a viscerosomatic reflex follow?","choices":{"A":"Soma to spinal cord to viscus","B":"Viscus A to ganglia to viscus B","C":"Viscus to spinal cord to soma","D":"Soma to ganglia to soma","E":"Viscus to brain to viscus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A viscerosomatic reflex runs from the viscus through the spinal cord to the soma.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: name the reflex by its direction, with the origin first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the primary trigger of a somatovisceral reflex?","choices":{"A":"Organ inflammation, ischemia, or infarction","B":"Capsular stretch, organ distension, or spasm","C":"Acute infection in a distant organ","D":"Vertebral, rib, or myofascial strain","E":"Chronic hypothalamic activation, cortisol excess, or stress"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The primary trigger of a somatovisceral reflex is somatic dysfunction, such as vertebral, rib, or myofascial strain. Inflammation, ischemia, capsular stretch, and distension trigger viscerosomatic reflexes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: for a somatovisceral reflex, look first at the spine, ribs, and myofascial tissue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is a viscerovisceral reflex?","choices":{"A":"A somatic restriction that changes the function of an organ","B":"Irritation of one organ that alters another organ's function","C":"A skin reflex confined to one dermatome of the body wall","D":"A reflex that connects two skeletal muscle groups together","E":"Spinal facilitation that lowers cord thresholds at one level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A viscerovisceral reflex is irritation or expansion in one organ altering the function of another organ, such as changes in blood flow, vital signs, or smooth muscle tone in a distant organ.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in the course, viscerovisceral reflexes are managed by treating both organs involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is allostatic load?","choices":{"A":"The acute rise in cortisol after one stressor","B":"The baseline level of sympathetic tone at rest","C":"Cumulative wear and tear of chronic stress","D":"The set point of the hypothalamic thermostat","E":"The rate of hormone clearance by the liver and kidney"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allostatic load is the cumulative wear and tear of chronic stress. Sustained cortisol elevation increases it, which impairs healing, suppresses immune function, and disrupts metabolic homeostasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic HPA activation raises cortisol and allostatic load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — autonomic and HPA axis effects of visceral manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which finding accompanies occult hypothyroidism in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes","B":"Heat intolerance with weight loss","C":"Tremor with a rapid pulse","D":"Exophthalmos with lid lag","E":"Hypoactive deep tendon reflexes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When thyroid hormone falls, patients can have systemic symptoms such as hypoactive deep tendon reflexes, fatigue, weight changes, and cognitive slowing. Occult hypothyroidism becomes more common with age.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypothyroidism slows reflexes and metabolism, and hyperthyroidism does the opposite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — autonomic and HPA axis effects of visceral manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what follows the senescent decrease in estrogen production?","choices":{"A":"Bone density loss and accelerated coronary disease","B":"Bone density gain and slowed coronary artery disease","C":"Muscle mass gain and lower parathyroid hormone output","D":"Faster glucose uptake and lower insulin resistance","E":"Higher cortisol output and thicker bone cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Declining estrogen at menopause alters structural homeostasis, predisposing women to rapid bone mineral density loss, which is osteoporosis, and accelerating coronary artery disease.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: estrogen loss affects both bone and vascular homeostasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — autonomic and HPA axis effects of visceral manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is released by tissue injury and immune cells as an inflammatory neuropeptide?","choices":{"A":"Thyroid hormone","B":"Pancreatic glucagon","C":"Aldosterone","D":"Substance P","E":"Growth hormone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tissue injury and somatic tension trigger release of inflammatory neuropeptides and cytokines, including interleukins, substance P, calcitonin gene-related peptide, histamine, and prostaglandins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: substance P and CGRP sensitize nociceptive endings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the splenic pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what happens to lymphatic channels in areas of increased mechanical tension?","choices":{"A":"They dilate, accelerating immune surveillance and drainage","B":"They are compressed, which obstructs immune surveillance","C":"They are unaffected because lymph flow is pressure independent","D":"They contract faster, which increases antigen clearance","E":"They fuse with venous channels, which bypasses the nodes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In areas of increased mechanical tension the low-pressure lymphatic channels become compressed, which obstructs normal immune surveillance and leads to localized fluid congestion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: lymphatic techniques such as the splenic pump aim to restore clearance of cells, antigens, and debris.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the splenic pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what has mobilizing the spleen by compression-gradient changes been shown to do?","choices":{"A":"Reduce the phagocytic capacity of circulating blood cells","B":"Raise platelet and eosinophil counts in peripheral blood","C":"Lower reticulocyte counts with unchanged leukocytes","D":"Raise hematocrit while reducing circulating leukocytes","E":"Increase circulating leukocytes and reticulocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mobilizing the spleen has been shown to increase circulating leukocytes, reticulocytes, and the phagocytic and bacteriolytic capacity of the blood, helping the body fight systemic infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen stores erythrocytes and leukocytes and filters damaged blood cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the splenic pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how do the nervous, endocrine, and immune systems operate?","choices":{"A":"As separate compartments that use distinct signals","B":"As a hierarchy in which the endocrine system governs the others","C":"As a chain in which the immune system drives the others","D":"As one integrated network linked by shared signals","E":"As parallel systems that meet at the thalamus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The nervous, endocrine, and immune systems function as a single integrated neuro-endocrine-immune network linked by shared signaling molecules and reflexive feedback loops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cytokines, neuropeptides, and hormones act across all three systems.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — circulation, lymph flow, and autonomic function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tracts carry nociceptive signals from restricted somatic tissue to the hypothalamus and pituitary, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Corticospinal, rubrospinal, and vestibulospinal tracts","B":"Dorsal column, spinocerebellar, and corticobulbar tracts","C":"Lateral corticospinal, tectospinal, and reticulospinal tracts","D":"Gracile, cuneate, and medial lemniscal tracts","E":"Spinothalamic, spinoreticular, and spinohypothalamic tracts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nociceptive signals ascend the spinal cord through the spinothalamic, spinoreticular, and spinohypothalamic tracts to higher centers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spinothalamic fibers carry pain and temperature from the body to the thalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — circulation, lymph flow, and autonomic function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":1,"stem":"What does Korr's concept of a neurologic lens describe, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"A facilitated segment that magnifies afferent input","B":"A relaxed segment that dampens incoming afferent input","C":"A reflex arc that blocks incoming nociceptive signals","D":"A hormone gradient that focuses cortisol on target tissues","E":"A fascial layer that concentrates lymph flow to the nodes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Korr conceptualized the facilitated spinal segment as a neurologic lens that focuses and amplifies baseline afferent impulses into muscle spasm, joint restriction, and sympathetic overactivity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: once facilitated, minimal input produces an exaggerated efferent response.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — circulation, lymph flow, and autonomic function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how are Chapman's reflex points described?","choices":{"A":"Taut muscle bands that refer pain into a distant region","B":"Positions of comfort found by passive shortening of tissue","C":"Joint capsule restrictions that limit motion at the facets","D":"Tender nodules reflecting visceral or lymphatic stress","E":"Tendon insertion sites that become inflamed with overuse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chapman and Owens described reflex points as localized, tender, nodular soft-tissue masses that act as visceral-autonomic-lymphatic reflections.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman points reflect visceral disease, while trigger points are myofascial.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where does a left pectoralis major trigger point refer pain?","choices":{"A":"Into the jaw and neck","B":"Into the abdomen and groin","C":"Into the chest and arm","D":"Into the back and buttock","E":"Into the hand and wrist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes a left pectoralis major myofascial trigger point that refers pain into the chest and left arm, creating a motor-sensory feedback loop to the heart.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: trigger points refer pain in a predictable pattern away from the tender spot.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the thoracic lab rubric, how is a counterstrain tender point treated?","choices":{"A":"Positioned until tenderness falls 70 percent or more, then held 90 seconds","B":"Thrust at the barrier, then rechecked after the end of exhalation","C":"Contracted against counterforce, then relaxed after 3 to 5 seconds and retested","D":"Pressed with sustained inhibition, then released after 90 seconds of contact","E":"Stretched along the fibers, then held until the tissue releases fully"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric has the physician set a 10 out of 10 pain scale, fine-tune the position until tenderness falls about 70 percent or more, hold for about 90 seconds while monitoring the point, and return slowly to neutral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: counterstrain treats a tender point by positioning it toward ease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A student says the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems act as strict opposites. According to the course, how are they better described?","choices":{"A":"Strict antagonists, switched on one at a time","B":"Independent systems, each supplying separate organs","C":"A hierarchy, in which the parasympathetic governs the sympathetic","D":"Redundant systems, in which one replaces the other during stress","E":"Cooperative, complementary partners that maintain homeostasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that the two divisions operate as cooperative, complementary, and synergistic partners rather than in a strictly antagonistic fashion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: homeostasis depends on balance between the two divisions, not on one switching off the other.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — balance between sympathetic and parasympathetic influence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A preganglionic sympathetic fiber bound for the body wall leaves the ventral root. According to the course, which sequence follows?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root to a trunk ganglion synapse, then gray ramus to the spinal nerve","B":"White ramus to a dorsal root ganglion synapse, then gray ramus to the spinal nerve","C":"Dorsal ramus to a collateral ganglion synapse, then white ramus to the viscera","D":"White ramus to a trunk ganglion synapse, then gray ramus to the spinal nerve","E":"Ventral ramus to a terminal ganglion synapse, then gray ramus to the viscera"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers pass through the white rami communicantes into the sympathetic trunk ganglia, synapse, and then return to the spinal nerves through the gray rami communicantes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: white rami carry preganglionic fibers and gray rami carry postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — sympathetic outflow and spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review and understand the physiology and organization of the autonomic system.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, to which organs does the vagus nerve provide parasympathetic outflow?","choices":{"A":"Bladder, rectum, and the external genitalia","B":"Heart and lungs, with no abdominal organs","C":"Heart, lungs, stomach, and most of the gut","D":"Distal transverse colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum","E":"Arms, legs, and the skeletal muscle vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The vagus nerve supplies extensive parasympathetic outflow to the heart, lungs, trachea, liver, gallbladder, esophagus, stomach, pancreas, spleen, kidneys, and the majority of the digestive tract. The sacral segments supply the pelvic organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus reaches most of the gut, and S2 to S4 take over at the distal colon.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — vagal and sacral parasympathetic mapping","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has sustained sympathicotonia. According to the course, which cardiovascular combination is expected?","choices":{"A":"Tachycardia, faster nodal conduction, and arteriolar vasoconstriction","B":"Bradycardia, slower nodal conduction, and arteriolar vasodilation","C":"Tachycardia, slower nodal conduction, and arteriolar vasodilation","D":"Bradycardia, faster nodal conduction, and arteriolar vasoconstriction","E":"Tachycardia, faster nodal conduction, and arteriolar vasodilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sustained sympathicotonia increases heart rate, accelerates SA and AV nodal conduction, increases contractility, and causes systemic arteriolar vasoconstriction, which raises blood pressure, total peripheral resistance, and afterload.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic tone raises heart rate, contractility, and vascular resistance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — sympathetic output and afterload","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has constipation, urinary retention, and increased sweating. According to the course, which autonomic state explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Sustained vagal hyperactivity","B":"Sustained sympathetic hyperactivity","C":"Excess sacral parasympathetic output","D":"Withdrawal of adrenal catecholamines","E":"Loss of segmental sudomotor tone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic sympathetic hyperactivity inhibits peristalsis, predisposes to urinary retention through trigone contraction and detrusor relaxation, and drives sudomotor and pilomotor activity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: constipation, urinary retention, and sweating each fit sympathetic hyperactivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — autonomic effects of spinal facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman under chronic stress has sustained sympathetic tone. According to the course, which adrenal response accompanies it?","choices":{"A":"Medullary release of acetylcholine and histamine","B":"Cortical suppression of cortisol with a lower metabolic rate","C":"Reduced catecholamine output with a lower basal metabolic rate","D":"Medullary release of epinephrine and norepinephrine","E":"Cortical release of insulin and glucagon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sympathetic activity stimulates adrenal medullary release of catecholamines, epinephrine and norepinephrine, and increases the basal metabolic rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adrenal medulla releases catecholamines under sympathetic control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — sympathetic effects on the adrenal medulla and metabolism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with a negative cardiac evaluation has a second rib dysfunction thought to be causing recurrent arrhythmia. According to the course, which reflex type and treatment fit?","choices":{"A":"Somatovisceral, treat the somatic source","B":"Viscerosomatic, treat the underlying heart disease","C":"Viscerovisceral, treat both organs","D":"Viscerosomatic, treat the paraspinal spasm","E":"Somatovisceral, treat the viscus and leave the rib"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course gives second rib dysfunction causing cardiac arrhythmia as an example of a somatovisceral reflex. The treatment focus is the primary responsible somatic dysfunction, and OMT resolves the visceral response.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: when the soma drives the organ, treat the soma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — somatovisceral and viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with an acute myocardial infarction has left paraspinal heat, muscle splinting, and rib involvement over several segments. According to the course, which reflex is this?","choices":{"A":"Somatovisceral reflex","B":"Viscerosomatic reflex","C":"Viscerovisceral reflex","D":"Somatosomatic reflex","E":"Axon reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multisegmental paraspinal TART changes, muscle splinting, heat, and rib involvement from organ ischemia are the clinical presentation of a viscerosomatic reflex.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a viscerosomatic reflex involves two to five paraspinal levels with heat, moisture, and splinting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — viscerosomatic reflex presentation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Utilize autonomic reflexes to describe the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with cholecystitis has right T7 to T9 paraspinal tissue texture changes. According to the course, which treatment focus fits?","choices":{"A":"Treat the somatic dysfunction first because it caused the disease","B":"Replace medical treatment of the organ with paraspinal OMT","C":"Withhold OMT because the paraspinal tissue is a bystander","D":"Treat the visceral pathology and use OMT to break the loop","E":"Treat the paraspinal tissue and leave the gallbladder untreated"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For a viscerosomatic reflex the course advises treating the underlying primary visceral pathology, with OMT to the soma used to break the positive feedback loop and relieve symptoms.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OMT supports medical treatment of the organ, and does not replace it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — treatment plan for viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with chronic muscle restriction has a continuous stream of nociceptive input. According to the course, what is the neuroendocrine response?","choices":{"A":"Input descends from the cortex to the adrenal medulla, lowering cortisol","B":"Input stays at the dorsal horn and does not engage the hypothalamus","C":"Input reaches the hypothalamus, raising cortisol and sympathetic tone","D":"Input reaches the thyroid directly, raising TSH and lowering sympathetic tone","E":"Input reaches the posterior pituitary, lowering cortisol and raising vagal tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nociceptive signals ascend to the hypothalamus and pituitary, which stimulate the adrenal glands to increase cortisol production while sympathetic activity rises.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic HPA activation raises cortisol and suppresses adaptive immunity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — HPA axis and visceral manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has progressive loss of muscle strength and mass. According to the course, which endocrine combination is a key determinant?","choices":{"A":"Rising estrogen together with high vitamin D","B":"Falling parathyroid hormone together with high calcium","C":"Rising insulin sensitivity together with low cortisol","D":"Falling cortisol together with high growth hormone","E":"Rising parathyroid hormone together with low vitamin D"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that age-related increases in parathyroid hormone, particularly with low vitamin D, are key determinants of sarcopenia, the progressive loss of skeletal muscle strength and mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin D deficiency raises PTH and affects both bone and muscle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the endocrine system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old with chronic anxiety and somatic stress is examined over the kidneys. According to the course, what is expected of the adrenal region?","choices":{"A":"A palpably softer region with increased mobility","B":"A normal region that moves independently of the diaphragm","C":"A tighter region that moves more freely with breathing","D":"A palpably tighter region with reduced mobility","E":"A region that is warm with a rapid pulsatile mass"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The adrenal glands lie inside the perirenal fascia superior to the kidneys and move passively with the respiratory diaphragm. In chronic anxiety or somatic stress the region can become palpably tighter, with loss of normal tissue distensibility and mobility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: adrenal region tightness is an osteopathic palpatory finding of chronic stress.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — visceral tissue findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman develops reactivation of a latent virus. According to the course, which age-related immune change contributes?","choices":{"A":"Rising primary antibody output from B cells","B":"Declining CD8 and T-cell competence","C":"Expanded regulatory control of viral infections","D":"Expanded thymic output of naive T cells","E":"Enhanced antibody response to vaccines"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CD8 T-cell function and general T-cell competence decline significantly with age, leaving older adults more vulnerable to infections, malignancies, and the reactivation of latent pathogens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cell-mediated immunity wanes with age.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — immunosenescence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man has a poor response to influenza vaccine. According to the course, why?","choices":{"A":"Primary and secondary antibody responses decline","B":"Primary responses are preserved but secondary ones rise","C":"Mucosal IgA output rises and blunts vaccine uptake","D":"Complement activity rises and clears the vaccine antigen quickly","E":"Naive B-cell numbers expand and dilute the response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"B-cell and antibody responses, both primary and secondary, decrease with age, which reduces vaccine effectiveness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vaccine responses fall with immune aging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — immunosenescence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the osteopathic impacts of the immune system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man has a chronic, poorly regulated inflammatory state and an autoimmune disease. According to the course, which age-related change predisposes to this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced production of autoantibodies by B cells","B":"Stronger regulatory control of inflammation","C":"Lower output of inflammatory cytokines and chemokines","D":"Expanded output of naive B cells from marrow","E":"Increased production of autoantibodies"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The production of autoantibodies increases with age, predisposing older adults to autoimmune disease and to chronic, poorly regulated inflammatory states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune aging brings weaker defense together with a tendency toward autoimmunity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — immunosenescence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has paraspinal spasm, and OMT is planned. According to the course, how does reducing it improve glandular function?","choices":{"A":"It raises sympathetic outflow, increasing glandular blood flow","B":"It frees vessels and nerves from compression, restoring delivery","C":"It increases nociceptive input, stimulating the glands directly","D":"It narrows the foramina, concentrating arterial flow to the glands","E":"It raises cortisol, enhancing nutrient delivery to the glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vessels and nerves supplying glands pass through muscular planes and narrow foramina. Somatic dysfunction compresses them and causes ischemia, and resolving the restriction restores arterial delivery and trophic function.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course links reduced paraspinal spasm to better neurovascular supply of glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — circulation effects of manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, why are veins and lymphatics the first structures compressed when fascial tension rises?","choices":{"A":"They lie deepest in the tissue, so tension reaches them last","B":"They are rigid walled, so tension has little effect on them","C":"They have the lowest pressures, so they yield first","D":"They have the highest pressures, so they resist compression","E":"They are densely innervated, so tension is sensed first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Veins and lymphatic vessels have the lowest fluid pressures in the circulatory loop, so they are the first structures to become compressed. This obstructs lymphatic drainage and causes interstitial fluid accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low-pressure vessels collapse first under external compression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — lymphatic flow and visceral manipulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe how the neuro, endocrine and immune systems affect homeostasis throughout the body and through a system of modulation and regulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician treats facilitated thoracic segments in a patient with sympathetic overactivity. According to the course, how does this restore autonomic balance?","choices":{"A":"It lowers nociceptive input, down-regulating the HPA axis","B":"It raises nociceptive input, resetting spinal thresholds","C":"It increases cortisol, lowering sympathetic tone directly","D":"It abolishes parasympathetic outflow, rebalancing the system","E":"It amplifies facilitation, restoring homeostasis in the segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treating facilitated segments reduces the nociceptive and proprioceptive barrage to the cord, decreases peripheral sensitization and facilitation, and down-regulates HPA axis activation, which lowers cortisol and sympathetic tone.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reducing somatic dysfunction lowers allostatic load.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — autonomic balance through OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":2,"stem":"A tender, pea-sized, gangliform fascial contraction is found in the second intercostal space near the sternum. According to the course, what is it?","choices":{"A":"A posterior Chapman point at a transverse process","B":"A palpable lymph node along the sternal border","C":"A second rib somatic dysfunction at the costotransverse joint","D":"A superior mesenteric ganglion surface landmark","E":"The anterior Chapman's point for the heart"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course places the anterior Chapman's reflex point for the heart in the second intercostal space near the sternum, where palpation finds an exquisitely tender, pea-sized, gangliform fascial contraction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman points reflect visceral stress, and trigger points refer pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — anatomical indicators of cardiac stress","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":2,"stem":"A tender spot below the right fifth rib, midway between the sternal margin and the nipple line, correlates with sinus tachycardia. According to the course, what is it?","choices":{"A":"A myofascial trigger point","B":"An anterior Chapman's reflex point","C":"A counterstrain tender point","D":"A somatic dysfunction of the fifth rib","E":"A sympathetic ganglion landmark"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a right fifth intercostal space myofascial trigger point, below the right fifth rib midway between the sternal margin and nipple line, that correlates with sinus tachycardia and atrial arrhythmias.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: trigger points are tender spots that can correlate with rhythm disturbances.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — myofascial trigger points in cardiac disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Differentiate between Chapman Points and other types of tenderpoints such as Trigger Points, Counterstrain points, etc.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with gastrointestinal disease has gangliform, edematous fascial congestion. According to the course, where are these points typically found?","choices":{"A":"In the deltoid, biceps, and forearm flexors","B":"Along the patella, ankle, and dorsum of the foot","C":"Around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus","D":"On the scalp, forehead, and face","E":"At the occiput, upper cervical spine, and jaw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that Chapman's points, localized gangliform and edematous fascial congestion, are typically found around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus in patients with gastrointestinal or pelvic disease.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: counterstrain tender points are named by spinal level, whereas Chapman points are tied to the organ.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Neuroendocrine/Immune II","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what did John Hilton note in 1863?","choices":{"A":"Tender points tracked visceral pain by segment","B":"Nodular soft tissue masses reflected visceral disease","C":"A displaced vertebra irritated a visceral organ","D":"Sweat gland activity mapped the organs","E":"Deep paravertebral stimulation altered organ color"},"correct":"A","explanation":"John Hilton in 1863 noted sympathies in which segmentally related tender points correlated with visceral pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this history underlies using somatic palpatory findings to aid visceral differential diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — history and research significance of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what did Myron Beal's palpatory studies in cardiovascular and pulmonary patients demonstrate?","choices":{"A":"Muscle tension appeared after organ surgery","B":"Palpation could not separate sick patients from healthy ones","C":"Pulmonary patients showed segmental findings but cardiac patients did not","D":"Visceral disease produced segmentally related paraspinal changes","E":"Findings were random with no link to the organ's level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Myron Beal validated palpatory testing for somatic dysfunction in patients with cardiovascular and pulmonary diseases and showed that visceral disease consistently produces segmentally related paraspinal tissue changes, matching the earlier animal findings of Burns.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: palpable somatic signs of organ disease are the empirical basis for using palpation in visceral diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — history and research significance of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which stimuli activate the visceral afferents that start a viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Joint motion, muscle stretch, vibration, or deep pressure","B":"Inflammation, ischemia, capsular stretch, or distension","C":"Hormone surges, cortisol output, glucose rise, or insulin","D":"Skin heat, cold, light touch, or pinprick pain","E":"Postural strain, rib motion, fascial tension, or muscle spasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chemoreceptors, mechanoreceptors, and thermosensitive visceral afferents are stimulated by inflammation, ischemia, organ capsule stretch, or distension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the afferent limb of this reflex begins in the organ, not in the body wall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and segmental facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 Chapman chart, which organs are the BETH points?","choices":{"A":"Bronchials, esophagus, tongue, and heart","B":"Bronchials, ear, thyroid, and heart","C":"Bronchials, esophagus, thyroid, and stomach","D":"Bronchials, esophagus, larynx, and heart","E":"Bronchials, esophagus, thyroid, and heart"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Week 5 chart marks the bronchial, esophagus, thyroid, and heart rows with the BETH label. All four share an anterior point in the second intercostal space close to the sternum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the four BETH points cluster in the upper thoracic region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 5 — diagnosing visceral pathology with Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 Chapman chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Right fifth intercostal space along the anterior axillary line","B":"Left fifth intercostal space between mid-mammary line and sternum","C":"Third intercostal space close to the sternum on the left side","D":"Left eighth intercostal space near the tip of the xiphoid process","E":"Fourth intercostal space close to the sternum on the right side"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart places the stomach point in the left fifth intercostal space on a line between the mid-mammary line and the sternum, with posterior points at the T5 and T6 intertransverse spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the course maps the stomach to T5 through T9 through the celiac ganglion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 5 — diagnosing visceral pathology with Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what kind of finding is a Chapman's point?","choices":{"A":"A somatovisceral trigger created by a rib restriction","B":"A taut muscle band that refers pain into a limb","C":"A viscerosomatic sign of gangliform fascia","D":"A joint capsule change caused by facet restriction","E":"A skin reflex confined to one dermatome of the arm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists Chapman's points among the findings of viscerosomatic reflexes: localized gangliform and edematous fascial congestion that reflects underlying visceral disease.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman points are reflex findings tied to a specific organ.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 5 — diagnosing visceral pathology with Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which spinal segments represent the cardiac ventricles?","choices":{"A":"T4 to T5","B":"T6 to T9","C":"T10 to T11","D":"T12 to L2","E":"T1 to T3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course gives ventricular representation at T1 to T3 and atrial representation at T4 to T5, with the heaviest cardiac nociceptive concentration at T4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac sympathetic input arises from the upper thoracic cord.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflex mapping","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which parts of the colon receive vagal parasympathetic outflow?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon and rectum together with the anal canal","C":"The entire colon from the cecum through the rectum","D":"Ascending colon and proximal transverse colon","E":"Cecum and appendix but not the transverse colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The vagus supplies the ascending colon and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sacral S2 to S4 outflow supplies the distal third of the transverse colon through the rectum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the vagus and the pelvic splanchnic nerves divide the colon near the distal transverse colon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflex mapping","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which spinal segments provide sympathetic innervation to the head and neck?","choices":{"A":"T1 to T5","B":"T6 to T9","C":"T9 to T11","D":"T10 to T11","E":"T12 to L2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Head and neck sympathetic innervation arises from T1 to T5, the same upper thoracic levels that serve the heart and lungs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: upper thoracic findings can reflect head, neck, heart, or lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflex mapping","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart note for pyloric stenosis, on which structures did Dr. Still press?","choices":{"A":"The spinous processes of L2 and L3","B":"The angle of Louis at the sternum","C":"The transverse processes of T5","D":"The anterior superior iliac spines","E":"The transverse processes of C2 and C3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart notes for pyloric stenosis that Dr. Still pressed on the transverse processes of T5. The Chapman points listed are the sternalis muscle anteriorly and the junction of the tip of T10 and the tenth rib posteriorly.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: for pyloric stenosis the chart warns that the condition may mimic a myocardial infarction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, which nerve is inhibited in a patient with epistaxis?","choices":{"A":"The suboccipital nerve","B":"The phrenic nerve of the neck","C":"The vagus nerve of the neck","D":"The long thoracic nerve","E":"The sciatic nerve of the thigh"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The chart note for epistaxis, listed with the nasal Chapman points, says to inhibit the suboccipital nerve.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the nasal Chapman point is at the first rib cartilage at the manubrium anteriorly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, where is inhibition applied for enuresis?","choices":{"A":"The greater trochanter","B":"The pubic symphysis","C":"The sacrococcygeal junction","D":"The ischial tuberosity","E":"The anterior superior iliac spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the bladder section the chart lists enuresis with the instruction to inhibit the pubic symphysis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart prints this instruction in the anterior column of the bladder row.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with ureteral colic from a kidney stone has diffuse lumbar paraspinal spasm and a rigid lumbar group curve. On passive testing the segments give a distinctive end-feel. According to the course, what is this characteristic finding of visceral-origin dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Reluctance to motion","B":"A hard articular barrier","C":"A normal free end-feel","D":"A structural scoliosis","E":"A positive straight-leg raise"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a characteristic reluctance to motion in somatic dysfunctions of visceral origin: a soft, rubbery, or hesitant end-feel rather than an absolute hard articular barrier. Passage of a kidney stone can create diffuse paraspinal spasm and rigid lumbar group curves.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reluctance to motion is the soft end-feel of visceral-origin dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — visceral tissue findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with an acute myocardial infarction has a T2 vertebral sidebending restriction to the left. According to the course, how does the T2 rib behave if this is a viscerosomatic cardiac dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"It restricts sidebending right, mirroring the vertebral segment","B":"It restricts sidebending left, the same direction as the vertebra","C":"It restricts exhalation on the left while the vertebra is free","D":"It restricts rotation right while the vertebra restricts sidebending","E":"It becomes hypermobile to compensate for the vertebral restriction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cardiac viscerosomatic dysfunction the course describes costal linkage, with the rib behaving in the same direction as the vertebra, for example sidebending restrictions to the left at C6, T2, and T6. A purely somatic dysfunction shows a mirror-image rib.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: same-direction rib and vertebral behavior suggests an organ source, and mirror-image behavior suggests a purely somatic cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — cardiac-induced versus noncardiac somatic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall and describe the concepts of segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with acute cholecystitis feels pain in the right upper back. According to the course, why does the nervous system read the visceral signal as coming from the body wall?","choices":{"A":"Visceral afferents travel in the spinal nerve to the overlying skin","B":"Alpha motor neurons relay the visceral signal to the dermatome","C":"The dorsal root ganglion fuses visceral and somatic cell bodies","D":"Visceral and somatic afferents from one segment share interneurons","E":"Sympathetic efferents carry the pain signal back to the muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral and somatic sensory fibers from the same segmental level converge on shared interneuronal pools in the dorsal horn, so the central nervous system interprets visceral nociceptive signals as arising from the segmentally related dermatomes and myotomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: convergence of visceral and somatic input in the dorsal horn underlies referred pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — dorsal horn integration of visceral input","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with recurrent cystitis has a tender point at the umbilicus. According to the Week 5 Chapman chart, where is the corresponding posterior Chapman point?","choices":{"A":"Across the superior edge of the posterior aspect of L2","B":"Between the PSIS and the spinous process of L5","C":"Across the posterior aspect of C2 and the occiput","D":"Along the fourth rib just under the scapula","E":"Between the 11th rib and the tip of the T11 transverse process"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The chart lists cystitis under the bladder, with the anterior point at the umbilicus and the posterior point across the superior edge of the posterior aspect of L2.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: most chart rows pair an anterior point with a posterior point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — organ-to-point correlation for visceral pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis and new diabetes is examined for a Chapman point. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior point?","choices":{"A":"Fifth intercostal space on the right mid-axillary line","B":"Third intercostal space on the left near the sternum","C":"Between ribs 8 and 10 from the xiphoid to the hypogastrium","D":"Right seventh intercostal space on the chest wall","E":"Sixth intercostal space between the nipple line and sternum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The chart places the pancreas point in the right seventh intercostal space anteriorly, with posterior points at the right T7 and T8 intertransverse spaces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists both pancreatitis and diabetes for this point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — organ-to-point correlation for visceral pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Integrate Chapman reflex points into osteopathic diagnosis, explaining their etiology and correlating Chapman point locations with specific visceral dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with adrenal burnout has a tender posterior Chapman point. According to the Week 5 chart, where is it and what does the chart note advise?","choices":{"A":"T12 and L1 intertransverse spaces, treating the L1 level first","B":"T8 to T10 intertransverse spaces, treating each level in turn","C":"T11 and T12 intertransverse spaces, treating both levels","D":"T5 and T6 intertransverse spaces, treating the T6 level first","E":"T10 and T11 intertransverse spaces, treating one side first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart places the adrenal points at the T11 and T12 intertransverse spaces posteriorly, with the note to treat both T11 and T12. The anterior point is about 2.5 inches above the kidney point.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists Addison disease, adrenal burnout, and post-traumatic stress disorder for this point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — organ-to-point correlation for visceral pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with esophageal dysmotility has paraspinal tissue changes from a viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which spinal segments reflect sympathetic input from the esophagus?","choices":{"A":"T2 to T6","B":"T7 to T9","C":"T9 to T11","D":"T10 to T11","E":"T12 to L2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course maps the esophagus to T2 to T6, and some listings narrow it to T4 to T6.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: esophageal and tracheobronchial reflexes cluster at the upper to mid thoracic levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — mapping sympathetic visceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with a Crohn disease flare of the small intestine has paraspinal changes. According to the course, which segments and ganglion relay sympathetic input from the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"T5 to T9 through the celiac ganglion","B":"T12 to L2 through the inferior mesenteric ganglion","C":"T10 to T11 through the celiac ganglion","D":"T9 to T11 through the inferior mesenteric ganglion","E":"T9 to T11 through the superior mesenteric ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course maps the small intestine to T9 to T11 through the superior mesenteric ganglion. The stomach, liver, gallbladder, spleen, and pancreas use the celiac ganglion, and the distal third of the transverse colon through the rectum use the inferior mesenteric ganglion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course pairs the abdominal sympathetic ranges with the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric ganglia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — mapping sympathetic visceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Map viscerosomatic reflex arcs by correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments (T1–L2, S2–S4, Vagus nerve) and explaining the mechanisms of spinal cord facilitation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has pelvic organ pain with paraspinal findings from a viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which segments and structure carry sympathetic input from the uterus and bladder?","choices":{"A":"T10 to T11 through the aorticorenal ganglion complex","B":"T12 to L2 through the inferior hypogastric plexus","C":"T5 to T9 through the celiac ganglion and plexus","D":"S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves","E":"T1 to T5 through the superior cervical ganglia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course maps the bladder, uterus, prostate, and other pelvic organs to T12 to L2 with relay through the inferior hypogastric plexus. The S2 to S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves are the parasympathetic supply.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the S2 to S4 outflow is parasympathetic and does not carry sympathetic input.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — mapping sympathetic visceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with asthma is examined using the Week 5 Chapman chart. According to the chart note, which treatment sequence is advised?","choices":{"A":"Treat the lower lung point first, then the upper lung point","B":"Treat the thyroid first, then the adrenals and the sinuses","C":"Treat the pelvis and innominates first, then the thyroid","D":"Treat the diaphragm and ribs first, then the sinuses","E":"Treat the adrenals first, then the lower lung point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart's asthma note says that, along with other ailments, the pelvis and innominates should be treated first and then the thyroid for immunity, to enhance proper balance and effectiveness of hormones.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart also lists pneumonia and tuberculosis at the lung Chapman points.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — treating visceral pathology with Chapman reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with a peptic ulcer has a tender nodule on the anterior chest wall and paraspinal tissue changes in the mid-thoracic region. According to the course, what is the treatment principle?","choices":{"A":"Treat the nodule first because the nodule caused the ulcer","B":"Treat the paraspinal tissue first and defer the ulcer until the reflex clears","C":"Withhold OMT because viscerosomatic reflexes resolve unaided","D":"Treat the T5 and T6 dysfunction and leave the ulcer untreated","E":"Treat the ulcer and use OMT to the soma to break the loop"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For a viscerosomatic reflex the course advises treating the underlying primary visceral pathology, with OMT to the soma used to break the positive feedback loop and relieve symptoms.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OMT supports medical treatment of the organ and does not replace it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the principles of treatment for Chapman Points and formulate a treatment plan to address somatic dysfunction associated with viscersomatic reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has weakness, fatigue, blood pressure changes, dyspepsia, and flatulence that the Week 5 chart groups as neurasthenia. According to the chart, where are the Chapman points?","choices":{"A":"Inner upper humerus from the surgical neck anteriorly and the TPs of C3 to C7 posteriorly","B":"Pectoralis major to rib 4 anteriorly and fourth rib below the scapula posteriorly","C":"Third and fourth ribs anteriorly and the T3 and T4 intertransverse spaces posteriorly","D":"Tip of the right twelfth rib anteriorly and the T11 intertransverse space posteriorly","E":"Second intercostal space anteriorly and the T2 and T3 intertransverse spaces posteriorly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart lists neurasthenia, which may be considered dysautonomia, with the anterior point along the pectoralis major down to rib 4 and the posterior point at the fourth rib under the scapula. The same posterior point is also associated with insomnia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart notes that this condition is like adrenal burnout and lists sleep disturbances along the pectoralis major.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — organ-to-point correlation for visceral pathology","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Chapman's Reflex Points","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, in which decade did Frank Chapman and Charles Owens introduce Chapman's reflex points?","choices":{"A":"The 1930s","B":"The 1840s","C":"The 1890s","D":"The 1960s","E":"The 1980s"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that Frank Chapman and Charles Owens introduced Chapman's reflex points in the 1930s.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman points were introduced after Louisa Burns's 1907 animal experiments on viscerosomatic reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — integrating Chapman points into osteopathic diagnosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, Chapman's reflex points were established as reflections of which three systems?","choices":{"A":"Visceral, endocrine, and vascular","B":"Somatic, immune, and endocrine reflexes","C":"Autonomic, skeletal, and vascular channels","D":"Visceral, autonomic, and lymphatic","E":"Visceral, muscular, and endocrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chapman and Owens established Chapman's points as localized, tender nodular soft-tissue masses that are visceral-autonomic-lymphatic reflections.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course describes Chapman points as tender nodular soft-tissue findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — integrating Chapman points into osteopathic diagnosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart note, which anemia is named for the stomach Chapman point?","choices":{"A":"Hemolytic anemia from G6PD deficiency","B":"Iron deficiency anemia from chronic bleeding","C":"Pernicious anemia from B12 deficiency","D":"Aplastic anemia from marrow failure","E":"Sickle cell anemia from hemoglobin S"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart lists the stomach point with acid, ulcer, neoplasm, gas and bloating, and nonspecific gastric hyper-congestion, and notes that it may include pernicious anemia, described in the chart as B12 deficiency from autoimmune attack of stomach intrinsic cells.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart also notes that the stomach point can mimic a duodenal ulcer or pyloric obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — integrating Chapman points into osteopathic diagnosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the tongue?","choices":{"A":"Second rib cartilage at the angle of Louis","B":"First rib cartilage at the manubrium of the sternum","C":"Upper edge of the second rib 2.5 inches from the sternum","D":"Interspace between the first and second ribs","E":"Sternalis muscle on the sternum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The chart places the tongue point at the second rib cartilage near the sternum at the angle of Louis, with the posterior point across the superior-posterior aspect of C2.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the tongue point sits at the second rib cartilage, the same rib level as the angle of Louis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — Chapman point locations by organ","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the ear?","choices":{"A":"Superior humerus at the surgical neck of the arm","B":"First rib cartilage at the manubrium of the sternum","C":"Medial aspect of the coracoid process of the scapula","D":"Upper edge of the second rib 3.5 inches from the sternum","E":"Upper edge of proximal clavicle at the first rib"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chart places the ear point at the upper edge of the proximal clavicle where it crosses the first rib, with the posterior point at C1.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists otitis media, mastoiditis, vertigo, and sea sickness for the ear point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — Chapman point locations by organ","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Left seventh intercostal space near the anterior axillary line","B":"Between ribs 8 and 10 from xiphoid to hypogastrium","C":"Outer middle thigh from the greater trochanter toward the knee","D":"Right fifth intercostal space near the anterior axillary line","E":"Upper to lower border of the pubic bone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart places the small intestine point between ribs 8 and 10 from the xiphoid to the hypogastrium, with posterior points at the T8, T9, and T10 intertransverse spaces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists terminal ileum involvement of Crohn disease for this point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — Chapman point locations by organ","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Right eighth intercostal space along the mid-axillary line","B":"Fourth intercostal space close to the sternum on the right","C":"Sixth intercostal space from mid-mammary line to sternum","D":"Left eighth intercostal space near the xiphoid process","E":"Third intercostal space on the left near the sternum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart places the gallbladder point in the sixth intercostal space on a line between the mid-mammary line and the sternum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists cholelithiasis for this point and notes liver involvement can be included.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — treatment principles for Chapman points","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart, which condition is listed with the spleen Chapman point?","choices":{"A":"Gallstones in the gallbladder","B":"Chronic pancreatic inflammation","C":"Bleeding internal hemorrhoids","D":"Infectious mononucleosis","E":"Recurrent bladder infection"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The chart lists leukemia, enlarged spleen, infectious mononucleosis, and generalized infections for the spleen point in the left seventh intercostal space.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart also lists infectious mononucleosis under tonsillitis for the throat point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — treatment principles for Chapman points","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 5 chart note, where else may a liver reflex be found?","choices":{"A":"Along the right trapezius","B":"Along the right deltoid","C":"Over the right sternoclavicular joint","D":"Along the right biceps","E":"Over the right lateral epicondyle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart note for the liver, listed as torpid and nonspecific with an anterior point in the right fifth intercostal space, says a reflex may also be found along the right deltoid.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the liver posterior point is at the T5 and T6 intertransverse spaces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — treatment principles for Chapman points","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has Hashimoto thyroiditis. According to the Week 5 chart, where are the thyroid Chapman points?","choices":{"A":"Sternalis muscle and the junction of the tip of T10 and the 10th rib","B":"Fourth intercostal space near the sternum and the T4 and T5 intertransverse spaces","C":"Left fifth intercostal space and the T5 and T6 intertransverse spaces","D":"Second intercostal space near the sternum and across the posterior aspect of T2","E":"Upper edge of the second rib 2.5 inches from the sternum and across the posterior aspect of C2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The chart lists hypothyroidism, including Hashimoto thyroiditis, and hyperthyroidism under the thyroid with the anterior point in the second intercostal space close to the sternum and the posterior point across the posterior aspects of T2.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the thyroid is one of the four BETH points on the chart.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has sciatica from a lumbosacral herniated nucleus pulposus. According to the Week 5 chart note, which dysfunction may contribute?","choices":{"A":"An innominate dysfunction","B":"A first rib dysfunction","C":"An occipitoatlantal dysfunction","D":"A sacral base dysfunction","E":"A sternoclavicular dysfunction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The chart note for the lower limb says innominate dysfunction may be involved because of the pelvic-thyroid-adrenal connection.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists six points in its anterior column for the lower limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Further understand the pathophysiologic mechanism of Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has Dupuytren contracture and complex regional pain syndrome of the arm. According to the Week 5 chart, where are the upper extremity Chapman points?","choices":{"A":"Inner upper humerus anteriorly and the TPs of C3, C4, C6, and C7 posteriorly","B":"Right seventh intercostal space anteriorly and right T7 and T8 posteriorly","C":"Third and fourth ribs anteriorly and the C1 vertebral level posteriorly","D":"Medial aspect of the coracoid process anteriorly and C1 posteriorly","E":"Third intercostal space anteriorly and T3 and T4 intertransverse posteriorly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chart places the upper extremity point in the third intercostal space, with the posterior point at the T3 and T4 intertransverse spaces. The chart notes it is often found with a depressed third rib, and also lists the anterior, superior, lateral margin of the scapula, approached from the front with the patient supine.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart ties this point to hypersympathetonia conditions such as complex regional pain syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has right lower quadrant pain. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Right greater trochanter","B":"Tip of the right twelfth rib","C":"Middle outer thigh on the right side","D":"Right seventh intercostal space","E":"Upper border of the pubic bone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart places the appendix point at the tip of the right twelfth rib, with the posterior point at the T11 intertransverse space.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists the appendix posterior point at the T11 intertransverse space.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has sigmoid colitis. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior Chapman point for the sigmoid colon?","choices":{"A":"Distal fifth of the right thigh","B":"Lesser trochanter at the insertion of the psoas","C":"Between the ASIS and the greater trochanter","D":"Left greater trochanter of the femur","E":"Outer middle thigh on the right side"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The chart places the sigmoid point at the left greater trochanter. The descending colon point is the outer middle left thigh.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the large intestine points run from the greater trochanters to one inch above the patella.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the palpation of Chapman's reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has chronic nephritis and hypertension. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the anterior kidney point?","choices":{"A":"Lateral and superior to the umbilicus at the 11 and 1 o'clock positions","B":"Under the superior hood of the umbilical fold at the midline","C":"About 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus at 10 and 2 o'clock","D":"Halfway between the acetabulum and the sciatic notch","E":"Between the sternalis muscle and the xiphoid process"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart places the kidney point about 1 inch lateral to the umbilicus at the 10 and 2 o'clock relative positions, with the posterior points at the T12 and L1 intertransverse spaces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart lists Bright disease, including chronic nephritis, edema, albuminuria, and hypertension, for this point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has cholelithiasis with possible liver involvement and paraspinal tenderness. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the posterior Chapman point for the gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Right T6 and T7 intertransverse spaces","B":"Junction of the tip of T10 and the 10th rib","C":"Across the posterior aspect of C2","D":"T8 through T10 intertransverse spaces","E":"T12 and L1 intertransverse spaces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The chart places the gallbladder posterior point at the right T6 and T7 intertransverse spaces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart notes a gallbladder reflex on the anteromedial right radial head.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has prostatitis. According to the Week 5 chart, where are the prostate Chapman points?","choices":{"A":"Umbilicus anteriorly and across the superior edge of the posterior aspect of L2","B":"Lesser trochanter anteriorly and the lower edge of the iliosacral joint posteriorly","C":"Outer-lateral thigh anteriorly and between PSIS and L5 spinous process posteriorly","D":"Pubic bone border anteriorly and T10 and T11 intertransverse spaces posteriorly","E":"Right greater trochanter anteriorly and a triangle of L2 to L4 across the iliac crest posteriorly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chart places the prostate point along the outer-lateral thigh anteriorly and between the PSIS and the spinous process of L5 posteriorly.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the chart notes that pelvic inflammatory disease of the seminal vesicles can be secondary to prostatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Diagnose and treat visceral pathology utilizing Chapman reflexes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has polycystic ovary syndrome. According to the Week 5 chart, where is the posterior Chapman point for the inner ovary?","choices":{"A":"T11 and T12 intertransverse spaces","B":"T9 and T10 intertransverse spaces","C":"T5 and T6 intertransverse spaces","D":"Across the superior edge of the posterior aspect of L2","E":"Between the PSIS and the spinous process of L5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chart lists PCOS under the ovary, with the anterior point from the upper to the lower border of the pubic bone. The inner ovary posterior point is the T9 and T10 intertransverse spaces, and the outer ovary is T10 and T11.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: PCOS is the condition the chart lists for the ovary point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — Chapman point locations","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Cardiology I","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the heart originates from which spinal cord segments?","choices":{"A":"T7 to T12","B":"C3 to C5","C":"L1 to L2","D":"S2 to S4","E":"T1 to T6"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the heart originates from the T1 to T6 segments of the spinal cord. Cardiac somatic dysfunction is therefore sought in the upper thoracic region.","boardPrep":"Step 1: preganglionic sympathetic neurons lie in the intermediolateral cell column. COMLEX: upper thoracic findings can reflect cardiac disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — sympathetic output and spinal cord facilitation in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":1,"stem":"The heaviest concentration of cardiac nociceptive afferents returns to which spinal segment?","choices":{"A":"T4","B":"T9","C":"T11","D":"L1","E":"S1"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The heaviest concentration of cardiac nociceptive afferents returns to T4, within the T1 to T6 segments that supply sympathetic innervation to the heart.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral afferents enter the cord at the levels of their sympathetic supply. COMLEX: the upper thoracic spine is the focus of the cardiac examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — sympathetic output and spinal cord facilitation in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":1,"stem":"Right-sided sympathetic fibers pass through the deep cardiac plexus to primarily innervate which structure?","choices":{"A":"Atrioventricular node","B":"Left ventricular myocardium","C":"Sinoatrial node","D":"Aortic valve leaflets","E":"Coronary sinus wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Right-sided sympathetic fibers pass through the deep cardiac plexus to innervate the sinoatrial node and the right side of the heart. Left-sided sympathetic fibers innervate the atrioventricular node and the left cardiac plexus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the SA node is the primary pacemaker of the heart. COMLEX: right and left sympathetic fibers have different cardiac targets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — sympathetic output and spinal cord facilitation in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":1,"stem":"Visceral nociceptive afferents synapse with which dorsal horn neurons that also receive somatic input?","choices":{"A":"Renshaw feedback inhibitory cells","B":"Ia reciprocal inhibitory interneurons","C":"Corticospinal pyramidal neurons","D":"Wide dynamic range interneurons","E":"Ventral horn alpha motor neurons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Within laminae I through VII of the dorsal horn, primary visceral afferents synapse with wide dynamic range interneurons, which act as summation units where visceral and somatic inputs converge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: wide dynamic range neurons respond to both noxious and non-noxious input. COMLEX: convergence on these neurons explains referred pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — spinal cord facilitation and baroreflex responses in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism produces referred pain from the heart?","choices":{"A":"Visceral afferents travel with somatic nerves to cutaneous receptors in the chest wall","B":"Somatic afferents synapse in the sympathetic chain ganglia on visceral neurons","C":"Visceral and somatic afferents converge on shared dorsal horn interneuronal pools","D":"Visceral fibers bypass the spinal cord and ascend directly to the thalamus","E":"Somatic afferents carry visceral pain signals to the heart by retrograde conduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Somatic and visceral inputs share common interneuronal pools in the dorsal horn, so the central nervous system interprets visceral nociceptive signals as arising from the corresponding somatic dermatomes and myotomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: convergence-projection is the standard explanation of referred pain. COMLEX: referred pain follows segmental dermatomes and myotomes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — spinal cord facilitation and baroreflex responses in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how do cardiac afferent fibers in the vagus nerve differ from the spinal nociceptive pathway?","choices":{"A":"They synapse in the dorsal horn at T1 to T6 before they ascend to the brainstem nuclei","B":"They bypass the spinal cord gate before they reach the brainstem vagal nuclei","C":"They decussate in the medullary pyramids before they reach the thalamus region","D":"They synapse in the celiac ganglion before they reach the brainstem nuclei","E":"They enter the spinal cord at the sacral level before they ascend to the brainstem"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cardiac vagus afferent fibers duplicate the transmission of nociceptive information but bypass the spinal cord gating mechanism, carrying sensory feedback directly to vagal motor nuclei in the brainstem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal afferents project to the brainstem rather than the spinal cord. COMLEX: this pathway lets cardiac ischemia alter nodal firing directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — spinal cord facilitation and baroreflex responses in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":1,"stem":"Rib raising for sympathetic regulation in a cardiac patient is performed on which ribs?","choices":{"A":"Ribs 6 to 12","B":"Ribs 7 to 10","C":"Ribs 11 to 12","D":"Ribs 1 to 6","E":"Ribs 4 to 9"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rib raising is performed on the upper thoracic rib cage, ribs 1 to 6, where it modulates the sympathetic trunk ganglia located immediately anterior to the rib heads.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic chain lies anterior to the rib heads. COMLEX: match the rib levels to the T1 to T6 cardiac sympathetic supply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — structured OMT sequence in a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":1,"stem":"Rib raising directly modulates which structures in a cardiac patient?","choices":{"A":"The vagal ganglia posterior to the rib heads","B":"The sympathetic trunk ganglia anterior to the rib heads","C":"The phrenic nerve roots at the first rib head","D":"The celiac ganglion beneath the crura of the diaphragm","E":"Dorsal root ganglia lateral to the transverse processes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rib raising directly modulates the sympathetic trunk ganglia located immediately anterior to the rib heads, which down-regulates excessive sympathetic outflow and decreases systemic vasoconstriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower sympathetic tone reduces afterload. COMLEX: rib raising also improves thoracic cage compliance for venous and lymphatic return.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — structured OMT sequence in a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":1,"stem":"Releasing somatic dysfunction at the OA joint and C2 influences which two structures?","choices":{"A":"The stellate ganglion and the phrenic nerve roots","B":"The celiac ganglion and the accessory nerve","C":"The phrenic nerve and the trigeminal ganglion","D":"The inferior cervical ganglion and the glossopharyngeal nerve","E":"The superior cervical ganglion and the vagus nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Releasing somatic dysfunctions at the OA joint and C2 influences both the superior cervical sympathetic ganglion and the vagus nerve as it exits the jugular foramen. Normalizing vagal tone stabilizes SA and AV nodal conduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve exits the skull at the jugular foramen. COMLEX: upper cervical OMT is aimed at parasympathetic balance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — structured OMT sequence in a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":1,"stem":"Cardiac viscerosomatic dysfunction is characteristically centered at which segmental levels?","choices":{"A":"T6 to T10 on the right","B":"T10 to L1 on the left","C":"C2 to C4 on the right","D":"T1 to T5 on the left","E":"L1 to L5 on the left"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cardiac viscerosomatic dysfunction and tissue texture changes are centered at T1 to T5 on the left, with the highest frequency at T2 and T3. More than 70 percent of cardiac somatic dysfunctions occur between T1 and T5 on the left.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac afferents enter the cord at the segments of their sympathetic supply. COMLEX: somatic findings cluster at T1 to T5 on the left, and T2 and T3 are the most frequent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — clinical findings that separate cardiac from non-cardiac causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the anterior Chapman's reflex point for the heart located?","choices":{"A":"The second intercostal space near the sternum","B":"The seventh intercostal space near the xiphoid process","C":"The fourth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line","D":"The first intercostal space at the clavicle","E":"The ninth intercostal space at the costal margin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anterior Chapman's reflex point for the heart is in the second intercostal space near the sternum. Palpation reveals an exquisitely tender, pea-sized, ganglioform fascial contraction indicating cardiac stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chapman points are reflex points reflecting visceral stress. COMLEX: a tender second-space point is consistent with cardiac stress alongside other findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — clinical findings that separate cardiac from non-cardiac causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":1,"stem":"The right fifth intercostal space trigger point is specifically correlated with which cardiac condition?","choices":{"A":"Ventricular fibrillation and heart block","B":"Sinus tachycardia and atrial arrhythmias","C":"Mitral stenosis and aortic regurgitation","D":"Pericarditis and pericardial effusion","E":"Angina pectoris and myocardial infarction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The right fifth intercostal space trigger point lies below the right fifth rib, midway between the sternal margin and the nipple line, and it is specifically correlated with sinus tachycardias and atrial arrhythmias.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this myofascial point is tied by the course to rhythm disturbance. COMLEX: the left pectoralis major trigger point refers pain to the chest and left arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — clinical findings that separate cardiac from non-cardiac causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement reflects the course's view of the role of OMT in cardiovascular disease?","choices":{"A":"OMT replaces drug therapy once somatic dysfunction has resolved","B":"OMT is a substitute for drug therapy in stable patients with arrhythmias","C":"OMT is an adjunct to emergency care and standard pharmacotherapy","D":"OMT is reserved for patients who cannot tolerate standard drug therapy","E":"OMT is an alternative to emergency care for patients with acute ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Standard-of-care medical management is essential to reduce afterload, manage intravascular volume, prevent ventricular remodeling, and control acute ischemia or life-threatening arrhythmias. OMT is an adjunct and does not replace emergency care or standard pharmacotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute ischemia and arrhythmia need immediate medical treatment. COMLEX: OMT complements medical therapy by targeting autonomic imbalance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic balance with pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does normalizing parasympathetic outflow with upper cervical OMT do in a cardiac patient?","choices":{"A":"It speeds heart rate, shortens diastolic filling time, and lowers cardiac output","B":"It slows heart rate, increases diastolic filling, and improves cardiac output","C":"It slows heart rate, increases diastolic filling time, and raises afterload","D":"It speeds heart rate, shortens diastolic filling time, and raises afterload","E":"It holds heart rate steady, shortens diastole, and lowers preload"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normalizing parasympathetic outflow through upper cervical OMT slows heart rate, increases diastolic filling time, and improves cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal tone slows the heart rate. COMLEX: upper cervical OMT is paired with medical therapy rather than substituted for it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic balance with pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which OMT target can enable reductions in diuretic or antihypertensive drug dosages?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic cage mechanics","B":"Sacral base position","C":"Pelvic diaphragm tension","D":"Lower extremity alignment","E":"Lumbar lordosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Addressing thoracic cage mechanics enhances fluid circulation, which the course says can enable reductions in diuretic or antihypertensive drug dosages while improving functional capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thoracic cage compliance supports venous and lymphatic return. COMLEX: dose changes remain a medical decision made with the prescriber.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic balance with pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the foundation of the integrative plan for congestive heart failure?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic spine muscle energy","B":"Cranial compression of the fourth ventricle","C":"A progressive walking program","D":"Bilateral rib raising","E":"Guideline-directed medical therapy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The foundation of the integrative plan is standard medical management with guideline-directed medical therapy. Targeted OMT is added to this foundation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: guideline-directed therapy reduces mortality in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. COMLEX: OMT is layered on top of medical therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — integrative treatment plan for a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":1,"stem":"Which lifestyle and monitoring measures are included in the medical foundation for CHF?","choices":{"A":"Sodium and fluid limits with daily weights","B":"Generous fluid loading with weekly weights","C":"High-sodium intake with monthly weight checks","D":"Strict bed rest with no weight monitoring","E":"Unrestricted salt intake with daily exercise"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lifestyle and monitoring component restricts dietary sodium and fluid intake, monitors daily weights, and treats underlying hypertension or ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a rapid weight gain signals fluid retention. COMLEX: daily weights guide diuretic adjustment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — integrative treatment plan for a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":1,"stem":"When does the CHF plan start a progressive walking program?","choices":{"A":"At the first visit before medical therapy is started","B":"During an episode of acute decompensation","C":"Once the patient is compensated","D":"Immediately after each lymphatic pump session","E":"When the daily weight rises by several pounds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rehabilitation phase starts a progressive walking program once the patient is compensated, with serial re-evaluation of left ventricular ejection fraction, exercise tolerance, peripheral edema, and medication requirements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: exercise is introduced after hemodynamic stability. COMLEX: long-term monitoring follows ejection fraction, edema, and drug needs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 5 — integrative treatment plan for a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has crushing substernal pain from myocardial ischemia. Which somatic referral sites are typical?","choices":{"A":"Right shoulder, right scapula, right flank, and lateral thigh","B":"Anterior chest, left arm, left jaw, and upper back","C":"Lower back, buttocks, posterior thigh, and calf muscles","D":"Occiput, temples, forehead, and orbits of the eyes","E":"Left flank, left groin, testicle, and medial thigh"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Viscerosomatic pain from myocardial ischemia or infarction typically refers to the substernal anterior chest wall, left arm and shoulder, left side of the jaw, epigastric region, left ribs, and the upper back.","boardPrep":"Step 1: convergence of cardiac and somatic afferents explains the referral pattern. COMLEX: the referral sites follow the T1 to T6 segmental supply.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — clinical findings that separate cardiac from non-cardiac causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has slowed atrioventricular nodal conduction. Which nerve primarily supplies the AV node?","choices":{"A":"The right vagus nerve","B":"The left phrenic nerve","C":"The right glossopharyngeal nerve","D":"The left hypoglossal nerve","E":"The left vagus nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parasympathetic innervation arises from the vagus nerve, with the right vagus nerve primarily innervating the SA node and the left vagus nerve supplying the AV node.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve slows the SA node and the AV node. COMLEX: upper cervical release aims to normalize vagal tone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — autonomic tone balance and parasympathetic influence","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the spinal segmental origin of sympathetic innervation to the heart (T1–T6) and list the common somatic referral sites for viscerosomatic pain associated with myocardial infarction.","batch":2,"stem":"A student asks where the preganglionic sympathetic neurons to the heart arise. Which answer is correct?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal horn gray matter at C1 to C4","B":"The ventral horn gray matter at T7 to T12","C":"The intermediolateral cell column at T1 to T6","D":"The nucleus ambiguus in the lower medulla","E":"The sacral parasympathetic nucleus at S2 to S4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the heart arises from T1 to T6, and increased sympathetic efferent outflow from the intermediolateral cell column produces local tissue changes across the T1 to T6 dermatomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the intermediolateral cell column is the lateral horn of the thoracic cord. COMLEX: efferent sympathetic changes appear in the same segments as the afferent input.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — sympathetic output and facilitation in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has repeated episodes of myocardial ischemia. Which effect results from the continuous afferent bombardment of her dorsal horn?","choices":{"A":"Raised firing thresholds that let the cord ignore normal somatic input from the chest wall","B":"Fixed firing thresholds that stop the referred pain signals from reaching the cortex","C":"Lowered firing thresholds that confine efferent responses to the visceral input itself","D":"Raised firing thresholds that reduce the sympathetic efferent outflow to the heart","E":"Lowered firing thresholds that let normal somatic input trigger exaggerated responses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Continuous, intense afferent bombardment from ischemic cardiac tissue produces central sensitization, or spinal cord facilitation. Facilitation lowers the firing threshold of dorsal horn interneurons and motor neurons, so even normal baseline somatic input triggers exaggerated efferent responses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: central sensitization lowers thresholds for pain transmission. COMLEX: a facilitated segment reacts excessively to normal input.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — spinal cord facilitation and sympathetic output","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with acute myocardial ischemia has facilitated efferent output at T1 to T6. Which tissue findings are expected?","choices":{"A":"Flaccid paraspinal muscles, tissue atrophy, pallor, and decreased skin moisture","B":"Bilateral limb weakness, absent reflexes, muscular hypotonia, and cool skin","C":"Fusiform paraspinal contraction, edema, erythema, and altered skin moisture","D":"Vertebral osteophytes, joint effusion, ligament laxity, and bony tenderness","E":"Diffuse myalgia, fever, lymphadenopathy, and skin vesicles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Facilitated efferent motor output produces fusiform paraspinal muscle contractions and intercostal hypertonicity, and increased sympathetic outflow induces localized subcutaneous edema, erythema, hyperesthesia, and altered skin moisture across the T1 to T6 dermatomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic outflow alters vascular tone and sweat gland activity. COMLEX: tissue texture changes mark the facilitated segments.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — spinal cord facilitation and somatic tissue changes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the neurophysiological mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation, detailing how cardiac nociceptive afferent signals integrate at the spinal cord dorsal horn to produce somatic tissue changes and referred pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has acute myocardial ischemia. How do cardiac afferents in the vagus nerve affect his heart rhythm according to the course?","choices":{"A":"They send feedback to brainstem vagal nuclei that alters SA and AV nodal firing rates","B":"They carry feedback to the intermediolateral cell column and raise ventricular contractility","C":"They carry feedback to the dorsal horn and block referred pain from the heart","D":"They carry feedback to the sympathetic chain and speed conduction through the AV node","E":"They carry feedback to the phrenic nucleus and slow the pulmonary arterial flow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cardiac vagus afferent fibers carry sensory feedback directly to vagal motor nuclei in the brainstem, directly altering SA and AV nodal firing rates and overriding inherent cardiac rhythm generators during acute ischemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal afferents project to the brainstem and not through the spinal cord gate. COMLEX: this pathway helps explain rhythm changes during ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — vagal tone and baroreflex sensitivity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with hypertension has segmental dysfunction at T2 and T3 on the left. Which approach does the course describe for these upper thoracic segments in a cardiac patient?","choices":{"A":"Forceful high-velocity thrust, aggressive articulation, or sustained traction","B":"Deep visceral manipulation, rapid oscillation, or forceful rib springing","C":"Rapid thoracic pump, heavy rib compression, or abrupt myofascial stretching","D":"Cranial compression, high-velocity cervical thrust, or rhythmic sacral rocking","E":"Gentle muscle energy, facilitated positional release, or soft tissue work"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Upper thoracic mobilization at T1 to T5 uses gentle muscle energy, facilitated positional release, or soft tissue techniques. Reducing segmental facilitation decreases sympathetic drive to the myocardium and coronary vasculature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lowering sympathetic drive reduces myocardial oxygen demand. COMLEX: gentle techniques are chosen for cardiac patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — structured OMT sequence in a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with heart failure receives compression of the fourth ventricle. What effect does the course attribute to this technique?","choices":{"A":"It suppresses Traube–Hering–Mayer oscillations and reduces baroreceptor reflex responsiveness","B":"It enhances Traube–Hering–Mayer oscillations and augments baroreceptor reflex responsiveness","C":"It raises intrathoracic pressure during inhalation and reduces thoracic duct drainage","D":"It enlarges the cardiac output by directly increasing myocardial contractility and stroke volume","E":"It lowers the thoracic inlet fascial tension and widens the clavicular space bilaterally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Compression of the fourth ventricle, CV-4, enhances the Traube–Hering–Mayer fluid oscillations and augments baroreceptor reflex responsiveness, assisting systemic fluid movement and autonomic balance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the baroreflex buffers blood pressure fluctuations. COMLEX: cranial OMT is one of the autonomic techniques in the course plan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — baroreflex responses in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Determine appropriate Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT) techniques (such as rib-raising, myofascial release, or treatment of upper cervical dysfunctions) to address excessive sympathetic tone and enhance lymphatic return in a cardiac patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has scalene, thoracic inlet, and diaphragm restrictions. How does releasing them improve fluid return?","choices":{"A":"Greater diaphragmatic excursion decreases negative intrathoracic pressure and traps lymph within the thorax","B":"Greater scalene tension elevates the first rib during inhalation and widens the thoracic inlet opening","C":"Greater abdominal pressure compresses the thoracic duct during exhalation and lowers lymph flow centrally","D":"Greater diaphragmatic excursion increases negative intrathoracic pressure and aids thoracic duct drainage","E":"Greater inlet fascial tension holds the clavicle elevated during inhalation and widens the venous angle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Treating fascial restrictions at the thoracic inlet, scalenes, and thoracoabdominal diaphragm restores the two-chambered intrathoracic pressure pump. Optimizing diaphragmatic excursion increases negative intrathoracic pressure during inhalation, which enhances thoracic duct drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inspiration draws venous blood and lymph toward the thorax. COMLEX: release of the inlet and diaphragm restores the pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — intrathoracic pressure pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has deep, crushing, poorly localized chest pressure that chest wall palpation does not reproduce. Neither rest nor a change of position relieves it, and he has diaphoresis and nausea. Which interpretation fits the course's description?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac pain, which is poorly localized, unprovoked by palpation, and has autonomic signs","B":"Musculoskeletal pain, which is poorly localized, unrelieved by rest, and accompanied by diaphoresis","C":"Musculoskeletal pain, which is unprovoked by palpation and accompanied by nausea","D":"Cardiac pain, which is sharp, well localized, and reproduced by chest wall palpation","E":"Musculoskeletal pain, which is unprovoked by palpation and relieved by postural change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the course's description, cardiac pain is deep, crushing, pressing, or aching, poorly localized, unprovoked by chest wall palpation, unrelieved by rest or postural changes, and accompanied by autonomic signs such as diaphoresis, dyspnea, nausea, and anxiety. Noncardiac musculoskeletal pain is well localized and reproducible by palpation or trunk motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic chest pain is visceral and poorly localized. COMLEX: pain reproduced by palpation or trunk motion favors a musculoskeletal source in the course's framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — clinical examination findings that separate cardiac from non-cardiac causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, a 66-year-old man with an inferior wall myocardial infarction has T1 to T5 findings. Which additional somatic finding is particularly frequent?","choices":{"A":"Lower lumbar dysfunction at L4 and L5","B":"Upper cervical dysfunction at OA and C2","C":"Sacral base dysfunction at S2 and S3","D":"Lower thoracic dysfunction at T10 to T12","E":"Pubic symphysis shear dysfunction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upper cervical dysfunctions at OA and C2 are frequently present with T1 to T5 findings, particularly in inferior or posterior wall myocardial infarctions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the infarct location shapes the pattern of autonomic and somatic findings. COMLEX: add the upper cervical region to the cardiac screening examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — autonomic tone balance and upper cervical OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction and noncardiac musculoskeletal pain by analyzing physical examination findings, segmental tissue texture changes (e.g., T1–T5 on the left), and specific anatomical indicators like Chapman points or Travell trigger points.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has chest wall pain. At T5 the vertebra shows sidebending restriction to the right, and the rib shows sidebending restriction to the left. Which interpretation fits the course?","choices":{"A":"Viscerosomatic cardiac dysfunction with costal linkage","B":"Viscerosomatic cardiac dysfunction with a mirror-image costal element","C":"Purely somatic dysfunction with costal linkage","D":"Purely somatic dysfunction with a mirror-image costal element","E":"Referred cardiac pain with incidental costal findings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In purely somatic dysfunction the costal element behaves as a mirror image of the corresponding vertebral segment, for example vertebral sidebending restriction right with costal sidebending restriction left. Viscerosomatic cardiac dysfunction shows costal linkage, in which the costal and vertebral elements restrict in the same direction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a mirror-image costal restriction is the mechanical pattern. COMLEX: costal linkage points to visceral or autonomic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — linked versus non-linked costal dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with acute chest pressure and signs of ischemia is evaluated by his osteopathic physician. Which approach follows the course?","choices":{"A":"Begin with upper thoracic OMT first, with medications delayed until the facilitation resolves","B":"Arrange emergency care and standard pharmacotherapy first, with OMT as an adjunct","C":"Treat with rib raising and observe, with any decision about emergency care made later","D":"Use cervical OMT first, with antiplatelet and nitrate therapy replaced by manipulation","E":"Refer for a cranial technique first, with medication avoided to restore autonomic balance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Standard-of-care medical management, including antiplatelets, nitrates, and other drugs, is essential to control acute ischemia or life-threatening arrhythmias, and OMT is an adjunct and does not replace emergency care or standard pharmacotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute coronary syndromes need immediate antiplatelet and anti-ischemic therapy. COMLEX: OMT is an adjunct and does not replace emergency care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — justification for combining pharmacotherapy and OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with hypertension receives treatment to down-regulate spinal facilitation at T1 to T5. Which effect does the course expect?","choices":{"A":"Less sympathetic efferent discharge, with peripheral vasodilation and lower afterload","B":"More sympathetic efferent discharge, with peripheral vasodilation and unchanged afterload","C":"Less sympathetic efferent discharge, with peripheral vasoconstriction and faster heart rate","D":"Unchanged sympathetic efferent discharge, with peripheral vasoconstriction and higher afterload","E":"Less parasympathetic outflow, with bradycardia and higher afterload"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Down-regulating spinal facilitation at T1 to T5 reduces excessive sympathetic efferent discharge, promoting peripheral vasodilation and reducing afterload.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic tone sets arteriolar resistance. COMLEX: lower sympathetic drive also reduces myocardial oxygen demand.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — autonomic tone balance and OMT as an adjunct","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Assess the clinical presentation and physical examination of a patient with cardiovascular disease (such as congestive heart failure or arrhythmias) to determine the balance between autonomic influences and standard-of-care pharmacologic interventions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man has heart failure with chronic sympathicotonia and impaired baroreceptor inhibition. According to the course, which set of changes does this autonomic state produce?","choices":{"A":"Greater peripheral vasodilation, faster heart rate, and reduced myocardial oxygen consumption","B":"Greater peripheral vasoconstriction, slower heart rate, and reduced myocardial oxygen consumption","C":"Greater peripheral vasoconstriction, faster heart rate, and higher myocardial oxygen consumption","D":"Lower peripheral vasoconstriction, slower heart rate, and higher myocardial oxygen consumption","E":"Greater peripheral vasodilation, slower heart rate, and unchanged myocardial oxygen consumption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A chronic elevation in sympathetic tone combined with impaired baroreceptor reflex inhibition is a major cardiovascular risk factor. Elevated sympathetic drive increases peripheral vasoconstriction, accelerates heart rate, and elevates myocardial oxygen consumption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and vascular resistance. COMLEX: this autonomic imbalance is the target of adjunctive OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — altered baroreflex responses in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with compensated congestive heart failure is evaluated for an integrative plan. Which plan reflects the course?","choices":{"A":"Guideline-directed drug therapy and sodium restriction, with gentle thoracic OMT and slow pumping","B":"Gentle upper thoracic OMT and a fast lymphatic pump, with drug therapy held until edema resolves","C":"Fluid loading and daily exercise, with forceful thoracic thrusts and a rapid lymphatic pump","D":"Cranial OMT in place of drug therapy, with sodium restriction and a rapid lymphatic pump","E":"Guideline-directed drug therapy and fluid loading, with forceful thoracic thrusts and rib raising"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The plan starts with guideline-directed medical therapy and sodium and fluid restriction and adds targeted OMT, including gentle upper thoracic treatment and slow, gentle oscillatory thoracic lymphatic pumps administered gradually.","boardPrep":"Step 1: heart failure therapy reduces preload, afterload, and neurohormonal activation. COMLEX: lymphatic pumps are given gradually in decompensated patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — structured OMT sequence and cautions in a cardiology case","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with CHF receives bilateral rib raising as part of the plan. Which effect is intended?","choices":{"A":"Increased sympathetic chain activity, with peripheral vasoconstriction and higher systemic vascular resistance","B":"Decreased vagal outflow, with tachycardia and shorter diastolic filling time","C":"Increased preload, with higher venous pressure and greater pulmonary edema formation","D":"Decreased sympathetic chain overactivity, with peripheral vasodilation and lower vascular resistance","E":"Decreased lymphatic return, with higher thoracic duct pressure and more peripheral edema"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral rib raising from ribs 1 to 6 decreases sympathetic chain overactivity, inducing peripheral vasodilation and decreasing systemic vascular resistance, or afterload.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance. COMLEX: rib raising is part of the afterload-reduction step of the plan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — rib raising and sympathetic outflow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Design an integrative osteopathic treatment plan for a patient presenting with congestive heart failure that combines standard medical therapy with targeted OMT to reduce afterload, improve lymphatic drainage, and balance autonomic tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with CHF has OA, C1, and C2 restrictions. Which step of the plan addresses them and what is the goal?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic inlet release to reduce mechanical traction on the phrenic nerve and stellate ganglia","B":"Rib raising to reduce mechanical traction on the vagus nerve and celiac ganglia","C":"Upper cervical treatment to reduce traction on the vagus nerve and superior cervical ganglia","D":"Diaphragm release to reduce mechanical traction on the glossopharyngeal nerve and splanchnic ganglia","E":"Lymphatic pump to reduce mechanical traction on the trigeminal nerve and sphenopalatine ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treating suboccipital and upper cervical restrictions at OA, C1, and C2 eliminates mechanical traction on the vagus nerve and superior cervical ganglia, which stabilizes heart rate and improves baroreceptor sensitivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve exits at the jugular foramen. COMLEX: this step targets parasympathetic tone and baroreflex sensitivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — suboccipital and cervical release in the structured sequence","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Cardiology II","questions":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits dorsal horn interneurons in spinal cord facilitation?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hypoexcitability","B":"Transient silent quiescence","C":"Refractory inhibition","D":"Periodic oscillation","E":"Chronic hyperexcitability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In spinal cord facilitation the dorsal horn interneurons remain in a chronic hyperexcitable state, which drives excessive, continuous sympathetic efferent output to segmentally related cardiovascular structures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitation lowers the firing threshold of dorsal horn neurons. COMLEX: a facilitated segment responds excessively to normal input.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics and the intrathoracic pressure pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":1,"stem":"Sustained sympathicotonia causes arteriolar vasoconstriction. Which hemodynamic variable does this raise directly?","choices":{"A":"Diastolic ventricular filling time","B":"End-diastolic ventricular volume","C":"Baroreflex buffering capacity","D":"Systemic vascular resistance","E":"Beat-to-beat heart rate variability"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sustained sympathicotonia causes arteriolar vasoconstriction throughout the systemic circulation, which directly elevates systemic vascular resistance, the afterload on the ventricle. Afterload increases myocardial tension, oxygen demand, and energy demand.","boardPrep":"Step 1: afterload is the wall tension the ventricle must overcome during contraction. COMLEX: down-regulating sympathetic tone lowers afterload.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics and the intrathoracic pressure pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, restricted diaphragmatic excursion affects venous return and preload in what way?","choices":{"A":"Increased venous return and higher preload","B":"Reduced venous return and lower preload","C":"Unchanged venous return and unchanged preload","D":"Increased venous return and lower afterload","E":"Unchanged venous return and lower afterload"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Restricted diaphragmatic excursion diminishes negative intrathoracic pressure during inspiration, which reduces venous return to the right atrium and the low-pressure pulmonary circulation and lowers preload, the end-diastolic ventricular volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative intrathoracic pressure during inspiration draws venous blood toward the heart. COMLEX: thoracic cage restriction lowers preload in a failing heart.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics and the intrathoracic pressure pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"In Johnston's three-segment functional approach, how do the segments above and below move relative to the dysfunctional segment?","choices":{"A":"In the reverse direction from it","B":"In the same direction as it","C":"With no motion relative to it","D":"In a random direction with each breath","E":"In a plane perpendicular to it"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asymmetric motor system responses occur across a three-segment unit: the central dysfunctional segment and the segments immediately above and below, which move in the exact opposite direction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: functional models assess motion at its initiation. COMLEX: the functional approach evaluates subtle motor responses rather than end-range resistance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for diaphragmatic release and related techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"In indirect functional treatment, toward which direction does the clinician stack the motion parameters?","choices":{"A":"The direction of greatest bind","B":"The direction of greatest ease","C":"The direction of the restrictive barrier","D":"The direction of neutral alignment","E":"The direction of the patient's pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The clinician introduces each motion pair and stacks the parameters in the direction of greatest ease, also called freedom of motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an indirect technique moves away from the barrier. COMLEX: the stacked position of ease induces sensory-motor recalibration and tissue release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for diaphragmatic release and related techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the clinician place the hands for thoracoabdominal diaphragm release?","choices":{"A":"Across the manubrium and medial clavicles","B":"Beneath the posterior rib angles bilaterally","C":"Over the clavicles and the sternal notch","D":"Along the iliac crests and the pubic symphysis","E":"Along the costal margins and xiphoid region"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For thoracoabdominal diaphragm release the patient is supine and the clinician places both hands along the anterior costal margins and xiphoid region, and the patient holds the breath in exhalation to tolerance to facilitate a release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm attaches to the costal margin and xiphoid process. COMLEX: diaphragm release is an indirect technique guided by tissue ease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for diaphragmatic release and related techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, at which levels is costal linkage commonly observed in patients with cardiovascular disease or hypertension?","choices":{"A":"C2, T4, and T8","B":"C6, T2, and T6","C":"T1, T5, and T9","D":"C4, T3, and T10","E":"C5, T7, and T11"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that costal linkage is commonly observed at C6, T2, and T6 in patients with cardiovascular disease or hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic outflow to the heart arises from T1 to T6. COMLEX: these levels are screened for costal findings in cardiovascular disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":1,"stem":"In the differential for edema, which viscerosomatic level corresponds to renal sympathetic innervation?","choices":{"A":"T1 to T5 on the left","B":"C2 to C4 segments","C":"T10 to L1 junction","D":"L4 to S2 segments","E":"T6 to T9 segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In renal failure, viscerosomatic reflexes are found at the T10 to L1 thoracolumbar junction, which corresponds to renal sympathetic innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic fibers arise from the lower thoracic and upper lumbar cord. COMLEX: the segmental level helps separate renal from cardiac causes of edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding points to liver failure as the cause of peripheral edema?","choices":{"A":"Elevated jugular venous distention and an S3 gallop","B":"Uremic frost and oliguria","C":"Daytime hypercapnia and polycythemia","D":"Pulmonary rales and orthopnea","E":"Spider angiomas and caput medusae"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Edema from cirrhosis is characteristically preceded by prominent ascites and accompanied by scleral icterus, spider angiomas, caput medusae, and asterixis.  The other choices are clues for other causes of edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension and low albumin synthesis cause edema in cirrhosis. COMLEX: the associated stigmata of liver disease separate it from cardiac edema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is a marker of end-organ damage in a patient with hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Age over 60 years in a male patient","B":"A long history of cigarette smoking","C":"EKG signs of left ventricular hypertrophy","D":"A family history of premature CVD in a relative","E":"Dyslipidemia with a low HDL cholesterol level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"End-organ damage markers include funduscopic AV nicking, hemorrhages, or exudates, EKG signs of left ventricular hypertrophy, diminished peripheral arterial pulses, and elevated serum creatinine or microalbuminuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic pressure overload causes left ventricular hypertrophy. COMLEX: end-organ damage strengthens the case for combined therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences and pharmacologic interventions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which item is a cardiovascular risk factor rather than a marker of end-organ damage?","choices":{"A":"Diabetes mellitus","B":"Funduscopic AV nicking","C":"Elevated serum creatinine","D":"Microalbuminuria","E":"Diminished peripheral arterial pulses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The risk factors in the course are age over 60 years, male sex or postmenopausal female status, smoking, dyslipidemia, diabetes mellitus, and a family history of premature cardiovascular disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes accelerates atherosclerosis and renal injury. COMLEX: risk factors and end-organ damage together guide how aggressively to treat.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences and pharmacologic interventions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of effects results from chronic elevation of sympathetic tone?","choices":{"A":"Lower afterload, faster heart rate, and improved cardiac remodeling","B":"Greater afterload, slower heart rate, and reverse remodeling of the myocardium","C":"Lower afterload, slower heart rate, and adverse cardiac remodeling","D":"Greater afterload, faster heart rate, and adverse cardiac remodeling","E":"Unchanged afterload, faster heart rate, and protective cardiac remodeling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic elevation of sympathetic tone increases peripheral vasoconstriction, accelerates heart rate, elevates allostatic load, and promotes adverse cardiac remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation raises both vascular resistance and heart rate. COMLEX: impaired vagal tone adds to the burden by lowering baroreflex sensitivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 5 — balancing autonomic influences and pharmacologic interventions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":1,"stem":"Per the JNC 8 guidance in the course, which initial drug class is chosen for the Black population without chronic kidney disease?","choices":{"A":"An ACE inhibitor or an angiotensin receptor blocker","B":"A beta-blocker or an alpha-adrenergic blocker","C":"A thiazide diuretic or a calcium channel blocker","D":"A loop diuretic or an aldosterone antagonist","E":"A centrally acting alpha agonist or a nitrate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the course's JNC 8 summary, the non-Black general population starts an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, a thiazide diuretic, or a calcium channel blocker, and the Black population starts a thiazide diuretic or a calcium channel blocker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides lower blood pressure by diuresis and calcium channel blockers by vasodilation. COMLEX: initial drug choice depends on population and comorbidity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 6 — integrative osteopathic treatment plan for congestive heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":1,"stem":"The lifestyle plan includes the DASH diet. What daily sodium limit does the course give?","choices":{"A":"Less than 3,000 mg per day","B":"Less than 4,000 mg per day","C":"Less than 6,000 mg per day","D":"Less than 2,000 mg per day","E":"Less than 8,000 mg per day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lifestyle modifications include weight loss to a BMI of 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m², the DASH diet with sodium restriction below 2,000 mg per day, regular low-impact aerobic exercise, smoking cessation, and alcohol moderation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sodium restriction lowers volume and blood pressure. COMLEX: lifestyle change is the foundation that medication and OMT add to.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 6 — integrative osteopathic treatment plan for congestive heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":1,"stem":"Which OMT in the structured sequence comes first to open the main lymphatic drainage pathway into the subclavian veins?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic inlet release","B":"Bilateral rib raising","C":"Upper cervical release","D":"Respiratory diaphragm release","E":"Thoracic lymphatic pump"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thoracic inlet release treats transverse fascial restrictions at the cervicothoracic junction to open the main lymphatic drainage pathway into the subclavian veins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymph drains into the venous system near the subclavian veins. COMLEX: clearing the inlet first allows lymph from later techniques to drain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 6 — integrative osteopathic treatment plan for congestive heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has heart failure with increased beta-adrenergic tone. Which consequence is expected for his baroreflex control?","choices":{"A":"Impaired buffering of blood pressure fluctuations and reduced heart rate variability","B":"Enhanced buffering of blood pressure fluctuations and increased heart rate variability","C":"Preserved baroreflex inhibition and lower heart rate with reduced beta-adrenergic tone","D":"Increased baroreceptor firing and loss of sympathetic drive to the heart","E":"Improved down-regulation of heart rate and restored vagal outflow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In heart failure the loss of baroreflex inhibition impairs the body's ability to buffer blood pressure fluctuations, down-regulate heart rate, and maintain heart rate variability, which removes a protective buffer against hemodynamic overload.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the baroreflex normally opposes changes in blood pressure. COMLEX: impaired baroreflex control is part of the autonomic imbalance in heart failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — autonomic imbalance in cardiovascular disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with heart failure has progressive thoracic cage restriction. According to the course and the Frank-Starling relationship, which sequence explains the worsening of her failing heart?","choices":{"A":"Increased venous return, longer end-diastolic fiber length, and improved stroke volume","B":"Reduced venous return, longer end-diastolic fiber length, and improved stroke volume","C":"Reduced venous return, shorter end-diastolic fiber length, and impaired stroke volume","D":"Increased venous return, shorter end-diastolic fiber length, and impaired stroke volume","E":"Unchanged venous return, longer end-diastolic fiber length, and impaired stroke volume"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Restricted diaphragmatic excursion reduces venous return and preload. By the Frank-Starling relationship, a reduced end-diastolic fiber length impairs the stroke volume of a failing heart. Circulatory stasis also aggravates peripheral and pulmonary edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stroke volume rises with end-diastolic fiber length up to a limit. COMLEX: thoracic cage restriction hinders the respiratory pump that supports venous return.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — respiratory pump and negative intrathoracic pressure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the pathophysiologic mechanisms by which spinal cord facilitation, increased sympathetic output, and thoracic cage motion restrictions contribute to impaired cardiovascular function and altered baroreflex responses in patients with heart failure.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, a physician palpates the cranial rhythmic impulse in a healthy patient. Which rhythm does the course say it corresponds to?","choices":{"A":"The respiratory sinus arrhythmia pattern","B":"The Cheyne–Stokes breathing pattern","C":"The circadian sleep-wake rhythm of the body","D":"The Traube–Hering–Mayer oscillation","E":"The arterial pulse pressure waveform"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under physiologic conditions the baroreflex modulates autonomic tone in a fluctuating pattern known as the Traube–Hering–Mayer oscillation, which the course says corresponds to the palpated cranial rhythmic impulse. In heart failure this baroreflex control is severely impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: baroreflex modulation of autonomic tone produces slow blood pressure oscillations. COMLEX: the course equates this oscillation with the palpated cranial rhythmic impulse.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — baroreflex sensitivity and autonomic tone in cardiovascular disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with cardiovascular somatic dysfunction is treated with Johnston's three-segment approach. Which description of the treatment steps is correct?","choices":{"A":"Engage the restrictive barrier in each plane, deliver a thrust at end-exhalation, and then re-examine","B":"Move to the point of greatest bind in each plane, hold the breath for 90 seconds at that point, and then re-examine","C":"Apply traction at the central segment, resist the patient's inhalation for 10 seconds, and then re-examine","D":"Isolate the central segment, ask the patient to contract the muscle hard for 20 seconds, and then re-examine","E":"Stack the planes of ease, hold the breath 3 to 5 seconds in the most relaxing phase, and then re-examine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The clinician monitors the central dysfunctional segment, stacks each motion pair in the direction of greatest ease, and asks the patient to inhale or exhale deeply in the phase that maximizes tissue relaxation and to hold the breath for 3 to 5 seconds. The patient is then returned to neutral and re-examined.","boardPrep":"Step 1: indirect techniques move the tissue away from the barrier. COMLEX: the course lists horizontal translation, axial traction and compression, and the respiratory phase as the translatory pairs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — mechanics of indirect and myofascial techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with heart failure and edema is treated with thoracic inlet myofascial release. Which set-up is correct?","choices":{"A":"Supine, with the clinician at the side of the table and the hands over the lower costal margins","B":"Supine, with the clinician at the head and the hands across the cervicothoracic junction","C":"Prone, with the clinician at the foot of the table and the hands beneath the sacrum","D":"Seated, with the clinician at the side of the table and the hands over the lumbar spine","E":"Supine, with the clinician at the foot of the table and the hands over the iliac crests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For thoracic inlet myofascial release the patient is supine and the clinician stands at the head of the table, placing the hands across the cervicothoracic junction to evaluate the transverse fascial planes of the clavicles, first ribs, and T1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic inlet is formed by T1, the first ribs, and the manubrium. COMLEX: freeing the inlet fascia optimizes lymphatic drainage into the subclavian veins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — thoracic inlet restriction and lymphatic drainage","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate the execution of indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques (such as Johnston’s three-segment functional approach, thoracic inlet release, or thoracoabdominal diaphragm release) for a patient presenting with cardiovascular-related somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with heart failure receives thoracoabdominal diaphragm release. What is the intended physiologic goal?","choices":{"A":"Raising intra-abdominal pressure to reduce venous return to the right atrium","B":"Stretching the pericardium to reduce afterload directly through the sympathetic chain","C":"Immobilizing the diaphragm to lower the work of breathing during inspiration","D":"Restoring diaphragmatic excursion to improve pressure gradients and fluid return","E":"Compressing the lungs to clear pulmonary edema through the airways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Restoring diaphragmatic excursion maximizes intrathoracic pressure gradients and enhances low-pressure fluid return. The patient holds the breath in exhalation to tolerance while the clinician introduces rotation, translation, and tilt toward ease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thorax and abdomen act as a two-chambered pressure pump. COMLEX: improved venous and lymphatic return optimizes preload and reduces congestion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — diaphragm and the two-chambered pressure pump","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has bilateral dependent pitting edema of the ankles, orthopnea, elevated jugular venous distention, an S3 gallop, and viscerosomatic dysfunction at T2 to T3 on the left. His serum creatinine and BMI are normal. Which cause is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Congestive heart failure","B":"Chronic renal failure","C":"Cirrhosis with portal hypertension","D":"Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","E":"Chronic venous insufficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Congestive heart failure produces bilateral dependent pitting edema, orthopnea, elevated JVD, an S3 or S4 gallop, and viscerosomatic dysfunction clustered at T1 to T5 on the left, especially T2 and T3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JVD and an S3 gallop reflect volume overload of a failing ventricle. COMLEX: left-sided T1 to T5 findings support a cardiac origin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — T1-T5 left findings in cardiac viscerosomatic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with a BMI of 42 has severe peripheral edema, dyspnea, daytime hypercapnia, somnolence, and polycythemia. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":{"A":"Congestive left heart failure","B":"Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","C":"Chronic renal failure with uremia","D":"Cirrhosis with portal hypertension","E":"Left-sided valvular heart disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Obesity hypoventilation syndrome causes severe peripheral edema and dyspnea from pulmonary hypertension and secondary right heart failure, with morbid obesity, daytime hypercapnia, somnolence, polycythemia, and severe mechanical chest wall restriction without primary left ventricular muscle failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic hypoventilation causes hypercapnia and pulmonary vasoconstriction. COMLEX: severe chest wall restriction is a clue to the mechanical contribution.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — thoracic cage mechanics and respiratory function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between linked vs. non-linked costal dysfunctions and distinguish cardiac causes of peripheral edema/dyspnea from non-cardiac etiology (such as renal failure, liver failure, or obesity hypoventilation syndrome) using clinical examination findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with hypertension has vertebral sidebending restricted to the left at T2. The T2 rib shows costal sidebending restriction to the left. Which interpretation fits the course's description?","choices":{"A":"Non-linked costal dysfunction, suggesting a primary visceral or autonomic origin","B":"Non-linked costal dysfunction, suggesting a purely somatic mechanical origin","C":"Linked costal dysfunction, suggesting a purely somatic mechanical origin","D":"Linked costal dysfunction, suggesting a primary renal origin","E":"Linked costal dysfunction, suggesting a primary visceral or autonomic origin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A linked costal dysfunction occurs when the costal element behaves in the same direction as the corresponding vertebral segment, and this linkage is characteristic of primary visceral or autonomic dysfunction, commonly at C6, T2, and T6 in cardiovascular disease or hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic output to the heart arises from T1 to T6. COMLEX: the course treats a non-linked, mirror-image costal element as a purely somatic mechanical dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — costal linkage versus mirror-image mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with hypertension smokes and has low HDL cholesterol. He has AV nicking on funduscopy and an elevated serum creatinine. Which two findings are markers of end-organ damage?","choices":{"A":"Smoking and low HDL cholesterol","B":"AV nicking and elevated creatinine","C":"Male sex and age over 60 years","D":"Smoking and elevated creatinine","E":"Low HDL cholesterol and AV nicking"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Funduscopic AV nicking and elevated serum creatinine are markers of end-organ damage. Smoking, low HDL cholesterol, male sex, and age over 60 years are cardiovascular risk factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertensive retinopathy and renal injury reflect chronic pressure damage. COMLEX: end-organ damage supports combining drugs with adjunctive OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — risk assessment before combining pharmacotherapy and OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with hypertension asks why her physician combines an ACE inhibitor with OMT. Which explanation fits the course?","choices":{"A":"Drugs target mechanical restriction while OMT targets neurohormonal pathways and the renin system","B":"OMT replaces the drug once the thoracic spine is treated and the facilitation has resolved","C":"Drugs target biochemical pathways while OMT targets neuromusculoskeletal drivers of tone","D":"Drugs target fascial restriction while OMT targets volume overload and aldosterone release","E":"OMT is required first because drugs cannot lower afterload while somatic dysfunction persists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Standard pharmacotherapy targets biochemical pathways to lower blood pressure, inhibit neurohormonal remodeling, and reduce volume overload. Targeted adjunctive OMT addresses the neuromusculoskeletal drivers of autonomic tone and fluid stasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors reduce afterload by blocking angiotensin II formation. COMLEX: OMT is an adjunct and never replaces standard pharmacotherapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — mandatory role of standard pharmacotherapy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Assess a patient’s cardiovascular risk profile, end-organ damage, and autonomic tone balance to justify combining standard pharmacotherapy with targeted adjunctive Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment (OMT).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has impaired parasympathetic tone. Which effect on baroreflex sensitivity and heart rate variability is expected?","choices":{"A":"Increased baroreflex sensitivity and increased heart rate variability","B":"Increased baroreflex sensitivity and reduced heart rate variability","C":"Reduced baroreflex sensitivity and greater beat-to-beat variability","D":"Reduced baroreflex sensitivity and reduced heart rate variability","E":"Unchanged baroreflex sensitivity and enhanced heart rate variability"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Impaired parasympathetic or vagal tone reduces baroreflex sensitivity and heart rate variability, while chronic sympathicotonia raises afterload, accelerates heart rate, and promotes adverse remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal tone slows the heart and supports beat-to-beat variability. COMLEX: upper cervical OMT is aimed at normalizing vagal outflow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — parasympathetic outflow and upper cervical OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old non-Black man with hypertension and diabetes asks for a plan that follows the course's JNC 8-guided approach with a structured OMT sequence. Which plan fits?","choices":{"A":"A DASH diet with sodium restriction, a loop diuretic, and bilateral rib raising","B":"A DASH diet with sodium restriction, an alpha-blocker, and a high-velocity cervical thrust","C":"A DASH diet with sodium restriction, an ACE inhibitor, and bilateral rib raising","D":"A DASH diet with sodium restriction, a nitrate, and a high-velocity thoracic thrust","E":"A DASH diet with sodium restriction, an aldosterone antagonist, and a sustained cranial thrust"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lifestyle modification includes the DASH diet with sodium restriction below 2,000 mg per day. Patients with diabetes or chronic kidney disease should include an ACE inhibitor or ARB, and the course's OMT sequence includes bilateral rib raising.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors and ARBs protect renal function in diabetes. COMLEX: OMT is added to standard medical therapy and does not replace it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 6 — integrating medical therapy and OMT in heart failure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with decompensated heart failure is to receive a thoracic lymphatic pump. What precaution does the course give?","choices":{"A":"Apply it rapidly to clear the pulmonary congestion before the next diuretic dose is due","B":"Apply it forcefully to increase the fluid return to the right ventricle quickly","C":"Apply it at once to both thorax and legs to shift the extremity fluid centrally","D":"Avoid the pump entirely because any lymph movement worsens heart failure","E":"Apply it gradually to avoid overwhelming the right ventricle with sudden fluid return"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In decompensated CHF, lymphatic pumps must be administered gradually and gently because a sudden return of fluid can overwhelm a compromised, inefficient right ventricle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a compromised right ventricle tolerates sudden volume shifts poorly. COMLEX: slow, oscillatory pumps are the form the course uses in cardiac patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 6 — cautions for lymphatic pumps in CHF","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl20_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Design an integrative therapeutic treatment plan for an OMS-II clinical case scenario that combines JNC 8-guided lifestyle and pharmacologic interventions with a structured OMT sequence (e.g., rib raising, lymphatic pumps, and suboccipital/cervical release) to optimize hemodynamics and reduce myocardial workload.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with hypertension has OA and C2 somatic dysfunction. According to the course, why does the plan include suboccipital release?","choices":{"A":"It reduces tension on the vagus nerve at the jugular foramen and the superior cervical ganglion","B":"It reduces tension on the phrenic nerve as it leaves the cervical spine and on the celiac ganglion","C":"It reduces tension on the glossopharyngeal nerve at the foramen ovale and on the stellate ganglion","D":"It reduces tension on the trigeminal nerve at the foramen rotundum and on the sphenopalatine ganglion","E":"It reduces tension on the splanchnic nerves at the diaphragm and on the inferior mesenteric ganglion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Suboccipital and upper cervical release addresses OA and C2 dysfunction to reduce mechanical tension on the vagus nerve exiting the jugular foramen and on the superior cervical ganglion, which stabilizes heart rate and improves baroreflex sensitivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve exits the skull through the jugular foramen. COMLEX: normalizing vagal tone slows excessive heart rate and improves baroreflex sensitivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — upper cervical dysfunction and autonomic balance","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Cardiology & Thoracic OMT Lab","questions":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where do somatic dysfunction and tissue texture changes of cardiac disease cluster?","choices":{"A":"T6 to T10 on the right","B":"T10 to L2 on the left","C":"C3 to C5 on the right","D":"T1 to T5 on the left","E":"L1 to L3 bilaterally"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course describes consistent somatic dysfunction and tissue texture change centered at T1 to T5 on the left, matching the T1 to T6 preganglionic sympathetic supply of the heart. More than 70 percent of cardiac somatic dysfunctions fall between T1 and T5 on the left.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cardiac viscerosomatic findings are upper thoracic and left sided. Step 1: segmental sympathetic outflow to the heart is T1 to T6.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where is the anterior Chapman's reflex point for the heart?","choices":{"A":"Second intercostal space near the sternum","B":"Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","C":"Seventh intercostal space near the xiphoid process","D":"Third intercostal space at the posterior axillary line","E":"Tip of the eleventh rib"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course places the anterior Chapman's reflex point for the heart in the second intercostal space near the sternum. Palpation finds a tender, pea-sized, ganglioform fascial contraction that signals cardiac stress.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman points are anterior or posterior reflex zones that mirror visceral stress. Step 1: they are tender fascial contractions, not trigger points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which levels show the highest frequency of somatic dysfunction in cardiac disease?","choices":{"A":"T7 and T8 on the left","B":"T4 and T5 on the right","C":"T9 and T10 on the right","D":"T11 and T12 on the left","E":"T2 and T3 on the left"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The highest frequency of cardiac-related somatic dysfunction occurs at T2 and T3 on the left.","boardPrep":"Step 1: preganglionic cardiac sympathetics arise from T1 to T6. COMLEX: expect left upper thoracic dysfunction with cardiac stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the thoracic lab rubric, for which sagittal pattern is prone Texas twist HVLA at T3 to T8 not appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Flexion dysfunction","B":"Neutral dysfunction","C":"Flexion dysfunction with right rotation","D":"Extension dysfunction","E":"Neutral dysfunction with left sidebending"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric states that prone Texas twist at T3 to T8 is appropriate for flexion and neutral dysfunctions and not for an extension dysfunction. The same restriction is written for prone upper thoracic HVLA at T1 to T4.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: match the technique to the sagittal pattern. The two prone thoracic HVLA techniques in the rubric are written for flexion or neutral dysfunctions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which condition is an absolute contraindication to direct HVLA thrust techniques?","choices":{"A":"Chronic paraspinal muscle hypertonicity","B":"Restricted rib motion without pain","C":"Bony metastasis in the target segment","D":"Mild age-related thoracic kyphosis","E":"Segmental sidebending restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists bony metastasis in the target segment among the absolute contraindications to direct, high-force techniques, because the weakened vertebral architecture risks pathological fracture. Other absolute entries include acute fracture, unstable patients, osteomyelitis, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and cauda equina syndrome.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: HVLA over a structurally weakened segment risks pathological fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the main risk of high-force HVLA that compresses the thoracic cage in severe osteoporosis?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic outlet compression, plexopathy, and arm numbness","B":"Sacroiliac strain, piriformis spasm, and sciatic pain","C":"Costal cartilage hypertrophy, kyphosis, and pleural thickening","D":"Diaphragmatic paralysis, phrenic neuropraxia, and dyspnea","E":"Costochondral, costovertebral, and midshaft rib fractures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that high-force procedures applying downward compression on the thoracic cage in the prone or supine position carry a high risk of costochondral, costovertebral, and midshaft rib fractures in severe osteoporosis, so they must be avoided.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: poor bone density makes thoracic cage compression during HVLA a fracture risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":1,"stem":"Which treatment position does the thoracic lab rubric give for anterior tender points AT3 to AT6?","choices":{"A":"Extension with the patient's arms hanging at the side","B":"Flexion with the patient's hands behind the head","C":"Extension with the patient's hands behind the head","D":"Flexion with the patient's arms hanging at the side","E":"Flexion with the patient seated and the arms crossed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric treats AT3 to AT6 in flexion with the patient supine and the arms hanging at the side. AT1 to AT2 are also treated in flexion but with the hands behind the head.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the arm position differs between AT1 to AT2 and AT3 to AT6.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the thoracic lab rubric, how long is the counterstrain position maintained once tenderness has dropped?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 15 seconds","B":"Approximately 30 seconds","C":"Approximately 5 minutes","D":"Approximately 10 minutes","E":"Approximately 90 seconds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After fine-tuning to roughly 70 percent or greater reduction in tenderness, the rubric has the physician maintain the position for approximately 90 seconds while monitoring the tender point, the therapeutic pulse, before slowly returning to neutral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: counterstrain is a passive indirect technique held about 90 seconds. Return slowly to neutral, then reassess.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":1,"stem":"What treatment position does the thoracic lab rubric give for posterior midline tender points PT1 to PT12?","choices":{"A":"Flexion, localized by flexing the head and neck","B":"Extension, localized by extending the head and neck","C":"Flexion, localized by a pillow beneath the chest","D":"Neutral, localized by sidebending the head and neck","E":"Neutral, localized by rotating the trunk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists posterior midline thoracic tender points as extension. The patient is prone and the head and neck are extended to localize extension to the involved thoracic segment.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: prone posterior points are treated with the head and neck extended to localize the segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"In seated post-isometric relaxation, how does the patient contract during the isometric phase?","choices":{"A":"Toward the position of freedom against physician resistance","B":"Toward the restrictive barrier against physician resistance","C":"Away from the position of freedom against physician resistance","D":"Toward the position of freedom with no physician resistance","E":"Toward the restrictive barrier while the physician follows the motion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric states that in post-isometric relaxation the patient contracts toward the position of freedom against an equal and opposite counterforce from the physician.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: PIR uses a gentle isometric contraction toward the position of freedom, followed by relaxation and a stretch to the new barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington’s law of reciprocal inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the thoracic lab rubric, how long is the isometric contraction held in seated PIR?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10 to 15 seconds","B":"Approximately 30 to 60 seconds","C":"Approximately 3 to 5 seconds","D":"Approximately 90 seconds","E":"Approximately 5 minutes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric has the patient hold the isometric contraction for approximately 3 to 5 seconds against an equal and opposite counterforce, then relax completely before the physician advances to the new barrier.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: PIR contractions are brief and gentle, followed by relaxation and a stretch to the new barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington’s law of reciprocal inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the physician do immediately after the patient relaxes in seated post-isometric relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Return the patient to neutral and reassess before any repeat","B":"Advance the patient into the newly available barrier","C":"Maintain the position for 90 seconds while monitoring the point","D":"Apply a short thrust at the end of exhalation","E":"Hold the barrier and ask for a stronger contraction at once"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the patient relaxes completely, the rubric has the physician advance the patient into the newly available motion barrier. The sequence is repeated at least three times before the thoracic dysfunction is reassessed.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: each PIR cycle ends by taking up the slack at the new barrier. The cycle is repeated at least three times.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington’s law of reciprocal inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":1,"stem":"At what point in the respiratory cycle is the thrust delivered in the supine Kirksville crunch?","choices":{"A":"At the end of exhalation","B":"At the end of inhalation","C":"At the start of inhalation","D":"At peak inhalation with the breath held","E":"During quiet mid-cycle breathing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric instructs the patient to inhale and then exhale, and the controlled downward force is applied at the end of exhalation. The thrust is localized to the dysfunctional segment.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric times each thoracic HVLA technique to end-exhalation, after the patient inhales and then exhales.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — rib treatment approaches including post-isometric relaxation and HVLA","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the physician stand for the supine thenar fulcrum technique called the Kirksville crunch?","choices":{"A":"At the head of the table facing the patient's feet","B":"Opposite the posteriorly rotated transverse process","C":"Behind the seated patient, who holds the arms crossed","D":"At the foot of the table facing the patient's head","E":"Straddling the patient with one knee on the table"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric places the physician on the side opposite the posteriorly rotated transverse process, with the patient supine and the arms crossed over the chest. The posterior transverse process rests on the thenar eminence of the fulcrum hand.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the Kirksville crunch treats Type I and Type II thoracic dysfunction, using the thenar eminence as the fulcrum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — rib treatment approaches including post-isometric relaxation and HVLA","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":1,"stem":"In prone upper thoracic HVLA at T1 to T4, through which contact is the thrust delivered?","choices":{"A":"The anchor hand on the anterior transverse process, accentuating extension","B":"The forearms on the patient's shoulders, accentuating flexion","C":"The hand on the head, accentuating rotation","D":"The crossed arms toward the thenar eminence, accentuating rotation","E":"The hand on the sacrum, accentuating sidebending"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the prone upper thoracic technique the inferior hand anchors the anterior transverse process and the superior hand sits over the occipitoparietal region. At end-exhalation the thrust passes through the hand on the head, accentuating rotation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: T1 to T4 prone HVLA is a head-hand thrust. It is appropriate for flexion or neutral dysfunctions, not extension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — rib treatment approaches including post-isometric relaxation and HVLA","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has a T2 segment with sidebending restriction to the left in both the vertebral and costal elements. According to the course, which interpretation fits?","choices":{"A":"Pure somatic dysfunction with mirror-image costal motion","B":"Viscerosomatic cardiac dysfunction with costal linkage","C":"Pulmonary parenchymal disease with fixed costal motion","D":"Gastric viscerosomatic reflex with absent costal motion","E":"Pure costal dysfunction without a vertebral component"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes costal linkage in cardiac viscerosomatic dysfunction, where the rib behaves in the same direction as the vertebra. Pure somatic dysfunction shows the rib moving as a mirror image of the vertebral segment.","boardPrep":"Course: costal linkage points toward a visceral source, and mirror-image rib and vertebral motion points toward a somatic problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — linked versus non-linked costal dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man is evaluated for suspected acute myocardial infarction. According to the course, which sites are common referral sites for viscerosomatic pain from it?","choices":{"A":"Right flank, right hip, and the inguinal groin","B":"Suprapubic region, medial thigh, and the perineal area","C":"Right scapula, right shoulder tip, and the umbilicus","D":"Left flank, left iliac fossa, and the testis","E":"Substernal chest, left arm and shoulder, and left jaw"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists the substernal anterior chest wall, left arm and shoulder, left side of the jaw, epigastric region, left ribs, and upper back as referral sites for ischemic myocardial pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral and somatic afferents converge on dorsal horn interneurons, so the brain reads cardiac pain as arising from the left upper somatic dermatomes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — sympathetic origin and somatic referral sites for myocardial infarction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correctly identify and localize thoracic somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with cardiac dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has well-localized sharp left-sided pain that is reproduced by palpation of the anterior thorax and by trunk rotation, with no diaphoresis or dyspnea. According to the course, which pain type fits?","choices":{"A":"Myocardial ischemic pain with referral to T2","B":"Reflux pain referred to the lower sternum","C":"Noncardiac musculoskeletal chest wall pain","D":"Pleuritic pain from an inflamed pleura","E":"Cardiac pain relayed through the dorsal horn"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes noncardiac musculoskeletal pain as well localized, sharp or achy, reproducible by palpation of local muscles or joints or by trunk motion, and lacking systemic autonomic signs. Cardiac pain is deep, poorly localized, and not provoked by palpation of the chest wall.","boardPrep":"Course: pain reproduced by palpation or motion favors a musculoskeletal source, though it does not exclude cardiac disease. Autonomic signs such as diaphoresis and nausea favor a cardiac source.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pain quality and behavior","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with severe osteoporosis has a thoracic somatic dysfunction at T5. According to the course, which plan is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Gentle indirect OMT such as counterstrain or myofascial release","B":"Prone HVLA with downward compression to restore thoracic motion","C":"Supine thenar fulcrum HVLA with a firm thrust through crossed arms","D":"Forceful high-amplitude rib springing to stretch the thoracic cage","E":"Withholding any OMT because osteoporosis is an absolute contraindication"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists severe osteoporosis as a relative contraindication to direct, high-force techniques that compress the thoracic cage. Safe management uses gentle indirect OMT such as strain-counterstrain, myofascial release, or balanced ligamentous tension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a relative contraindication changes the technique, not the decision to treat. Switch from direct HVLA to indirect OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — relative versus absolute contraindications in older adults","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with prostate cancer has imaging showing a bony metastasis at T6, which also has a flexion somatic dysfunction. According to the course, what is the appropriate decision about prone HVLA at T6?","choices":{"A":"Proceed because flexion dysfunctions suit the Texas twist technique","B":"Proceed with a smaller thrust amplitude at the end of inhalation","C":"Proceed with the Kirksville crunch in place of the prone technique","D":"Proceed after the patient rates the pain below 3 out of 10","E":"Withhold it because manual force risks a pathological fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists bony metastasis in the target segment as an absolute contraindication to direct thrust techniques, because it weakens the vertebral architecture and makes pathological fracture a high risk. The appropriate sagittal pattern does not override this.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: an absolute contraindication outweighs a favorable technique match. Both prone and supine thrusts load the same segment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — absolute contraindications to direct techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize indications for thoracic OMT and discuss contraindications and precautions for HVLA and muscle energy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has an extension Type II dysfunction at T6. According to the thoracic lab rubric, which HVLA technique is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Prone Texas twist for T3 to T8","B":"Prone upper thoracic HVLA for T1 to T4","C":"Supine thenar fulcrum Kirksville crunch","D":"Seated thrust through crossed arms at end-inhalation","E":"Prone thenar fulcrum with the head rotated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric excludes extension dysfunction from the prone Texas twist and prone upper thoracic HVLA. The supine thenar fulcrum Kirksville crunch is written for Type I and Type II thoracic dysfunction and carries no such exclusion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pick the thrust by sagittal pattern. Extension dysfunctions avoid the prone thoracic thrusts in the lab rubric.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — selecting rib and thoracic HVLA by dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":2,"stem":"A prone patient has a tender point on the posterior T6 transverse process. According to the thoracic lab rubric, which treatment position fits?","choices":{"A":"Extension, sidebending away, and rotation away","B":"Extension, sidebending toward, rotation toward","C":"Flexion, sidebending away, rotation toward","D":"Extension, sidebending away, rotation toward","E":"Extension with sidebending and rotation toward"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric treats posterior transverse process tender points at PT1 to PT9 with extension, sidebending away, and rotation toward, written E SART. Inferolateral spinous process points use extension with sidebending and rotation both away, written ESARA.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in the position-of-ease shorthand, S and R each take T for toward or A for away, so SART is sidebending away and rotation toward.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — indirect positioning toward ease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":2,"stem":"A prone patient has a tender point at the T11 transverse process. According to the thoracic lab rubric, how is a lower thoracic posterior point positioned?","choices":{"A":"The axilla on the involved side is supported","B":"A pillow is placed beneath the upper thoracic spine","C":"The patient's hands are placed behind the head","D":"The pelvis on the side of the point is elevated","E":"The patient is turned supine with the arms hanging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For posterior counterstrain the rubric positions the patient prone, and for lower thoracic points the physician supports the axilla on the side of the tender point as appropriate.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: posterior thoracic counterstrain is performed prone. Lower thoracic points use axillary support on the side of the point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — patient positioning for indirect treatment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate application of thoracic counterstrain, including identification and treatment of thoracic tender points.","batch":2,"stem":"A tender point is found on the anterior lateral chest wall at the level of T8. According to the thoracic lab rubric, which treatment position fits?","choices":{"A":"Seated with extension, sidebending toward and rotation away","B":"Seated with flexion, sidebending toward and rotation away","C":"Seated with flexion, sidebending away and rotation away","D":"Supine with the arms at the side in flexion","E":"Prone with extension, sidebending and rotation toward"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists AT7 to AT9 as flexion with sidebending toward and rotation away, written F STRA, with the patient seated. AT9 to AT12 use the same pattern with the patient supine.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: lateral anterior thoracic points use F STRA, so sidebending toward and rotation away. Midline AT1 to AT6 points use flexion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — patient positioning for indirect treatment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, what does Sherrington's law of reciprocal inhibition state?","choices":{"A":"When a prime mover contracts, its direct antagonist contracts in synergy","B":"When a muscle is stretched, its own motor neurons are inhibited","C":"When an agonist fatigues, the antagonist increases its strength","D":"When a prime mover contracts, its antagonist is inhibited and relaxes","E":"When a muscle relaxes, its antagonist is facilitated to contract"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that when a prime mover contracts or becomes chronically hypertonic, spinal cord interneurons inhibit and relax its direct functional antagonist. This is the basis of reciprocal inhibition.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reciprocal inhibition is a spinal reflex that uses the agonist contraction to relax the antagonist.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — reciprocal inhibition in crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with upper crossed syndrome has chronically hypertonic pectoral muscles and weak rhomboids. According to the course, which mechanism weakens the rhomboids?","choices":{"A":"Compression of the dorsal scapular nerve by the shortened and hypertonic pectoral muscles","B":"Hypertrophy of the rhomboids that leads to early fatigue and failure","C":"Ia afferent signals through spinal interneurons inhibit rhomboid motor neurons","D":"Loss of dorsal horn facilitation that reduces motor output to the scapula","E":"Vascular insufficiency of the rhomboids from sustained pectoral tension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course explains that hypertonic pectoral muscles send continuous Ia afferent signals through spinal interneurons to motor neurons in the spinal cord, shutting down and weakening the rhomboids and the middle and lower trapezius. This is reciprocal inhibition.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: tight muscles inhibit their antagonists. Treating the hypertonic muscle can restore recruitment of the weak one.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Janda upper crossed syndrome mechanism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of thoracic muscle energy, including post-isometric relaxation and reciprocal inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"Which set-up matches the thoracic lab rubric for seated post-isometric relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Patient seated with feet flat, physician behind monitoring the involved transverse process","B":"Patient seated with legs crossed, physician in front with a monitoring finger on the spinous process","C":"Patient supine with knees flexed, physician at the side with a monitoring finger on the rib angle","D":"Patient prone with a pillow beneath the chest, physician at the head with a hand on the occiput","E":"Patient seated with feet flat, physician behind monitoring the sacral base"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric seats the patient with the feet flat on the floor, with the physician standing behind. A monitoring finger is placed on the involved transverse process and the opposite forearm or hand rests on the patient's shoulder, which is used to bring the torso to the feather's edge of each barrier.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: monitor the involved transverse process while the shoulder contact positions the torso into the barriers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — set-up for post-isometric relaxation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":2,"stem":"A prone patient has a right-rotated T5 flexion dysfunction and will be treated with the Texas twist. According to the thoracic lab rubric, where does the physician stand?","choices":{"A":"On the patient's right, beside the rotation","B":"At the head of the table, opposite the rotation","C":"At the foot of the table, beside the rotation","D":"Behind the patient, who is sitting upright","E":"On the patient's left, opposite the rotation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric places the physician on the side opposite the patient's rotation in the prone Texas twist, and gives the example that a right-rotated dysfunction is treated from the patient's left. The thrust is delivered at the end of exhalation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: stand opposite the rotation for prone T3 to T8 HVLA. T5 flexion is an appropriate pattern for this thrust.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — operator positioning for HVLA","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":2,"stem":"A prone patient has a flexion dysfunction at T2. According to the thoracic lab rubric, which sequence describes the HVLA?","choices":{"A":"Rotate the head away from the barrier, sidebend it away, then thrust through the head hand at end-inhalation","B":"Rotate the head toward the barrier, sidebend it into the barrier, then thrust through the anchor hand at end-exhalation","C":"Rotate the head toward the barrier, extend the neck fully, then thrust through the head hand at end-inhalation","D":"Rotate the head toward the barrier, sidebend it into the barrier, then thrust through the head hand at end-exhalation","E":"Keep the head neutral, flex the neck, then thrust through the anchor hand at end-exhalation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For prone upper thoracic HVLA at T1 to T4 the rubric has the physician at the head of the table, rotating the head and neck toward the side of the barrier and sidebending the head into the barrier until the segment is localized. The thrust is short, rapid, and low amplitude through the hand on the head at end-exhalation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: T1 to T4 prone HVLA uses head positioning to localize, then a head-hand thrust at end-exhalation. It suits flexion or neutral dysfunctions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — localizing a segment before the thrust","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Demonstrate application of prone and supine thoracic HVLA.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a Type II dysfunction with a right posteriorly rotated T7 transverse process. According to the thoracic lab rubric, how is the Kirksville crunch set up?","choices":{"A":"Physician stands on the right and sidebends the patient toward the right transverse process","B":"Physician stands on the left and sidebends the patient away from the right transverse process","C":"Physician stands on the left and sidebends the patient toward the right transverse process","D":"Physician stands on the right and sidebends the patient away from the left transverse process","E":"Physician stands at the head and sidebends the patient toward the left transverse process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric places the physician on the side opposite the posteriorly rotated transverse process, here the left, with the patient supine and the arms crossed over the chest. The patient is sidebent toward the affected segment and away from the posterior transverse process, which sits on the thenar eminence of the fulcrum hand.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the posterior transverse process is the fulcrum contact and the physician stands opposite it. The thrust is applied at end-exhalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — supine HVLA setup","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Pulmonology 1","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Boyle's law, what happens to intrathoracic pressure as thoracic volume increases during inhalation?","choices":{"A":"It becomes more positive relative to atmospheric pressure","B":"It stays constant while airway resistance falls in the bronchi","C":"It equalizes with abdominal pressure as the diaphragm descends","D":"It becomes positive in the pleural space but not in the alveoli","E":"It becomes more negative relative to atmospheric pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pressure and volume share an inverse relationship. As thoracic volume increases during inhalation, intrathoracic and intrapleural pressures become more negative relative to atmospheric pressure, and this gradient draws air into the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inspiration is driven by negative intrathoracic pressure. COMLEX: diaphragm down plus ribs up increases thoracic volume.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which motion of the upper anterior ribs occurs during inhalation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral expansion in a bucket-handle motion","B":"Superior movement in a pump-handle motion","C":"Inferior movement in a pump-handle motion","D":"Posterior rotation with no change in rib height","E":"Medial compression with descent of the sternum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During inhalation the upper anterior ribs move superiorly in a pump-handle motion, while the lower lateral ribs expand laterally in a bucket-handle motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rib motion increases the anteroposterior and transverse thoracic dimensions. COMLEX: pump-handle motion is the upper rib pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement describes diaphragmatic excursion in normal ventilation?","choices":{"A":"About 0.5 to 1 cm in quiet breathing and 3 to 5 cm in deep breathing","B":"About 5 to 8 cm in quiet breathing and 15 to 20 cm in deep breathing","C":"About 1.5 to 4 cm in both quiet breathing and deep breathing","D":"About 1.5 to 4 cm in quiet breathing and 7 to 13 cm in deep breathing","E":"About 10 to 15 cm in quiet breathing and 20 to 25 cm in deep breathing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During quiet respiration the diaphragm descends about 1.5 to 4 cm, and during deep vital capacity breathing its excursion can reach 7 to 13 cm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm is the principal muscle of inspiration. COMLEX: restricted diaphragmatic travel limits ventilation and venous return.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which resistance is primarily increased in obstructive conditions such as asthma and COPD?","choices":{"A":"Airway resistance from bronchospasm and mucosal edema","B":"Parenchymal resistance from stiffened lung tissue","C":"Alveolar surface tension resistance from surfactant deficiency","D":"Vascular resistance from constriction of pulmonary arterioles","E":"Pleural resistance from friction between the pleural surfaces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Obstructive conditions primarily increase airway resistance through bronchospasm, mucosal edema, or airway collapse. Parenchymal resistance is the primary increase in restrictive disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstruction impairs expiratory flow. COMLEX: the work of breathing has airway, parenchymal, and tissue resistance components.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — the energy cost of breathing during respiratory infection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which resistance is primarily increased in restrictive conditions such as pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Airway resistance","B":"Pulmonary vascular resistance","C":"Parenchymal resistance","D":"Pleural resistance","E":"Surface tension resistance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Restrictive conditions primarily increase parenchymal resistance because the lung tissue is stiffened and noncompliant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibrosis lowers lung compliance. COMLEX: both obstructive and restrictive states also add tissue resistance across the chest wall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — the energy cost of breathing during respiratory infection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":1,"stem":"How does severe obstructive or restrictive lung disease affect the energy cost of breathing?","choices":{"A":"It falls because hyperinflation stores elastic energy in the chest wall","B":"It remains near baseline because accessory muscles share the workload","C":"It rises to about one-half of total metabolic energy expenditure","D":"It can rise to about one-third of total metabolic energy expenditure","E":"It rises in obstructive disease but falls in restrictive disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In severe disease the work of breathing can rise many-fold and consume up to about one-third of the body's total metabolic energy, which explains the activity limitation seen in pulmonary patients. Both obstructive and restrictive diseases increase the work of breathing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: increased resistance raises the respiratory muscle energy demand. COMLEX: improving thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics can reduce the mechanical burden.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — the energy cost of breathing during respiratory infection","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":1,"stem":"Which muscles are primary muscles of inspiration?","choices":{"A":"Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid","B":"Rectus abdominis and obliques","C":"Internal intercostals and transversus abdominis","D":"Rectus abdominis and scalenes","E":"Diaphragm and external intercostals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The principal muscles of inspiration are the diaphragm, the external intercostals, and the interchondral part of the internal intercostals. The scalenes are accessory muscles, and the abdominal wall muscles act in active expiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm does most of the work of quiet inspiration. COMLEX: the scalenes help elevate the upper ribs when the primary muscles struggle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":1,"stem":"Which muscles are accessory muscles of respiration?","choices":{"A":"Rectus abdominis, obliques, and psoas major","B":"Pectoralis major, deltoid, and biceps brachii","C":"Anterior, middle, and posterior scalenes","D":"Psoas major, iliacus, and gluteus maximus","E":"Latissimus dorsi, quadratus lumborum, and psoas major"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior, middle, and posterior scalenes are accessory muscles that forcefully elevate the upper ribs when the primary muscles tire. The diaphragm and intercostals are primary muscles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory muscles are recruited under load. COMLEX: scalene use rises when the primary muscles struggle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":1,"stem":"Which muscles produce active expiration?","choices":{"A":"External intercostals and the diaphragm muscle","B":"Internal intercostals and abdominal wall muscles","C":"Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid muscles of the neck","D":"Diaphragm and scalenes with the trapezius","E":"Serratus posterior superior and levator scapulae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Active expiration is produced by the internal intercostals and the abdominal wall musculature, which includes the rectus abdominis, obliques, and transversus abdominis. Quiet expiration is passive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: quiet expiration is passive elastic recoil. COMLEX: abdominal muscles assist when ventilation is forceful.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":1,"stem":"What causes the barrel chest deformity of chronic COPD?","choices":{"A":"Chronic lower lobe atelectasis that decreases the anteroposterior chest diameter","B":"Thoracic kyphosis from multiple vertebral compression fractures in the spine","C":"Chronic pleural effusion that expands the volume of the hemithorax","D":"Chronic air trapping and hyperinflation raising the anteroposterior diameter","E":"Hypertrophy of the pectoral muscles that widens the anterior chest wall"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic air trapping and alveolar hyperinflation keep the lungs and chest wall from returning to their normal resting position during exhalation, which permanently increases the AP chest diameter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: barrel chest reflects hyperinflation from air trapping. COMLEX: hyperinflation shortens the respiratory musculature and raises resting tone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":1,"stem":"In a hyperinflated COPD patient, what does contraction of the flattened diaphragm do to the lower rib margin during inspiration?","choices":{"A":"It pulls the margin inward as paradoxical motion called Hoover's sign","B":"It pushes the margin outward and exaggerates bucket-handle expansion of the chest","C":"It lifts the margin superiorly and exaggerates pump-handle motion of the ribs","D":"It leaves the margin still because the diaphragm has lost its contractility","E":"It rotates the margin posteriorly while the sternum moves inferiorly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperinflation flattens the diaphragmatic dome, so its fibers are horizontally displaced and contraction no longer expands the chest laterally. It instead pulls the lower costal margin inward during inspiration, which is paradoxical costal motion or Hoover's sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hoover's sign is a classic finding of hyperinflation. COMLEX: a flattened diaphragm cannot produce normal lateral expansion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair describes the adaptation of the respiratory musculature in chronic COPD?","choices":{"A":"Lengthened muscles with increased contractile force","B":"Hypotrophic muscles with normal resting tone","C":"Atrophic muscles with reduced oxygen demand","D":"Normal length muscles with reduced resting tone","E":"Shortened muscles with increased resting tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic hyperinflation contributes to shortened respiratory musculature and increased resting muscle tone, with reduced efficiency of respiratory muscle function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinflation holds the respiratory muscles shortened. COMLEX: shortened hypertonic respiratory muscles are an OMT target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":1,"stem":"Which effect is attributed to OMT rather than to pharmacotherapy in pulmonary disease?","choices":{"A":"Improving lung parenchymal compliance by reducing alveolar inflammation","B":"Restoring thoracic cage compliance by relieving myofascial tension","C":"Treating the infectious organism by direct bacterial killing","D":"Dilating bronchial smooth muscle through beta-adrenergic stimulation","E":"Reducing airway inflammation by suppressing the immune response"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pharmacotherapy can improve lung parenchymal compliance, but OMT directly restores thoracic cage compliance by relieving myofascial tension and joint restrictions across the spine, ribs, and sternum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pharmacologic therapy acts on the airway and parenchyma. COMLEX: OMT targets the chest wall and complements medical care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":1,"stem":"How does releasing shortened hypertonic respiratory muscles improve aerobic metabolism?","choices":{"A":"It restores efficient length-tension and lowers the oxygen cost of breathing","B":"It increases resting muscle tone and raises the oxygen cost of breathing","C":"It shortens the diaphragm further and reduces its excursion","D":"It lengthens the diaphragm to its maximum and increases the work of breathing","E":"It increases the airway resistance and spares the systemic metabolic energy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Releasing shortened, hypertonic respiratory muscles restores a more efficient resting length-tension relationship, which improves contraction efficiency, decreases the oxygen cost of breathing, and spares systemic metabolic energy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: efficient muscle mechanics lower oxygen demand. COMLEX: OMT may reduce the mechanical burden of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature of the terminal airways and alveoli distinguishes their fluid clearance from that of the upper airways?","choices":{"A":"Absence of any capillary blood supply","B":"Absence of resident immune cells","C":"Absence of the mucociliary escalator","D":"Direct drainage into the thoracic duct","E":"Clearance by negative pleural pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The mucociliary escalator does not extend to the terminal airways and alveoli, so their fluid clearance relies on the pulmonary lymphatics. Respiratory excursion pumps pulmonary lymph, so restoring diaphragmatic travel and thoracic mobility improves drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ciliated epithelium clears the large airways. COMLEX: lymphatic techniques aim to improve fluid return.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman breathes in quietly at rest. Which sequence generates airflow into the lungs?","choices":{"A":"The diaphragm descends and the ribs depress, thoracic volume rises, and intrathoracic pressure becomes positive","B":"The diaphragm descends and the ribs elevate, thoracic volume rises, and intrathoracic pressure falls","C":"Abdominal wall contraction lifts the diaphragm and compresses the thorax, which lowers intrathoracic pressure","D":"The diaphragm relaxes upward and the ribs elevate, thoracic volume falls, and intrathoracic pressure rises","E":"The diaphragm descends and the ribs elevate, thoracic volume falls, and the alveolar pressure becomes positive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inspiration is produced when the diaphragm contracts and moves downward and the ribs elevate and expand, which increases thoracic volume and lowers intrathoracic pressure so that air flows into the lungs. The lung is a passive structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diaphragm down plus ribs up raises thoracic volume and lowers pressure. COMLEX: ventilation depends on skeletal muscle action on the thorax.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — pressure gradients and lymphatic flow in respiratory mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A student states that the lungs themselves actively expand to draw air in. Which statement corrects this?","choices":{"A":"The parenchyma is passive and skeletal muscle action on the thorax drives air exchange","B":"The parenchyma contracts rhythmically and skeletal muscle assists at high flow rates","C":"Smooth muscle in the alveolar walls drives inspiration while the diaphragm supports expiration","D":"The visceral pleura actively pumps air through the airways by ciliary motion","E":"Elastic recoil of the parenchyma generates the inspiratory flow and relaxes during exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lung parenchyma is a passive tissue structure. Air exchange is driven by skeletal muscles acting on the osseous thorax, which changes intrathoracic volume and therefore pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lungs follow the chest wall. COMLEX: thoracic somatic dysfunction can limit ventilation even when the parenchyma is healthy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — pressure gradients and lymphatic flow in respiratory mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the role of thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation and the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has restricted diaphragmatic excursion from chronic upper abdominal surgery adhesions. Which hemodynamic and fluid effect is expected?","choices":{"A":"Stronger negative intrathoracic pressure that increases central venous and lymphatic return","B":"Higher intrathoracic pressure that increases venous return through the inferior vena cava","C":"Unchanged venous return because lymph flows by gravity into the subclavian veins","D":"Weaker negative intrathoracic pressure that reduces central venous and lymphatic return","E":"Reduced intra-abdominal pressure that pulls lymph from the thorax into the abdomen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diaphragmatic descent lowers intrathoracic pressure, which draws venous blood and low-pressure lymph centrally. Restricted excursion weakens the negative intrathoracic pressure and reduces central venous and lymphatic return.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiratory excursion powers venous and lymphatic return. COMLEX: restoring diaphragmatic travel is a rationale for diaphragm release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — diaphragmatic release and venous and lymphatic return","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with an asthma exacerbation labors to breathe. Which mechanism raises her work of breathing?","choices":{"A":"Increased airway resistance from bronchospasm and mucosal edema","B":"Increased parenchymal resistance from stiffened lung tissue and fibrosis","C":"Increased pulmonary vascular resistance from hypoxic arteriolar constriction","D":"Decreased tissue resistance from loosened myofascial tension in the chest wall","E":"Decreased airway resistance from dilation of the bronchi and bronchioles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asthma is an obstructive condition that primarily increases airway resistance through bronchospasm and mucosal edema. Stiffened lung tissue is the restrictive mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: airway resistance rises in obstruction. COMLEX: the work of breathing is the sum of airway, parenchymal, and tissue resistance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — the energy cost of breathing","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has pulmonary fibrosis plus rib and spinal somatic dysfunction. Which component of the work of breathing do the somatic dysfunctions add?","choices":{"A":"Airway resistance from smooth muscle constriction that narrows the large bronchi","B":"Parenchymal resistance from alveolar scarring that stiffens the lung tissue","C":"Vascular resistance from pulmonary hypertension that raises right heart afterload","D":"Mucociliary resistance from impaired clearance that thickens the airway secretions","E":"Tissue resistance from chest wall myofascial tension lowering thoracic compliance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both obstructive and restrictive states increase tissue resistance across the chest wall, and somatic dysfunction and myofascial tension reduce thoracic cage compliance. Fibrosis itself raises parenchymal resistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chest wall compliance is separate from lung compliance. COMLEX: OMT can address the tissue resistance component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — soft-tissue and rib techniques that address chest wall restriction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how obstructive and restrictive pulmonary conditions increase the work of breathing and energy expenditure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with severe COPD becomes breathless walking to the mailbox. Which explanation best accounts for her activity limitation?","choices":{"A":"The oxygen cost of breathing is increased but offset by lowered cardiac energy demand","B":"The work of breathing consumes a large share of total metabolic energy","C":"The airway resistance is reduced, so the respiratory muscles contract against less load","D":"The energy drain is confined to the diaphragm and spares the accessory muscles of the neck","E":"The respiratory muscles shift to anaerobic work that eliminates the energy cost of breathing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In severe obstructive or restrictive disease the work of breathing can rise many-fold and consume up to one-third of the body's total metabolic energy. This large energy drain accounts for the activity limitation seen in pulmonary patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: increased respiratory work competes with exercising muscle for energy. COMLEX: improving mechanics may reduce the mechanical burden of breathing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — the energy cost of breathing","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with a COPD exacerbation sits upright with prominent, continuous scalene contraction at rest. Which interpretation fits?","choices":{"A":"Normal compensation for mild exertion that needs no further evaluation","B":"Improved diaphragmatic efficiency from chronic hyperinflation of the lungs","C":"Primary respiratory muscle fatigue with possible impending failure","D":"Cervical somatic dysfunction from prolonged neck flexion at a desk","E":"Respiratory alkalosis from hypocarbia with preserved muscle strength"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prominent, continuous accessory muscle use at rest indicates severe mechanical strain, markedly elevated work of breathing, and impending respiratory muscle exhaustion. It is a sign that the primary muscles are struggling to meet demand.","boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory muscle use signals that the primary muscles are struggling. COMLEX: it can foreshadow respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — the muscles that move the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with hyperinflated lungs needs the neck muscles to lift the upper chest on every breath. Why does his body recruit accessory muscles?","choices":{"A":"The primary muscles tire or operate at a mechanical disadvantage","B":"The primary muscles hypertrophy and no longer require assistance from neck muscles","C":"The accessory muscles are normally the first muscles to initiate quiet inspiration","D":"The diaphragm becomes more domed and contracts too forcefully for the ribs","E":"The scalenes relax to open the thoracic inlet and reduce airway resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In healthy resting states inspiration is accomplished mainly by the diaphragm. When the primary muscles tire or operate at a mechanical disadvantage, such as a flattened diaphragm, the body recruits accessory muscles to elevate the sternum and upper ribs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a flattened diaphragm is mechanically disadvantaged. COMLEX: hypertonic accessory muscles accompany hyperinflation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — the muscles that move the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the primary and accessory muscles of respiration and recognize accessory muscle use as a clinical sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.","batch":2,"stem":"A resident reviews the mechanisms that can drive respiratory failure in a patient with severe asthma. Which set is correct?","choices":{"A":"Hypoxemia, hypocarbia, and hypertrophy of the respiratory musculoskeletal system","B":"Hypoxemia, hypercarbia, and failure of the respiratory musculoskeletal system","C":"Hyperoxemia, hypercarbia, and failure of the pulmonary vascular system","D":"Hypoxemia, hypocarbia, and improved mucociliary clearance of the small airways","E":"Hyperoxemia, hypocarbia, and failure of the central nervous system drive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Respiratory failure can be driven by hypoxemia, hypercarbia, and failure of the respiratory musculoskeletal system. Conditions associated with it include severe asthma, COPD exacerbation, and pulmonary edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiratory failure includes failure of oxygenation, ventilation, and the pump. COMLEX: muscle failure is one of three drivers of respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — the muscles that move the ribs","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with COPD has chronically tight scalenes and neck flexors with soreness. Which mechanism explains the soreness and fatigue?","choices":{"A":"Low resting tone increases capillary flow but causes atrophy of the muscle fibers","B":"High resting tone improves oxygen delivery and delays the onset of fatigue","C":"High resting tone impairs local capillary blood flow and accelerates fatigue","D":"Low resting tone permits lengthening that overloads the sternoclavicular joint","E":"High resting tone suppresses afferent input and prevents the sensation of pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronically elevated resting tone in accessory muscles helps maintain the hyperinflated chest shape, but the high resting tension impairs local capillary blood flow, which accelerates muscle fatigue and soreness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sustained contraction compresses capillaries and causes local ischemia. COMLEX: hypertonic accessory muscles are a soft-tissue treatment target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — soft-tissue techniques for hypertonic respiratory muscles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with COPD has weak inspiratory force despite normal muscle bulk. Which principle explains the inefficiency of his respiratory muscles?","choices":{"A":"Hyperinflation lengthens the fibers beyond optimal overlap so the force of contraction falls","B":"Hyperinflation shifts the fibers to an ideal resting length so the contraction becomes efficient","C":"Hyperinflation removes the elastic recoil so the muscles need not contract during inspiration","D":"Hyperinflation increases the number of sarcomeres so the contraction speed becomes excessive","E":"Hyperinflation holds the fibers shortened so they work at an inefficient length-tension point"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By the length-tension relationship, contraction strength depends on resting fiber length. Hyperinflation holds respiratory and accessory muscles in a chronically shortened state, so they function at an inefficient position on the curve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscle force depends on resting length. COMLEX: releasing shortened muscles restores a more efficient length-tension relationship.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — soft-tissue techniques for hypertonic respiratory muscles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe common musculoskeletal and respiratory adaptations associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including barrel chest, shortened respiratory musculature, and increased resting muscle tone.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with COPD has a greater respiratory muscle workload. Which metabolic consequence can disturb her acid-base balance?","choices":{"A":"Greater reliance on aerobic metabolism, which is more efficient and prevents acid buildup","B":"Lesser reliance on glycolysis, which is slower and reduces the production of lactic acid","C":"Greater reliance on ketone oxidation, which is efficient and buffers the arterial blood","D":"Greater reliance on anaerobic metabolism, which is less efficient and adds acid","E":"Lesser reliance on oxygen delivery, which is unaffected by the workload of the muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Increased respiratory muscle workload may cause greater reliance on anaerobic metabolism, which is less efficient and can contribute to acidosis. Mouth breathing may also contribute to respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anaerobic metabolism produces lactate. COMLEX: lowering the work of breathing reduces this metabolic burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — rationale for OMT in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with COPD receives OMT to the diaphragm and thorax. Which proposed mechanism would explain improved clearance of edema from his small airways?","choices":{"A":"Restored ciliary beat in the terminal airways clears the edema through the mucociliary escalator","B":"Increased intrathoracic pressure at rest forces fluid back across the alveolar wall into the capillaries","C":"Paralysis of bronchial smooth muscle from manual pressure on the chest wall reduces the mucosal edema","D":"Reduced pulmonary lymph production decreases the volume that the vessels must remove","E":"Restored diaphragmatic travel and thoracic mobility open lymphatic vessels and enhance fluid return"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pulmonary lymph is pumped mainly by respiratory excursion. Restoring diaphragmatic travel and thoracic cage mobility opens initial lymphatic vessels, clears inflammatory exudate and edema from small airways, and enhances fluid return to the venous circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiratory excursion drives pulmonary lymphatic flow. COMLEX: lymphatic techniques target fluid return.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — lymphatic pump and diaphragmatic release","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with COPD asks whether OMT can replace her inhalers. Which response is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"OMT replaces the medical treatment because it restores lung parenchymal compliance","B":"OMT is unnecessary if the inhaler adherence is good and the symptoms are stable","C":"OMT complements the medical treatment of the underlying respiratory disease","D":"OMT is contraindicated in COPD because it increases the work of breathing","E":"OMT substitutes for oxygen therapy but not for inhaled medication"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Respiratory OMT improves mechanics, thoracic compliance, lymphatic and venous drainage, and autonomic balance while complementing, not replacing, appropriate medical treatment of the underlying disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OMT is adjunctive in pulmonary disease. COMLEX: pneumonia, asthma, and COPD are conditions in which OMT is used as an adjunct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — integrating OMT with conventional management","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease, including improved respiratory mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return to the vascular system.","batch":2,"stem":"A student lists the proposed benefits of OMT in patients with pulmonary disease. Which list is correct?","choices":{"A":"Improved lung parenchymal compliance, paralysis of bronchial smooth muscle, and increased surfactant synthesis","B":"Improved airway inflammation control, increased resting muscle tone, and more anaerobic metabolism","C":"Reduced chest wall motion, reduced fluid return to the vessels, and lower oxygen consumption","D":"Improved diaphragmatic excursion, rib and thoracic motion, and aerobic metabolism efficiency","E":"Improved alveolar gas exchange, increased surfactant production, and eradication of the pathogen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Potential benefits include improving diaphragmatic excursion, improving rib and thoracic motion, reducing restrictions that increase the work of breathing, improving respiratory muscle function, supporting more efficient aerobic metabolism, and facilitating fluid return to the vascular system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OMT acts on mechanics rather than on the pathogen. COMLEX: the goal is to reduce the mechanical burden of breathing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Pulmonology 2","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":1,"stem":"Which system does the course identify as clearing fluid, proteins, and inflammatory debris from the terminal bronchioles and alveoli?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary lymphatic system","B":"Mucociliary escalator","C":"Bronchial arterial system","D":"Azygos venous system","E":"Pulmonary arteriolar system"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The mucociliary mechanism does not extend to the terminal bronchioles or alveoli, so the course identifies the pulmonary lymphatic system as responsible for clearing fluid, proteins, and inflammatory debris from the deep terminal airways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the mucociliary escalator clears the upper airways. COMLEX: pulmonary lymphatics follow both the vasculature and the airways.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":1,"stem":"What primarily propels pulmonary lymph centrally?","choices":{"A":"Ciliary beating of the airway epithelium that sweeps the lymph upward toward the pharynx","B":"Gravity drainage of the lymph from the lung apices toward the lung bases","C":"Pulsatile flow in the bronchial arteries that pushes the lymph along the airways","D":"Respiratory excursion that alternately opens and closes the initial lymphatic channels","E":"Peristaltic waves in the pleural mesothelium that move the lymph toward the hilum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulmonary lymph is propelled primarily by respiratory excursion, which alternately opens and closes the initial lymphatic channels. Infection, inflammation, and somatic dysfunction of the ribs, spine, and diaphragm impair this pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiratory movement helps drive low-pressure lymph flow. COMLEX: restoring excursion restores lymphatic drainage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatic dysfunction of the ribs, spine, and diaphragm impairs respiratory excursion. What follows for lymphatic drainage and lung function?","choices":{"A":"Lymph flow accelerates, interstitial pressure falls, and lung compliance increases","B":"Lymphatic vessels dilate, venous return rises, and the work of breathing falls","C":"Fluid shifts into the pleural space, tension falls, and lung compliance increases","D":"Lymph drains faster through the thoracic duct, and the airway edema resolves","E":"Interstitial fluid accumulates, tension rises, and lung compliance decreases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When lymphatic drainage is impaired, interstitial fluid accumulates, mechanical tension rises, and lung compliance decreases, which increases the work of breathing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: edema stiffens the lung. COMLEX: impaired lymph drainage raises the work of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in normal ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Which foundational osteopathic tenet underlies treating somatic dysfunction in respiratory infection?","choices":{"A":"The body depends on external agents to correct mechanical barriers to recovery","B":"The body has an inherent capacity for self-regulation and self-healing","C":"Antimicrobial therapy restores normal physiologic function in respiratory infection","D":"Somatic dysfunction arises from treatment and has no effect on recovery","E":"Structure and function are independent in the course of respiratory disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The body possesses an inherent capacity for self-regulation and self-healing. When infection disrupts homeostasis, mechanical and physiologic barriers can impede recovery, and OMT aims to support these self-healing processes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: infection can be accompanied by mechanical barriers to recovery. COMLEX: the osteopathic goal is to support physiologic function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement distinguishes antibiotics from OMT in respiratory infection?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotics treat the infectious organism while OMT addresses functional compromise","B":"Antibiotics restore thoracic cage compliance while OMT targets bacterial cell walls","C":"Antibiotics restore normal physiologic function while OMT eradicates the organism directly","D":"Antibiotics support lymphatic flow while OMT sterilizes the infected airways","E":"Antibiotics address functional compromise while OMT kills the pathogen by pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Antibiotics treat the infectious organism but do not themselves restore normal physiologic function. OMT addresses functional compromise, with the goal of supporting physiologic function and the body's self-healing mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: antibiotics are indicated for bacterial infection. COMLEX: OMT complements rather than replaces antimicrobial therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":1,"stem":"Which osteopathic physician described the lymphatic pump procedure in 1923?","choices":{"A":"Bailey","B":"Smith","C":"Miller","D":"Still","E":"Denslow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Miller described the lymphatic pump procedure in 1923 and proposed that enhanced lymphatic circulation could support immune defenses. Bailey described trigeminal stimulation in 1922, and Smith reported influenza outcomes in 1919.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymphatic circulation supports immune function. COMLEX: the lymphatic pump derives from this historical work.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — the proposed physiologic benefits of OMT in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":1,"stem":"Pulmonary visceral afferents are particularly associated with which facilitated segments?","choices":{"A":"Lower thoracic segments, T10–L2","B":"Upper cervical segments, C1–C2","C":"Lumbar and sacral segments, L1–S4","D":"Upper thoracic segments, T2–T6","E":"Lower lumbar segments, L4–L5"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory afferents from the lungs, bronchi, and deep airways establish segmental facilitation in the upper thoracic dorsal horn, with the pulmonary visceral-somatic relationship associated with T2–T6 and prominent changes at T2–T4. This produces paraspinal hypertonicity, tissue texture changes, and vertebral and costal restrictions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lung afferents enter the upper thoracic cord. COMLEX: look for upper thoracic paraspinal and rib findings in lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — rib and related somatic dysfunctions associated with pulmonary dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":1,"stem":"How do elevated or restricted upper ribs affect respiratory function?","choices":{"A":"They disrupt bucket-handle mechanics, relax the scalenes and trapezius, and widen the thoracic inlet","B":"They enhance pump-handle mechanics, decrease scalene and trapezius tension, and widen the thoracic inlet","C":"They disrupt diaphragmatic descent, increase abdominal tension, and narrow the thoracic outlet","D":"They disrupt caliper motion, increase latissimus tension, and narrow the costal angle","E":"They disrupt pump-handle mechanics, raise scalene and trapezius tension, and narrow the thoracic inlet"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Elevated or restricted upper ribs disrupt pump-handle mechanics, increase tension in the scalenes and trapezius, and narrow the thoracic inlet.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper ribs move in pump-handle motion. COMLEX: a narrowed thoracic inlet obstructs lymphatic drainage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — rib and related somatic dysfunctions associated with pulmonary dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":1,"stem":"Cervical somatic dysfunction at C3–C5 alters motor drive to which structure through the phrenic nerve?","choices":{"A":"Thoracoabdominal diaphragm","B":"External intercostal muscles","C":"Anterior scalene muscles","D":"Serratus anterior muscle","E":"Pectoralis minor muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The phrenic nerve arises from C3–C5, so cervical somatic dysfunction at these levels alters motor drive to the thoracoabdominal diaphragm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C3, C4, and C5 keep the diaphragm alive. COMLEX: C3-C5 dysfunction alters diaphragm motor drive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — rib and related somatic dysfunctions associated with pulmonary dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure is the proposed target of rib raising?","choices":{"A":"The vagus nerve at the jugular foramen","B":"The phrenic nerve roots at C3–C5","C":"The thoracic duct at the thoracic inlet","D":"The sympathetic chain ganglia","E":"The superior cervical sympathetic ganglion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rib raising articulates the rib heads and, through fascial attachments, affects the sympathetic chain ganglia that lie near them. It also mobilizes the costovertebral joints and restores pump-handle and bucket-handle excursion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic chain lies along the rib heads. COMLEX: rib raising also helps loosen tenacious mucus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — improved mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return with OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":1,"stem":"Which physiologic mechanism underlies lymphatic pump techniques?","choices":{"A":"Sustained positive intrathoracic pressure forces lymph back into the pulmonary capillaries","B":"Alternating intrathoracic and intra-abdominal pressure shifts propel lymph centrally","C":"Direct stimulation of ciliary beating moves lymph up the tracheobronchial tree","D":"Continuous negative abdominal pressure pulls lymph into the portal circulation","E":"Stretching of the thoracic duct wall causes active peristaltic contraction of lymph"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lymphatic pump techniques introduce alternating positive and negative intrathoracic and intra-abdominal pressures that propel lymph centrally through one-way valved vessels. This increases circulating leukocytes, enhances phagocytic and bacteriolytic activity, and clears pulmonary edema from small airways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymphatic vessels have one-way valves. COMLEX: pumps move lymph toward the venous circulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — improved mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return with OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":1,"stem":"What does diaphragmatic release accomplish?","choices":{"A":"It shortens the diaphragmatic crus and reduces the thoracoabdominal pressure gradient","B":"It immobilizes the diaphragm and removes the pressure gradient between the chambers","C":"It restores vertical diaphragm excursion and the thoracoabdominal pressure gradient","D":"It lengthens the abdominal wall and increases the intrathoracic pressure at rest","E":"It stretches the pleura and increases the negative pressure within the pleural space"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diaphragmatic release relaxes hypertonic crus and peripheral muscle fibers. Restoring vertical excursion restores the intrathoracic to intra-abdominal pressure gradient that draws venous blood and lymph into the chest during inspiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm drives venous and lymphatic return. COMLEX: a hypertonic diaphragm limits excursion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — improved mechanics, aerobic metabolism, and fluid return with OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course lecture, which set lists the common bacterial pathogens of pneumonia?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Escherichia coli, and Shigella dysenteriae","B":"Haemophilus influenzae, Salmonella enterica, and Corynebacterium diphtheriae","C":"Moraxella catarrhalis, Clostridium difficile, and Campylobacter jejuni","D":"Escherichia coli, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, and Treponema pallidum","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lecture lists Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis as common bacterial pathogens in lower respiratory disease and pneumonia. Each other set includes at least one organism not listed in the lecture as a common pneumonia pathogen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: S. pneumoniae is a leading bacterial cause of pneumonia. COMLEX: antibiotics are indicated for bacterial infection when appropriate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct to conventional medical care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the primary conventional approach to viral respiratory infections?","choices":{"A":"Empiric antibiotics followed by culture","B":"Antibiotics given as a full course at presentation","C":"Supportive and symptomatic management","D":"Hospitalization with intravenous antibiotics","E":"Vaccination given after symptom onset"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Viral infections are managed primarily with supportive and symptomatic care. Antibiotics, narrowed by culture results when appropriate, are used for bacterial infections.","boardPrep":"Step 1: antibiotics do not treat viruses. COMLEX: supportive care includes hydration, antipyretics, and mucolytics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct to conventional medical care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which element of conventional management serves as the primary preventive measure for respiratory disease?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy","B":"Vaccination","C":"Hospitalization","D":"Supportive care","E":"Empiric antibiotics"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vaccination, for example pneumococcal, influenza, and COVID-19, is the primary preventive measure that builds adaptive immunity and reduces disease severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vaccination builds adaptive immunity. COMLEX: OMT complements vaccination and does not replace it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct to conventional medical care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with painful rib fractures breathes shallowly. Which effect on venous blood and lymph is expected?","choices":{"A":"A larger pressure gradient with more venous return and faster central lymph movement","B":"A reversed pressure gradient with lymph flowing toward the abdomen through the thoracic duct","C":"An unchanged pressure gradient because lymph flow depends on the arterial pulse","D":"A smaller pressure gradient with unchanged venous return and less central lymph movement","E":"A smaller pressure gradient with less venous return and less central lymph movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Diaphragmatic descent compresses abdominal contents and expands the thoracic cavity, which creates a pressure gradient that draws venous blood through the superior vena cava and sucks lymph centrally. Shallow breathing weakens this gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiration is a venous and lymphatic pump. COMLEX: splinting from pain impairs this pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — negative intrathoracic pressure and diaphragmatic descent","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A student lists the three motions that increase thoracic volume during inspiration. Which list is correct?","choices":{"A":"Inferior pump-handle motion, medial bucket-handle motion, and diaphragmatic ascent","B":"Superior pump-handle motion, lateral bucket-handle motion, and diaphragmatic ascent","C":"Superior pump-handle motion, lateral bucket-handle motion, and diaphragmatic descent","D":"Lateral pump-handle motion, superior bucket-handle motion, and diaphragmatic descent","E":"Posterior pump-handle motion, anterior bucket-handle motion, and sternal descent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thoracic volume increases through three motions: the anterior rib cage rises superiorly in pump-handle motion, the lateral rib cage expands laterally in bucket-handle motion, and the thoracoabdominal diaphragm descends.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inspiration increases thoracic volume. COMLEX: pump-handle motion involves the anterior rib cage and bucket-handle motion the lateral rib cage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how respiratory mechanics, thoracic pressure gradients, and lymphatic flow influence pulmonary and upper respiratory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology teacher compares the roles of the diaphragm and the rib cage in vital capacity breathing. Which statement is correct?","choices":{"A":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for approximately 75 percent of vital capacity ventilation","B":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for approximately 25 percent of vital capacity ventilation","C":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for approximately 50 percent of vital capacity ventilation","D":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for approximately 10 percent of vital capacity ventilation","E":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for nearly 100 percent of vital capacity ventilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diaphragmatic displacement accounts for approximately three-quarters (75 percent) of vital capacity ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm is the dominant inspiratory muscle. COMLEX: restoring diaphragmatic travel has a large effect on ventilation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — thoracic and diaphragmatic mechanics in ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is hospitalized with pneumonia and receives antibiotics. How could resolving his somatic dysfunctions help the treatment work?","choices":{"A":"Closing the lymphatic vessels traps the antibiotics within the infected small airways","B":"Raising intrathoracic pressure forces the exudate across the alveolar wall into the blood","C":"Opening compressed venous and lymphatic vessels helps deliver immune cells and drugs","D":"Direct bactericidal action of the manipulation kills the organism in the lung","E":"Suppressing the immune response limits the inflammatory exudate in the small airways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infection generates inflammatory exudate in the small airways. Resolving somatic dysfunctions opens compressed low-pressure venous and lymphatic vessels, which enables drainage of metabolic wastes and helps deliver immune cells and drugs to infected tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: perfusion and drainage determine drug and cell delivery. COMLEX: OMT complements pharmacotherapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — fluid return to the vascular system with OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old has a cold with nasal congestion and cough. Which regions would a physician most likely evaluate for somatic dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar region, sacrum, pelvis, hips, knees, and lower extremities","B":"Upper thoracic region, upper ribs, clavicles, cranium, and cervical region","C":"Lower thoracic region, lower ribs, costal margin, lumbar region, and sacrum","D":"Upper extremities, elbows, forearms, wrists, hands, and fingers","E":"Lower ribs, lumbar region, pelvis, sacrum, ankles, and feet"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For upper respiratory infection the important regions are the upper thoracic region, upper ribs, clavicles, cranial region, and cervical region, with specific targets including the suboccipital region, trigeminal system, neck soft tissues, thoracic inlet, and thoracic lymphatic system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper airways drain through the neck and thoracic inlet. COMLEX: think open the pathways and improve mechanics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — improved mechanics with OMT in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic rationale for treating somatic dysfunction in patients with upper and lower respiratory tract infections, including the goal of supporting the body’s self-healing mechanisms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with pneumonia says her osteopathic physician told her that OMT will cure the infection. Which statement best reflects the rationale for OMT?","choices":{"A":"It addresses functional compromise to support physiologic function","B":"It kills the infectious organism by direct mechanical action on the lung","C":"It substitutes for antibiotic therapy in infection by a bacterial pathogen","D":"It suppresses the inflammatory response so that antibiotics are unnecessary","E":"It replaces vaccination by building adaptive immunity through lymph movement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OMT addresses functional compromise with the goal of supporting physiologic function and the body's self-healing mechanisms. Antibiotics treat the organism, and OMT is complementary rather than a replacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteopathic care is integrated with medical care. COMLEX: OMT supports rather than replaces antimicrobial therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man is admitted with pneumonia. Which ribs did Still emphasize as particularly important in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Lower ribs 9 and 10","B":"Floating ribs 11 and 12","C":"First and second ribs","D":"Costal cartilages of ribs 8 to 10","E":"Mid-thoracic ribs 6 to 8"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pneumonia, Still emphasized dysfunction of the upper and mid-thoracic ribs, particularly ribs 6 to 8, and their potential effects on intercostal vessels and pulmonary autonomic function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic chain lies near the rib heads. COMLEX: treating the thoracic spine and ribs is part of pneumonia care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — localizing rib somatic dysfunctions associated with pulmonary dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has myofascial restriction at the thoracic inlet. Which effect on drainage is expected?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced drainage of the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct into the jugular veins","B":"Obstruction of arterial supply to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk","C":"Enhanced venous return from the lower extremities through the inferior vena cava","D":"Impaired systemic and pulmonary lymphatic drainage into the subclavian veins","E":"Obstruction of bronchial drainage into the esophageal and azygos veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The thoracic inlet, formed by T1, the first ribs, and the manubrium, is the anatomical bottleneck through which the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct drain into the subclavian veins. Myofascial restriction here impairs systemic and pulmonary lymphatic drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct drains into the subclavian vein system. COMLEX: the thoracic inlet is a key lymphatic region.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — upper ribs and thoracic inlet dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the relevant somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships associated with respiratory pathology, including the T2-T6 facilitated segments, upper ribs, cervical region, thoracic inlet, and cranial structures.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with a viral respiratory infection has occipitomastoid suture dysfunction near the jugular foramen. Which structure is compressed or irritated?","choices":{"A":"The phrenic nerve as it exits the cervical spine","B":"The vagus nerve as it exits the skull","C":"The glossopharyngeal nerve at the foramen ovale","D":"The trigeminal nerve at the foramen rotundum","E":"The sympathetic chain at the first rib head"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Somatic dysfunction near the jugular foramen and occipitomastoid suture compresses or irritates the vagus nerve as it exits the skull, maintaining parasympathetic facilitation and altering airway reflex responses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus exits the skull through the jugular foramen. COMLEX: cranial and upper cervical dysfunction can alter vagal outflow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — rib and cervical dysfunctions associated with pulmonary dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a cough has hypertonic scalenes and intercostals. How do soft-tissue techniques help his respiratory muscles?","choices":{"A":"They compress the capillaries and trap irritants such as substance P and prostaglandins in the muscle","B":"They increase muscle tone and flush irritants such as surfactant proteins and immunoglobulin A","C":"They remove capillary impingement and flush irritants such as substance P and prostaglandins","D":"They shorten the muscle fibers and flush irritants such as lysozyme and lactoferrin","E":"They reduce blood flow and clear irritants such as insulin, glucagon, and cortisol"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Soft-tissue techniques reduce hypertonicity in primary and accessory respiratory muscles. This removes micro-impingements on local capillaries, increases blood flow to remove irritating metabolites such as substance P, prostaglandins, and interleukins, and restores an efficient length-tension relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertonic muscle compresses its own blood supply. COMLEX: scalenes, intercostals, and trapezius are common targets.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — releasing shortened hypertonic respiratory muscles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man receives rib raising that is sustained for more than 90 seconds. Which effect on regional sympathetic output is expected?","choices":{"A":"An initial fall in sympathetic output followed by sustained up-regulation of excessive tone","B":"A sustained rise in sympathetic output with no later change in tone at any duration","C":"An initial rise in parasympathetic output followed by loss of sympathetic innervation","D":"A sustained fall in sympathetic output that begins within the first few seconds","E":"An initial rise in sympathetic output followed by sustained down-regulation of tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rib raising initially stimulates regional sympathetic output, followed by a sustained down-regulation of excessive sympathetic tone. As traditionally described, a short duration produces sympathetic stimulation and a longer duration produces inhibition of sympathetic outflow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duration determines whether rib raising stimulates or inhibits. COMLEX: longer sustained rib raising down-regulates sympathetic tone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — autonomic and mechanical benefits of OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the physiologic rationale for common OMT approaches used in respiratory disease, including rib raising, lymphatic pump techniques, diaphragmatic release, and soft-tissue techniques.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with pneumonia is weak and bedridden and cannot sit up. Which rib raising technique is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Seated rib raising","B":"Standing rib raising","C":"Prone rib raising","D":"Supine rib raising","E":"Lateral recumbent rib raising"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Supine rib raising is especially useful for the patient who cannot sit up, a weakened or sick patient, or a comatose patient. The seated technique suits patients who can sit upright, especially those with difficulty breathing when supine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: position follows patient ability. COMLEX: seated rib raising helps patients who cannot lie flat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — rib raising in seated and supine positions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with pneumonia has hypoxemia, respiratory distress, and unstable blood pressure. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy and hospitalization with monitoring","B":"Outpatient OMT with supportive care and rest at home","C":"Vaccination against pneumococcus to build adaptive immunity","D":"Antipyretics and oral hydration with rest at home","E":"Mucolytics with a lymphatic pump as outpatient care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oxygen therapy and hospitalization are indicated for hypoxemia, respiratory distress, or hemodynamic instability that requires continuous monitoring and advanced supportive care. OMT, supportive care, and vaccination do not replace them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoxemia and instability need monitored care. COMLEX: OMT is adjunctive and should not delay necessary medical therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct to medical care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with a viral upper respiratory infection asks for antibiotics. Which response is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotics are the standard treatment for viral etiologies and should be started","B":"Antibiotics are ineffective against viral etiologies and supportive care is used","C":"Antibiotics are required for viral etiologies when OMT is not available","D":"Antibiotics are replaced by vaccination once the symptoms have begun","E":"Antibiotics are effective against viral etiologies when given by culture"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibiotics are indicated for confirmed or highly suspected bacterial infections and are ineffective against viral etiologies. Viral infections are managed with supportive and symptomatic care, and OMT can be added as an adjunct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: antibiotics have no activity against viruses. COMLEX: OMT is complementary to supportive care.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct to medical care","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Integrate OMT with conventional management of respiratory disease by distinguishing the roles of supportive care, antibiotics, vaccination, oxygen therapy, hospitalization, and osteopathic treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"The Multicenter Osteopathic Pneumonia Study in the Elderly enrolled hospitalized older adults with pneumonia. Which finding was reported in the per-protocol analysis with OMT as an adjunct to standard care?","choices":{"A":"Equivalent outcomes when OMT replaced antibiotics","B":"A longer hospital stay with a lower rate of vaccination","C":"A reduction in the incidence of pneumonia after discharge","D":"A reduction in the length of hospital stay","E":"A reduction in antibiotic resistance among the organisms"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The MOPSE trial found that adding OMT to standard care in hospitalized pneumonia patients significantly reduced the length of hospital stay in per-protocol analysis. OMT was an adjunct to conventional care and not a replacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MOPSE tested OMT as an adjunct to standard care. COMLEX: MOPSE compared conventional care, light touch, and OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — OMT as an adjunct in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Pulmonology and Rib Review Lab","questions":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"In the rib screening approach, which rib is the key rib in an inhalation dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"The highest rib of the group","B":"The central rib of the group","C":"The first rib of the thoracic cage","D":"The rib nearest the sternal border","E":"The lowest rib of the group"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For an inhalation dysfunction the key rib is the lowest rib of the group, and for an exhalation dysfunction the key rib is the upper rib. Treatment is directed toward the key rib.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an inhalation dysfunction is a rib held in elevation. COMLEX: identify the key rib before choosing a technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic cage structure and rib motion in ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rib-muscle table, which muscle is linked to ribs 3 to 5?","choices":{"A":"Pectoralis minor","B":"Serratus anterior","C":"Latissimus dorsi","D":"Quadratus lumborum","E":"Anterior scalene"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rib-muscle table links the first rib to the anterior and middle scalenes, rib 2 to the posterior scalene, ribs 3 to 5 to the pectoralis minor, ribs 6 to 9 to the serratus anterior, and rib 12 to the quadratus lumborum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectoralis minor is a muscle of the anterior upper chest wall. COMLEX: the lab table pairs each rib group with its muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic cage structure and rib motion in ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Which ribs are the floating ribs?","choices":{"A":"Ribs 8 through 10","B":"Ribs 11 and 12","C":"Ribs 6 and 7","D":"Ribs 3 through 5","E":"Ribs 1 and 2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs with no anterior attachment. Ribs 8 to 10 are false ribs that attach indirectly through the costal cartilage of the rib above.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs that attach directly to the sternum. COMLEX: ribs 11 and 12 have a single vertebral facet and no neck or tubercle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — thoracic cage structure and rib motion in ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Still emphasized the upper and mid-thoracic ribs in pneumonia. Which ribs did he emphasize particularly?","choices":{"A":"Ribs 11 to 12","B":"Ribs 9 to 10","C":"Ribs 6 to 8","D":"Ribs 1 to 2","E":"Ribs 3 to 5"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In pneumonia, Still emphasized dysfunction of the upper and mid-thoracic ribs, particularly ribs 6 to 8, and their potential effects on intercostal vessels and pulmonary autonomic function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercostal vessels run with the ribs. COMLEX: rib dysfunction is part of the osteopathic picture of pneumonia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Which rib motion is disrupted when ribs 1 and 2 are elevated or restricted?","choices":{"A":"Pump-handle motion","B":"Bucket-handle motion","C":"Caliper motion","D":"Diaphragmatic descent","E":"Sternal rotation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elevated or restricted upper ribs disrupt pump-handle mechanics, which is the motion that raises the anterior upper rib cage during inspiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pump-handle motion raises the anterior upper rib cage. COMLEX: upper rib restriction limits upper chest expansion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"Pulmonary visceral afferents are associated with which facilitated segments?","choices":{"A":"Lower thoracic segments, T10 to L2","B":"Upper cervical segments, C1 to C3","C":"Lower lumbar segments, L4 to L5","D":"Sacral segments, S2 to S4","E":"Upper thoracic segments, T2 to T6"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pulmonary visceral afferents are associated with the upper thoracic facilitated segments, T2 to T6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral afferents converge on segmental spinal cord levels. COMLEX: facilitated segments produce paraspinal and rib findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — somatic dysfunctions and reflex relationships in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":1,"stem":"Post-isometric relaxation is the lab approach for inhalation motion dysfunction of which ribs?","choices":{"A":"Ribs 3 through 6","B":"Ribs 7 through 10","C":"Ribs 11 and 12","D":"Ribs 1 and 2","E":"Ribs 5 through 8"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The review lab uses post-isometric relaxation for inhalation dysfunction of ribs 1 and 2. Inhalation dysfunction of ribs 3 to 12 is treated with respiratory assist or HVLA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: post-isometric relaxation uses an isometric contraction followed by stretch. COMLEX: match the technique to the rib level and the direction of dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":1,"stem":"In the lab muscle energy rib techniques, how long is the isometric contraction held?","choices":{"A":"10 to 15 seconds","B":"3 to 5 seconds","C":"20 to 30 seconds","D":"1 to 2 seconds","E":"30 to 60 seconds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The isometric contraction is maintained for 3 to 5 seconds, and then the patient is told to stop and relax.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a brief isometric contraction is followed by a relaxation phase. COMLEX: after the relaxation the physician takes up the new barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":1,"stem":"In respiratory assist for an inhaled rib, what does the physician's hand do while the patient inhales?","choices":{"A":"Exaggerates the inhalation motion of the rib","B":"Releases contact with the rib completely","C":"Resists the inhalation motion of the rib","D":"Thrusts the rib toward the sternum","E":"Applies an unyielding counterforce to the arm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For an inhaled rib in respiratory assist, the physician exaggerates the exhalation motion of the rib while the patient exhales and resists its inhalation motion while the patient inhales. The cycle is repeated five to seven times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rib is carried toward its restricted exhalation barrier. COMLEX: respiratory assist uses the patient's breathing as the force.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — physiologic rationale for OMT approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":1,"stem":"For which patient is supine rib raising especially useful?","choices":{"A":"A patient who can sit upright and prefers to stay upright","B":"A weakened or sick patient who is unable to sit up","C":"A patient who breathes more easily when sitting forward","D":"A patient who needs a high-velocity thrust to the ribs","E":"A patient who has a fixed rotation of the cervical spine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Supine rib raising is especially useful for the patient who is unable to sit up, for a weakened or sick patient, or for a comatose patient. Seated rib raising suits patients who can sit upright and prefer to be upright.","boardPrep":"Step 1: supine rib raising lets the table support the patient. COMLEX: choose supine or seated rib raising by the patient's ability to sit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — rib raising and its physiologic rationale","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do the physician's fingers contact the ribs during rib raising?","choices":{"A":"At the costotransverse junctions","B":"At the anterior sternocostal junctions","C":"At the costochondral junctions","D":"At the lateral midaxillary line","E":"At the thoracic spinous processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In both supine and seated rib raising, the fingers contact the ribs at the costotransverse junctions, which lifts and rotates the rib heads.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tubercle of a typical rib articulates with the transverse process. COMLEX: lifting and rotating the rib heads is thought to influence the sympathetic chain ganglia through fascial attachments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — rib raising and its physiologic rationale","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides improving rib motion, which aim does the lab text give for rib raising in certain pulmonary diseases?","choices":{"A":"Removing fluid from the pleural space","B":"Shrinking the volume of the lungs","C":"Dilating the bronchi through direct stretch","D":"Loosening tenacious mucus in the airways","E":"Clearing the bacteria from the alveoli"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In certain pulmonary diseases, rib raising is helpful to loosen tenacious mucus and to articulate the ribs to improve their motion. Rib raising achieves both goals as the physician elevates groups of ribs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thick mucus raises the work of breathing. COMLEX: rib raising complements medical treatment of the underlying disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 4 — rib raising and its physiologic rationale","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a persistent cough and asymmetric rib motion. His physician screens the rib region and finds several dysfunctional ribs. Which plan follows the approach taught in the rib review lab?","choices":{"A":"Treat the thoracic spine first and then direct treatment to the key rib","B":"Treat each rib in order from rib 1 to rib 12 and then reassess the spine","C":"Treat the most tender rib first and then screen the thoracic spine","D":"Treat the diaphragm first and then direct treatment to the sternum","E":"Treat the lowest rib of each group and then reassess the spine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lab approach is to screen the rib region, treat the thoracic spine first, and then direct treatment toward the key rib.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rib articulates with the thoracic vertebra, so spinal and rib restrictions interact. COMLEX: identify the key rib before choosing a technique.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — upper ribs and thoracic spine in respiratory somatic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has an exhaled right 7th rib. According to the lab rib-muscle table, which muscle is linked to this rib group?","choices":{"A":"Pectoralis minor","B":"Latissimus dorsi","C":"Quadratus lumborum","D":"Serratus anterior","E":"Anterior scalene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ribs 6 to 9 are linked to the serratus anterior, and the muscle energy technique for exhaled ribs 6 to 8 uses scapular protraction as the patient pushes the elbow toward the ceiling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the serratus anterior protracts the scapula. COMLEX: the lab table pairs ribs 6 to 9 with the serratus anterior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — soft-tissue and muscle approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review diagnostic approach to Ribs 1-12 and identify the musculature involved in the motion of the ribs and their role in somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has an exhaled right 12th rib. Which muscle attaches to the inferior medial aspect of this rib and is contracted in the lab muscle energy technique?","choices":{"A":"Serratus anterior","B":"Pectoralis minor","C":"Anterior scalene","D":"Transversus thoracis","E":"Quadratus lumborum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The quadratus lumborum attaches to the inferior medial aspect of rib 12, and the lab muscle energy technique for an exhaled 12th rib uses a quadratus lumborum contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the quadratus lumborum attaches to the 12th rib. COMLEX: the lab rib-muscle table guides which muscle to engage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — rib motion and respiratory muscles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with a respiratory infection has limited upper chest expansion. Her physician finds elevated ribs 1 and 2. Which combination of effects on the thoracic inlet and neck muscles is expected?","choices":{"A":"Widened thoracic inlet with decreased scalene tension","B":"Widened thoracic inlet with increased latissimus tension","C":"Narrowed thoracic inlet with increased scalene tension","D":"Narrowed costal angle with increased abdominal tension","E":"Unchanged thoracic inlet with decreased trapezius tension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elevated or restricted upper ribs disrupt pump-handle mechanics, raise scalene tension, and narrow the thoracic inlet.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the scalenes elevate the upper ribs. COMLEX: upper rib restriction is a screening target in respiratory infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — upper ribs, thoracic inlet, and respiratory function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man is admitted with pneumonia. Which potential effects of rib dysfunction did Still emphasize in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Effects on renal blood flow and ureteral peristalsis function","B":"Effects on intercostal vessels and pulmonary autonomic function","C":"Effects on hepatic bile flow and gallbladder emptying function","D":"Effects on cranial suture motion and cerebrospinal fluid function","E":"Effects on pancreatic enzyme release and glucose control function"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Still emphasized rib dysfunction in pneumonia for its potential effects on intercostal vessels and pulmonary autonomic function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercostal vessels and nerves run with the ribs. COMLEX: rib findings accompany the viscerosomatic picture of lung disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — T2-T6 facilitated segments and rib findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correctly identify and localize rib somatic dysfunctions that may be associated with pulmonary dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a chronic cough has myofascial restriction at the thoracic inlet. Which bony structures form the thoracic inlet?","choices":{"A":"T4, the fourth ribs, and the sternal body","B":"T1, the clavicles, and the scapulae","C":"T12, the twelfth ribs, and the xiphoid process","D":"T1, the first ribs, and the manubrium","E":"T6, the sixth ribs, and the costal margin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The thoracic inlet is formed by T1, the first ribs, and the manubrium. Myofascial restriction at this level can impair lymphatic drainage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct and the right lymphatic duct drain near the thoracic inlet. COMLEX: the thoracic inlet is a screening region in respiratory disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — thoracic inlet restriction and lymphatic drainage","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has an inhaled left 6th rib. His physician uses a supine HVLA technique with exhalation emphasis. Which description of the thrust is correct?","choices":{"A":"A thrust slightly caudad to the thenar eminence, placed posterior and inferior to the rib angle","B":"A thrust directly anterior to the thenar eminence, placed over the costal cartilage near the sternum","C":"A thrust slightly lateral to the thenar eminence, placed along the midaxillary line of the chest wall","D":"A thrust slightly medial to the thenar eminence, placed along the sternal edge of the costal margin","E":"A thrust slightly cephalad to the thenar eminence, placed posterior and superior to the rib angle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For an inhaled rib in ribs 3 to 10, the thenar eminence is placed posterior and superior to the angle of the dysfunctional rib, and at the end of exhalation a thrust is delivered through the chest wall slightly cephalad to the thenar eminence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an inhaled rib is held in elevation. COMLEX: for this inhaled-rib technique the thrust is delivered at the end of the patient's exhalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — mechanical rationale for manual techniques in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman has an exhaled right 4th rib. The physician applies caudad and lateral traction at the superior angle of the rib. What does the patient do for the muscle energy technique?","choices":{"A":"Places the hand over the head and pushes the elbow against the physician's hand","B":"Pulls the iliac crest toward the ipsilateral shoulder against the physician's hand","C":"Abducts the arm to 90 degrees and pushes it into the physician's thigh","D":"Flexes the shoulder to 90 degrees and protracts the scapula toward the ceiling","E":"Rotates the head away from the rib and flexes the neck against the physician's resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For an exhalation dysfunction of ribs 3 to 5, the patient raises the arm and places the hand over the head, and the patient pushes the elbow against the physician's hand, which applies an unyielding counterforce. The contraction is held for 3 to 5 seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectoralis minor is linked to ribs 3 to 5. COMLEX: the physician disengages the rib with caudad and lateral traction before each contraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — muscle energy and soft-tissue approaches in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review and demonstrate treatment approaches for the rib region including respiratory assist, post-isometric relaxation muscle energy, and HVLA","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has an exhaled right 12th rib. For the lab muscle energy technique, the physician stands at her left side. How is she positioned?","choices":{"A":"Supine with the legs moved 15 to 20 degrees to the left","B":"Prone with the legs moved 15 to 20 degrees to the left","C":"Seated with the trunk side bent 15 to 20 degrees to the left","D":"Prone with the legs moved 30 to 45 degrees to the right","E":"Supine with the trunk rotated 30 to 45 degrees to the right"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For an exhaled right 12th rib, the patient lies prone and the physician moves the legs 15 to 20 degrees to the left, putting tension on the quadratus lumborum. The patient then pulls the right iliac crest toward the right shoulder against the physician's counterforce.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tensing the quadratus lumborum pulls the 12th rib caudad. COMLEX: the leg position puts tension on or takes tension off the muscle depending on the dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — rib motion and respiratory muscles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with COPD is more comfortable upright and has difficulty breathing when supine. Which rib raising approach fits him?","choices":{"A":"Seated rib raising with the fingers at the costotransverse junctions","B":"Supine rib raising with the fingers at the costotransverse junctions","C":"Seated rib raising with the fingers at the sternocostal junctions","D":"Supine rib raising with the fingers at the costochondral junctions","E":"Seated rib raising with the fingers at the xiphoid process"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Seated rib raising applies to patients who can sit upright, especially those who have difficulty breathing when supine. In both positions the fingers contact the ribs at the costotransverse junctions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COPD hyperinflation raises the work of breathing. COMLEX: the patient's position tolerance chooses the rib raising approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — OMT benefits in chronic pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with pneumonia receives seated rib raising. How does the technique elevate his anterior rib cage?","choices":{"A":"The physician pulls his shoulders backward as he sits upright","B":"He falls forward onto his crossed arms as the physician rocks back","C":"He extends his trunk against resistance as the physician rocks back","D":"The physician lifts his arms overhead as he inhales deeply","E":"He rotates toward the physician as the physician presses the sternum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In seated rib raising the patient crosses the arms with the elbows resting on the physician's upper arms, and the physician's fingers grasp a group of ribs at the costotransverse junctions. The physician rocks back while pressing down on the posterior ribs as the patient falls forward onto the arms, which elevates the anterior rib cage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rib tubercle meets the transverse process at the costotransverse joint. COMLEX: the patient can inhale as the physician rocks back and exhale fully as the physician moves forward.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — rib raising and thoracic mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Review and demonstrate rib raising in both seated and supine positions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with a respiratory infection receives seated rib raising continuously for two minutes. As traditionally described, which effect on sympathetic outflow is expected?","choices":{"A":"Stimulation of sympathetic outflow","B":"No change in sympathetic outflow","C":"Inhibition of sympathetic outflow","D":"Permanent loss of sympathetic tone","E":"Selective loss of parasympathetic tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As traditionally described, rib raising for less than 30 seconds produces a sympathetic stimulatory response, and rib raising for more than 90 seconds produces inhibition of sympathetic outflow. Two minutes exceeds 90 seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sympathetic chain ganglia lie anterior to the rib heads. COMLEX: as traditionally described, under 30 seconds stimulates and over 90 seconds inhibits sympathetic outflow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — autonomic effects of OMT in pulmonary disease","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"SDL 25 — Upper Crossed Syndrome, Janda Part 1","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of muscles is tight, facilitated, and shortened in Janda's classic Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Upper trapezius, rhomboids, and pectoralis minor","B":"Levator scapulae, serratus anterior, and pectoralis major","C":"Suboccipitals, middle trapezius, and scalenes","D":"Upper trapezius, levator scapulae, and pectoralis minor","E":"Sternocleidomastoid, lower trapezius, and levator scapulae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The tight, facilitated, shortened group in classic UCS is the upper trapezius, levator scapulae, pectoralis major and minor, suboccipitals, sternocleidomastoid, and scalenes. Each other choice pairs a tight muscle with a muscle from the weak, inhibited group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: classic UCS is a sagittal-plane imbalance with tight posterior-superior and anterior-inferior muscles crossing weak anterior-superior and posterior-inferior muscles. COMLEX: the muscles to release are the tight group, and the weak group is strengthened afterward.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the tonic and phasic muscle groups of the upper and lower crossed syndromes and their postural deformities","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":1,"stem":"Which postural distortion is expected in classic Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Decreased cervical lordosis with thoracic hyperlordosis and bilateral scapular retraction with shoulder elevation","B":"Symmetrical forward head posture with thoracic hyperkyphosis and bilateral shoulder protraction","C":"Posterior head carriage with reduced thoracic kyphosis and bilateral shoulder retraction with scapular adduction","D":"Lateral head tilt with unilateral shoulder elevation and flattening of the cervical curve with thoracic scoliosis","E":"Anterior pelvic tilt with increased lumbar lordosis and bilateral hip flexion with a protruding abdomen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic UCS produces symmetrical forward head posture, increased cervical lordosis, increased thoracic hyperkyphosis, and bilateral rounding and protraction of both shoulders. The other choices reverse the sagittal curves, describe a pelvic pattern, or introduce asymmetry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: increased cervical lordosis and thoracic kyphosis accompany forward head carriage. COMLEX: look for a symmetrical pattern when the history points to prolonged desk work.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the tonic and phasic muscle groups of the upper and lower crossed syndromes and their postural deformities","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":1,"stem":"Morris, Bonnefin, and Darville updated Janda's model into the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome. Which planes does the updated model describe?","choices":{"A":"The sagittal plane with symmetrical bilateral distortion","B":"The transverse plane with distortion confined to the cervical spine","C":"The sagittal plane plus transverse rotation and frontal tilt","D":"The frontal plane with lateral flexion of the thoracic spine","E":"The sagittal plane plus frontal tilt without a rotational component"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TUCS is a multi-planar, three-dimensional update that adds transverse (rotational) and frontal plane distortions to the sagittal-plane pattern of Janda's classic model. A purely sagittal, symmetrical description is the classic UCS, and the other choices omit planes the model includes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: classic UCS is two-dimensional and TUCS is three-dimensional. COMLEX: the added rotational and frontal components create the asymmetric findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the tonic and phasic muscle groups of the upper and lower crossed syndromes and their postural deformities","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"Constant mechanoreceptor and nociceptor activation from a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion produces which local change?","choices":{"A":"Hypotonia and lengthening of the ipsilateral pectoral muscles with reduced nociceptor firing","B":"Flaccid weakness of the ipsilateral pectoralis major with compensatory scalene hypertonicity","C":"Contralateral pectoral hypertonicity with ipsilateral pectoral inhibition and reduced tenderness","D":"Atrophy of the ipsilateral pectoralis major from disuse with loss of fascial glide","E":"Hypertonicity and shortening of the ipsilateral chest wall myofascial tissues"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The inter-fascial adhesion drives continuous afferent activation, which induces hypertonicity and shortening of ipsilateral chest wall tissues including the pectoralis major and minor, intercostals, and sternalis. Hypotonia, flaccid weakness, contralateral change, and atrophy are not the local response described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sustained nociceptor input facilitates the segmental motor response. COMLEX: the first change is local pectoral hypertonicity, and the distant effects follow from it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington reciprocal inhibition and Korr spinal facilitation in the crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"Torsional fascial traction from a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion asymmetrically specifically loads which upper cervical structures?","choices":{"A":"C5–C6 zygapophyseal joints and the C6 anterior tubercle region","B":"C2–C3 zygapophyseal joints and the C2 transverse process","C":"Occipitoatlantal joint capsule and the C1 transverse process","D":"C4–C5 zygapophyseal joints and the C4 articular pillar region","E":"C7–T1 junction and the T1 transverse process on one side"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The superficial lamina of the deep pectoral fascia carries asymmetric tension up through the clavicle and neck, loading the C2–C3 zygapophyseal joints and C2 transverse process. This explains the marked tenderness over the ipsilateral C2 transverse process in TUCS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper cervical loading is the link from chest wall fascia to the head. COMLEX: C2 tenderness with head tilt toward the protracted shoulder points to TUCS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington reciprocal inhibition and Korr spinal facilitation in the crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"Asymmetric loading of the upper cervical joints from torsional fascial traction triggers which type of headache?","choices":{"A":"Tension-type or cervicogenic headache","B":"Cluster headache with ipsilateral autonomic signs","C":"Migraine with aura from cortical spreading depression","D":"Trigeminal neuralgia in the maxillary distribution","E":"Giant cell arteritis with jaw claudication"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The asymmetric upper cervical loading produces tension-type or cervicogenic headaches. The other choices are primary or vascular headache and facial pain syndromes with different mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cervicogenic headache arises from cervical structures. COMLEX: recurrent unilateral headache with C2 tenderness and shoulder protraction should prompt a chest wall fascial exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — Sherrington reciprocal inhibition and Korr spinal facilitation in the crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the pectofascial glide assessment performed?","choices":{"A":"Over the 2nd and 3rd ribs along the mid-clavicular line","B":"Over the 6th and 7th ribs along the mid-clavicular line","C":"Over the 4th and 5th ribs along the mid-clavicular line","D":"Over the 4th and 5th ribs along the posterior axillary line","E":"Over the 8th and 9th ribs along the midaxillary line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The glide is tested bilaterally over the 4th and 5th ribs along the mid-clavicular line, about one to two ribs cephalad to the inframammary fold. That is the location of the circular tender adhesion of the lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: surface landmarks anchor a palpatory exam. COMLEX: test both sides and compare tenderness and glide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques within the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which two findings define a positive pectofascial assessment?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral muscle tightness with restricted rib cage excursion during forced inhalation","B":"Pectoralis major hypertonicity with weakness on resisted shoulder adduction at 90 degrees","C":"Tenderness over the C2 transverse process with restricted cervical rotation to the left","D":"Skin hypersensitivity with reduced sensation over the fourth thoracic dermatome on one side","E":"Unilateral tenderness and restricted multi-planar glide of the superficial fascia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The diagnostic criteria are unilateral pectofascial tenderness and restricted multi-planar gliding of the loose superficial fascial layer over the deep pectoral fascia. The other choices describe muscle, rib, cervical, or sensory findings that are not part of the maneuver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a focal fascial adhesion is a different finding from diffuse muscle tightness. COMLEX: compare the sides, since the finding is unilateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques within the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":1,"stem":"Which approach gives the most reliable palpation of the pectofascial layers in a woman with substantial breast tissue?","choices":{"A":"Direct anterior palpation over the center of the breast tissue surface","B":"A lateral approach through the axilla along the latissimus dorsi border","C":"A superior approach from beneath the clavicle and the first rib margin","D":"A caudal approach from the inframammary fold beneath the breast tissue","E":"A posterior approach over the scapular spine and the medial scapular border"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In patients with excess adipose, direct anterior or lateral palpation is unreliable because of superficial breast tissue bulk. Access from the sub-mammillary, inframammary fold allows direct pectofascial palpation under the breast tissue. Direct mid-pectoral palpation suits males and patients with minimal adipose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overlying adipose and breast tissue obscure deeper fascia. COMLEX: choose the approach by patient anatomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques within the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding distinguishes Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome from classic Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Increased thoracic hyperkyphosis with bilateral shoulder rounding and protraction","B":"Unilateral shoulder protraction on the side of a focal fascial adhesion","C":"Forward head posture with increased cervical lordosis and symmetrical carriage","D":"Hypertonicity of the upper trapezius and levator scapulae on both sides","E":"Diffuse bilateral pectoral tightness without a focal tender fascial lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TUCS shows unilateral shoulder protraction on the side of the lesion, with ipsilateral head tilt and C2 tenderness. Bilateral rounding, forward head posture with increased lordosis, and diffuse bilateral pectoral tightness describe the symmetrical classic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a unilateral focal lesion produces asymmetric findings. COMLEX: asymmetry on exam suggests TUCS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":1,"stem":"Which history is typical of classic Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged desk work with computer and phone posture","B":"Unilateral chest wall trauma from a seat belt injury","C":"Breast augmentation surgery with later scar formation","D":"Radiation therapy with scarring of the chest wall tissue","E":"Repetitive overhead throwing in a sport such as softball"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classic UCS follows prolonged desk work and computer or phone posture. TUCS follows unilateral chest wall trauma, post-surgical or radiation scars, breast augmentation, repetitive sports such as softball, or irritation from tight compressive clothing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic postural stress drives the classic imbalance. COMLEX: ask about trauma, surgery, and sports when the presentation is asymmetric.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":1,"stem":"Which symptom profile suggests Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Generalized bilateral neck and shoulder stiffness with postural fatigue and tiredness","B":"Bilateral hand weakness with atrophy of the thenar eminence and numb fingertips","C":"Progressive loss of cervical lordosis with morning stiffness in both shoulders","D":"Bilateral occipital headaches with fatigue after prolonged sitting at a computer","E":"Recurrent unilateral shoulder pain with chest wall pain and arm dysesthesias"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TUCS presents with recurrent unilateral shoulder pain, chest wall pain, and arm dysesthesias. Generalized bilateral stiffness and postural fatigue are the classic UCS profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a focal fascial lesion causes unilateral symptoms. COMLEX: chest wall pain with arm paresthesias and a head tilt supports TUCS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":1,"stem":"Why does indirect myofascial release fail to release a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion?","choices":{"A":"The adhesion lies within the pectoralis major muscle belly, beyond the reach of any fascial release","B":"The deep pectoral fascia is too thick for fascial slack to be taken up at the lesion site","C":"Indirect release increases nociceptor afferent input and amplifies the local hypertonicity","D":"The superficial pectoral fascia lacks skeletal attachment and moves with the pectoralis major","E":"The lesion lies beneath the ribs where fascial slack cannot be reached from the skin surface"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indirect myofascial release acts on muscle origins and insertions, but the superficial pectoral fascia has no skeletal muscle attachment. Moving the pectoralis major carries the superficial fascia along without separating the two layers, so the adhesion stays intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an inter-fascial adhesion needs a shearing force between layers. COMLEX: direct release is the technique that separates them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — Janda sequencing rule of releasing hypertonic antagonists before strengthening inhibited agonists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":1,"stem":"Which law explains why strengthening the scapular stabilizers fails while a hypertonic pectoral complex remains active?","choices":{"A":"Sherrington's law of reciprocal inhibition","B":"Fryette's law of spinal motion and coupling","C":"Wolff's law of bone remodeling and loading","D":"Hilton's law of joint and muscle innervation","E":"Davis's law of soft tissue adaptation and strain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By Sherrington's law of reciprocal inhibition, the shortened hypertonic pectoral complex sends continuous Ia inhibitory signals to spinal motor neurons, shutting down the antagonist scapular stabilizers: the rhomboids, serratus anterior, and lower trapezius.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition means activation of an agonist inhibits its antagonist. COMLEX: a tight muscle keeps its antagonist inhibited.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — Janda sequencing rule of releasing hypertonic antagonists before strengthening inhibited agonists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":1,"stem":"According to Janda's core sequencing rule, what must happen before an inhibited muscle is strengthened?","choices":{"A":"Stretching of the inhibited muscle itself","B":"Strengthening of its hypertonic antagonist","C":"Release of its hypertonic antagonist","D":"Plyometric training of the inhibited muscle","E":"Strengthening of a nearby synergist muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Janda's rule is never to strengthen an inhibited muscle before releasing its hypertonic antagonist. Strengthening is deferred until the antagonist is released.","boardPrep":"Step 1: release first, then strengthen. COMLEX: sequence treatment so the antagonist is released before rehabilitation of the weak muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — Janda sequencing rule of releasing hypertonic antagonists before strengthening inhibited agonists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old accountant works at a computer for ten hours a day and reports neck stiffness and fatigue. Examination shows symmetrical forward head posture, increased thoracic kyphosis, and bilaterally rounded shoulders. Which set of muscles is most likely inhibited and lengthened?","choices":{"A":"Longus colli, pectoralis major, and rhomboids","B":"Middle trapezius, scalenes, and serratus anterior","C":"Longus colli, lower trapezius, and rhomboids","D":"Lower trapezius, levator scapulae, and longus capitis","E":"Rhomboids, sternocleidomastoid, and serratus anterior"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Classic UCS inhibits and lengthens the deep neck flexors (longus colli and longus capitis), middle and lower trapezius, rhomboids, and serratus anterior. The pectoralis major, scalenes, levator scapulae, and sternocleidomastoid belong to the tight, shortened group, so each other choice mixes in a tight muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: weak anterior-superior and posterior-inferior muscles cross the tight posterior-superior and anterior-inferior group. COMLEX: strengthen the inhibited group only after the tight group is treated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — identifying the hypertonic and inhibited muscle groups in the crossed syndromes and predicting the postural deformity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has right shoulder protraction and a head tilt after years of repetitive softball. Which fascial continuity best explains how chest wall tension reaches the neck?","choices":{"A":"Continuity of the superficial pectoral fascia with the quadratus lumborum and gluteus maximus muscles","B":"Continuity of the pectoral fascia with the erector spinae and multifidus of the lumbar region","C":"Continuity of the pectoral fascia with the diaphragm and transversus abdominis muscle sheaths","D":"Continuity of the deep pectoral fascia with the sternocleidomastoid and occipital cervical chains","E":"Continuity of the pectoral fascia with the biceps brachii and brachialis of the upper arm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The superficial lamina of the deep pectoral fascia communicates across myofascial slings with the sternocleidomastoid superiorly, the deltoid, trapezius, and latissimus laterally, the contralateral pectoralis across the chest wall, and the external obliques inferiorly. It passes over the clavicle to integrate with occipital and cervical chains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fascial slings transmit tension between regions. COMLEX: chest wall lesions can present as neck and head findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — fascial and neurophysiologic mechanisms behind regional muscle imbalance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recall the anatomical layers, fascial continuity, and muscular imbalances characteristic of Vladimir Janda’s classic Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS) versus the Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome (TUCS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a tender, circular adhesion of about 1 cm in the left mid-pectoral region. Between which two layers is the adhesion located?","choices":{"A":"The deep pectoral fascia over the muscle and the periosteum of the 4th and 5th ribs beneath it","B":"The pectoralis major muscle belly and the pectoralis minor muscle belly lying directly beneath it","C":"The dermis of the overlying skin and the superficial fascia enveloping the breast tissue","D":"The intercostal muscles of the 4th space and the parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall","E":"The superficial pectoral fascia over breast tissue and the deep fascia on the pectoralis major"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An MPFL is an adhesion at the loose areolar sub-mammillary interface between the superficial pectoral fascia, which envelops breast tissue and platysma, and the deep pectoral fascia, which adheres to the pectoralis major.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a loose areolar fascial plane lets layers glide until an adhesion forms. COMLEX: the lesion is between fascial layers, not within muscle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — palpatory examination in the upper crossed syndrome work-up","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman had a left seat belt injury six months ago. She now has recurrent left shoulder pain, left shoulder protraction, and a head tilt to the left. Which mechanism best links the chest wall to the neck findings?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral latissimus hypertonicity rotating the thoracic spine and tilting the head to the opposite side of the body","B":"Bilateral deep neck flexor hypertonicity flexing the head forward over the upper thorax and the sternum","C":"Tension carried from the pectoral fascia over the clavicle into the occipital and cervical chains","D":"Primary degeneration of the C2–C3 facets that secondarily tightens the ipsilateral pectoralis major muscle","E":"Ipsilateral serratus anterior hypertonicity retracting the scapula and tilting the head away from the shoulder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lesion causes ipsilateral pectoral hypertonicity that pulls the glenohumeral joint into protraction. Tension then travels through the pectoral fascia over the clavicle to the occipital and cervical chains, asymmetrically loading C2–C3 and tilting the head toward the protracted shoulder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: local hypertonicity propagates through connected fascial chains. COMLEX: trace the pattern from the chest wall up to the cervical spine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — mechanisms of regional muscle imbalance and spinal facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man has a long-standing left Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion. Which additional somatic findings would be expected on examination?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral greater than ipsilateral costosternal, sternoclavicular, and acromioclavicular joint dysfunction","B":"Ipsilateral greater than contralateral costosternal, sternoclavicular, and acromioclavicular findings","C":"Bilateral symmetric sacroiliac, lumbosacral, and thoracolumbar junction dysfunction with pelvic tilt","D":"Ipsilateral tarsal, metatarsal, and talocrural joint dysfunction with a shortened stride on that side","E":"Bilateral temporomandibular, sphenobasilar, and occipitomastoid suture dysfunction of the cranium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lesion generates a cascade of costosternal, sternoclavicular, acromioclavicular, costovertebral, and thoracic spinal somatic dysfunctions that are ipsilateral greater than contralateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: unilateral lesions produce asymmetric kinetic chain effects. COMLEX: check ribs, clavicular joints, and thoracic spine on the side of the lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — predicting the postural and regional dysfunctions of crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathophysiological mechanism by which a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion (MPFL) alters regional kinetic chains, resulting in asymmetric posture, cervical dysfunction, and distant somatic dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old softball player with an MPFL has scapular instability and subacromial impingement on the same side. Which MPFL-related mechanism explains the impingement?","choices":{"A":"A primary supraspinatus tear destabilizing the scapula and altering scapulohumeral kinematics at rest","B":"Deltoid weakness allowing superior migration of the humeral head against the acromion during abduction","C":"Rhomboid hypertonicity retracting the scapula and narrowing the subacromial space during elevation","D":"Pectoral hypertonicity pulling the shoulder into protraction and altering scapulohumeral kinematics","E":"Anterior glenohumeral capsule laxity increasing humeral head translation and causing impingement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pectoral hypertonicity pulls the ipsilateral glenohumeral joint into anterior protraction, altering scapulohumeral kinematics and producing scapular instability and subacromial impingement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: altered scapulohumeral rhythm narrows the subacromial space. COMLEX: consider the chest wall fascia in recurrent shoulder impingement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — hypertonic muscle groups and the postural deformities they predict","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with large breasts has recurrent right shoulder and chest wall pain and a right head tilt. A mid-pectoral lesion on the right is suspected. Which palpation and treatment plan is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Palpate from the inframammary fold and treat seated with light shearing thrusts toward the opposite shoulder","B":"Palpate over the center of the breast and treat supine with pisiform thrusts toward the opposite shoulder and the clavicle","C":"Palpate from the inframammary fold and treat supine with a sustained indirect release of the pectoral fascia","D":"Palpate along the axillary fold and treat seated with muscle energy to the pectoralis minor and the serratus","E":"Palpate over the center of the breast and treat seated with sustained thumb pressure on the 4th rib and the sternum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In females with excess adipose, the lesion is reached from the inframammary fold. Treatment is seated so gravity tenses the superficial mammillary tissue away from the deep fascia, using 3 to 5 light shearing thrusts toward the opposite shoulder and then toward the ipsilateral shoulder. Indirect and muscle energy methods contacting muscle attachments fail.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gravity separates the fascial layers in the seated position. COMLEX: follow the release with daily self chest wall mobilization and postural retraining.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with minimal chest adipose has recurrent left shoulder pain after a direct impact to the chest. A tender mid-pectoral adhesion is palpated. Which direct technique is used?","choices":{"A":"Seated web-space contact with shearing thrusts directed toward the ipsilateral hip and held for 5 seconds","B":"Supine pisiform contact with slack taken toward the opposite shoulder and 3 to 5 light thrusts","C":"Supine thenar contact with slack taken toward the ipsilateral shoulder and held for 90 seconds of pressure","D":"Prone hypothenar contact with 3 to 5 thrusts directed toward the ipsilateral scapula and the spine","E":"Supine pisiform contact with a single high-velocity thrust along the pectoralis major fiber direction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In males the patient is supine, a pisiform or hypothenar contact is placed over the lesion, slack is taken toward the opposite shoulder to a hard barrier, and 3 to 5 light quick thrusts are directed toward the opposite shoulder, also in an orthogonal 90 degree plane, to separate the fibrotic adhesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: shear separates fascial layers. COMLEX: treatment position differs between males and females.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate the appropriate diagnostic palpatory maneuvers (such as the pectofascial glide assessment) and treatment strategies (direct vs. indirect release) based on patient anatomy and clinical presentation","batch":2,"stem":"A seated woman is treated for a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion with access from beneath the breast. Which hand contact is used?","choices":{"A":"The pisiform with access through the axillary fold and the lateral chest wall","B":"The thenar eminence pressed over the sternum and the costal cartilages","C":"The fingertips placed on the nipple line over the 4th intercostal space","D":"The heel of the hand placed over the clavicle and the sternoclavicular joint","E":"The first and second interdigital web as a functional spade"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For the seated female technique, the first and second interdigital web acts as a functional spade via inframammary access, delivering 3 to 5 light shearing thrusts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a broad contact edge shears a fascial plane. COMLEX: the technique is adapted to patient anatomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old accountant works nine hours a day at a desk. Examination shows forward head posture, bilateral shoulder rounding, and diffuse pectoral tightness with no focal tender lesion and no head tilt. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":{"A":"Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome from a mid-pectoral fascial lesion on one side","B":"Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome from post-surgical scarring of the chest wall","C":"Lower Crossed Syndrome with anterior pelvic tilt and increased lumbar lordosis","D":"Classic Upper Crossed Syndrome with a symmetrical sagittal-plane imbalance","E":"Cervicogenic headache syndrome from C2–C3 dysfunction with ipsilateral tenderness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Symmetrical bilateral findings from prolonged desk work without a focal adhesion or head tilt describe classic UCS. TUCS needs asymmetric findings and a localized lesion, and the lower crossed syndrome affects the pelvis and lumbar spine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral symmetric findings indicate a sagittal pattern. COMLEX: no focal lesion and no tilt rule out TUCS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old softball player has right shoulder protraction, a right head tilt, and a tender 1 cm adhesion over the anterior chest. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":{"A":"Classic Upper Crossed Syndrome from repetitive overhead activity of the shoulders","B":"Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome with a Mid-Pectoral Fascial Lesion on that side","C":"Classic Upper Crossed Syndrome with left-sided compensation of the head and neck","D":"Cervical radiculopathy from C2 nerve root compression at the foramen","E":"Thoracic outlet syndrome from first rib dysfunction with brachial plexus traction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unilateral shoulder protraction, ipsilateral head tilt, ipsilateral C2 tenderness, and a palpable tender adhesion at the 4th and 5th ribs on the mid-clavicular line define TUCS with an MPFL. Repetitive sports such as softball are a recognized cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a focal fascial lesion with ipsilateral findings. COMLEX: palpate the 4th and 5th ribs along the mid-clavicular line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between classic sagittal-plane Upper Crossed Syndrome and multi-planar Torsional Upper Crossed Syndrome based on structural examination findings and clinical history","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has worn a tight compressive brassiere for years and now has left shoulder protraction with recurrent left shoulder pain. Which cervical finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral C2 transverse process tenderness with head tilt toward the protracted shoulder","B":"Contralateral C2 transverse process tenderness with head tilt away from the protracted shoulder","C":"Bilateral C5 tenderness with symmetrical forward head posture and cervical flattening","D":"Ipsilateral C7 tenderness with head rotation away from the protracted shoulder","E":"Bilateral suboccipital tenderness with posterior head carriage and reduced lordosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Compressive clothing irritation is a recognized cause of TUCS. The torsional pattern includes a head tilt toward the protracted shoulder and marked tenderness over the ipsilateral C2 transverse process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ipsilateral fascial traction loads the upper cervical joints on the same side. COMLEX: ask about clothing and surgical history in unilateral presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old softball player has recurrent right shoulder pain after eight weeks of rhomboid, serratus anterior, and lower trapezius strengthening plus indirect myofascial release of the pectoralis major. Examination shows an active right MPFL. Which explanation best accounts for the failure?","choices":{"A":"Scapular stabilizer weakness is primary, and indirect release over-lengthens the pectoralis major muscle","B":"Pectoral hypotonicity disinhibits the scapular stabilizers, and indirect release separates the adhesion","C":"Cervical facet dysfunction inhibits the scapular stabilizers, and indirect release overloads the rib cage","D":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy limits strengthening, and indirect release lengthens the superficial fascia","E":"Pectoral hypertonicity inhibits the stabilizers, and indirect release moves both fascial layers together"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The hypertonic pectoral complex sends continuous inhibitory signals that shut down the scapular stabilizers. Indirect release acts on muscle attachments, and the superficial pectoral fascia has none, so the two layers move as one and the adhesion persists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition suppresses the antagonist. COMLEX: persistent symptoms despite rehabilitation should prompt a search for the primary lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — releasing the hypertonic antagonist before strengthening the inhibited agonist","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":2,"stem":"A softball player with an active MPFL has already failed strengthening and indirect myofascial release. Which treatment order is correct?","choices":{"A":"Direct release of the adhesion, then strengthening of the scapular stabilizers","B":"Strengthening of the scapular stabilizers, then direct release of the adhesion","C":"Indirect myofascial release of the pectoralis major, then strengthening of the scapular stabilizers","D":"Pectoral stretching with concurrent scapular strengthening, then release of the adhesion","E":"Manipulation of C2 first, then pectoral strengthening"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hypertonic antagonist must be released before the inhibited muscle is strengthened. The adhesion needs direct shearing release, since indirect release does not separate the layers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: release the tight antagonist, then restore recruitment. COMLEX: treatment order matters for lasting results.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Janda sequencing rule in the management plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Justify why traditional indirect myofascial release techniques or targeted shoulder rehabilitation may fail in patients presenting with recurrent upper quarter pain secondary to an active MPFL","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist proposes to strengthen the serratus anterior and lower trapezius in a patient with an unreleased MPFL. Which outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Restored motor recruitment with gradual remodeling and resolution of the adhesion","B":"Transient improvement followed by permanent atrophy of the pectoralis major muscle","C":"Poor motor recruitment with compensatory strain and rapid symptom recurrence","D":"Improved motor recruitment with new weakness of the deep neck flexor muscles","E":"Progressive release of the adhesion from repeated loading of the scapular stabilizers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exercising against an unreleased MPFL fails to restore motor recruitment because the hypertonic pectoral complex keeps the stabilizers inhibited. It induces compensatory strain and rapid recurrence of symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inhibition persists while the antagonist remains hypertonic. COMLEX: release before rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Janda sequencing rule in the management plan","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":26,"title":"SDL 26 — Upper, Lower Crossed Syndrome, Janda Part 2","questions":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"According to Janda's model, which response to chronic stress characterizes the postural stabilizer muscle group?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition with weakness and early fatigue","B":"Hypotonia with lengthening and gradual atrophy","C":"Normal resting tone with delayed firing onset","D":"Reduced spindle sensitivity with joint laxity","E":"Hypertonicity with spasm and shortening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tonic postural stabilizers respond to stress, static posture, and repetitive strain with hypertonicity, spasm, and shortening. Phasic dynamic movers respond with inhibition, weakness, and fatigue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Janda describes muscle imbalance as a central nervous system response to chronic overload. COMLEX: tonic muscles tighten and phasic muscles weaken.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 5 — why strengthening fails while a hypertonic antagonist remains active","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement describes Sherrington's law of reciprocal inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Spinal interneurons facilitate the antagonist of a contracting prime mover","B":"Spinal interneurons inhibit the antagonist of a chronically hypertonic prime mover","C":"Golgi tendon organs relax the prime mover when muscle tension rises too far","D":"Muscle spindles excite the synergist of a stretched muscle through alpha motor neurons","E":"Descending cortical input suppresses the muscles that cross a single joint during movement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a prime mover contracts or becomes chronically hypertonic, spinal cord interneurons automatically inhibit and relax its direct functional antagonist. This is the neurophysiologic basis of the crossed syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition is mediated by spinal interneurons. COMLEX: a chronically tight muscle keeps its antagonist weak.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 5 — why strengthening fails while a hypertonic antagonist remains active","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"Korr conceptualized a facilitated spinal segment as which type of neurologic structure?","choices":{"A":"Neurologic filter","B":"Neurologic gate","C":"Neurologic relay","D":"Neurologic lens","E":"Neurologic switch"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Korr described the facilitated segment as a neurologic lens that channels and magnifies normal baseline afferent impulses, so that minimal stimuli trigger exaggerated motor efferent responses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitation involves sustained subthreshold depolarization of dorsal horn interneurons. COMLEX: facilitated segments keep paraspinal muscles in spasm and sustain sympathetic overactivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 5 — why strengthening fails while a hypertonic antagonist remains active","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":1,"stem":"Which imaging result is typical of a functional musculoskeletal pathology?","choices":{"A":"Normal plain films and MRI or incidental age-related change","B":"Vertebral collapse visible on CT with a focal myotomal deficit","C":"Pars fracture visible on oblique plain films of the lumbar spine","D":"Disc compression visible on MRI at the symptomatic level","E":"Bone destruction visible on plain film and CT of the spine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional pathology is nondiagnostic on imaging: plain films, CT, and MRI appear normal or show incidental age-related change, because they fail to capture dynamic motor firing dysfunction. Fractures, collapse, pars defects, and disc compression are structural findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: imaging shows structure but not motor firing. COMLEX: normal imaging does not exclude a functional cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":1,"stem":"Which examination finding favors a functional rather than a structural pathology?","choices":{"A":"Fixed articular barriers that do not change with treatment","B":"Focal dermatomal deficit in a single nerve root distribution","C":"Anatomical joint instability on stress testing of the segment","D":"Joint restrictions that improve dynamically with OMT","E":"Localized sharp pain over a single bony landmark"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Functional pathology shows altered movement patterns, reciprocal muscle weakness, postural distortion, and joint restrictions that improve dynamically with OMT. Fixed barriers, focal dermatomal or myotomal deficits, anatomical instability, and localized sharp pain suggest structural pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a restriction that changes with treatment is not fixed anatomy. COMLEX: response to OMT supports a functional cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism underlies a functional musculoskeletal pathology?","choices":{"A":"Static permanent disruption of anatomical tissue with a fixed barrier","B":"Dynamic neuro-fascial muscle imbalance with altered motor firing","C":"Fracture of a vertebral body with loss of height and sharp focal pain","D":"Ligament tear producing anatomical joint instability on stress testing","E":"Disc herniation compressing an exiting nerve root with a focal deficit"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Functional pathology is a dynamic neuro-fascial muscle imbalance with altered motor firing order and reciprocal inhibition. Structural pathology is static, permanent anatomical tissue damage or disruption such as fracture, ligament tear, or disc herniation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: functional disorders reflect altered neural control rather than tissue destruction. COMLEX: causes include postural collapse and repetitive motor strain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":1,"stem":"Which muscles form the tight, hypertonic group in Lower Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Iliopsoas, transversus abdominis, and thoracolumbar erector spinae","B":"Rectus femoris, gluteus maximus, and thoracolumbar erector spinae","C":"Erector spinae, internal obliques, and the rectus femoris","D":"Iliopsoas, rectus femoris, and thoracolumbar erector spinae","E":"Gluteus medius, iliopsoas, and rectus femoris"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The tight group in LCS is the thoracolumbar extensors (erector spinae) posteriorly and the hip flexors (iliopsoas and rectus femoris) anteriorly. Each other choice includes an abdominal wall or gluteal muscle from the inhibited group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCS pairs tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors with weak abdominals and gluteals. COMLEX: release the tight group before strengthening the weak group.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — the muscular imbalances of the classic upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of muscles is inhibited in Lower Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Rectus abdominis and iliopsoas muscle","B":"Rectus abdominis and gluteus maximus","C":"Gluteus medius and rectus femoris muscle","D":"Internal obliques and erector spinae","E":"Transversus abdominis and iliopsoas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The inhibited, phasic group in LCS is the abdominal wall (rectus abdominis, transversus abdominis, and obliques) and the gluteus maximus and medius. Each other pair combines an inhibited muscle with a tight one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: weak abdominals and gluteals oppose tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors. COMLEX: inhibited gluteals force the hamstrings to take over hip extension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — the muscular imbalances of the classic upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":1,"stem":"Which postural deformity is predicted by Lower Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Posterior pelvic tilt with lumbar flattening","B":"Pelvic obliquity with a convex lumbar scoliosis","C":"Anterior pelvic tilt with lumbar hyperlordosis","D":"Hip hyperextension with knee recurvatum","E":"Posterior pelvic tilt with thoracolumbar kyphosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A tight iliopsoas pulls the anterior pelvis inferiorly into anterior pelvic tilt, and the exaggerated sagittal lumbar curve creates facet compression and L5–S1 shear stress. The other choices describe a posterior tilt, frontal-plane distortion, or hip and knee hyperextension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anterior pelvic tilt increases lumbar lordosis. COMLEX: lumbar hyperlordosis loads the facets and the L5–S1 junction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — the muscular imbalances of the classic upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"During the Supine Active Neck Lift, which sequence is the normal motor firing pattern?","choices":{"A":"The chin nods inward first through deep cervical flexor activation","B":"The jaw thrusts forward through early sternocleidomastoid activation","C":"The head lifts as one unit through simultaneous scalene firing","D":"The shoulders elevate toward the ears before the head lifts","E":"The chin extends upward through suboccipital activation first"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normally the chin nods inward first through activation of the longus colli and longus capitis, followed by smooth cervical flexion. Early jaw thrust with SCM and anterior scalene activation is the abnormal pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: deep cervical flexors initiate cervical flexion. COMLEX: an abnormal pattern indicates deep neck flexor weakness and inhibition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — dynamic movement testing in the crossed syndrome examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"During the Push-Up Movement Pattern Test, scapular winging confirms inhibition and firing failure of which muscle?","choices":{"A":"Upper trapezius","B":"Levator scapulae","C":"Serratus anterior","D":"Pectoralis minor","E":"Sternocleidomastoid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Scapular winging, in which the medial border lifts away from the posterior rib cage, confirms inhibition and firing failure of the serratus anterior. The other muscles are hypertonic members of the tight group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the serratus anterior holds the scapula against the rib cage. COMLEX: winging during push-up points to serratus anterior inhibition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — dynamic movement testing in the crossed syndrome examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":1,"stem":"During a push-up the shoulders elevate toward the ears. What does this shoulder shrug reveal?","choices":{"A":"Hyperactive serratus anterior overcoming a weak upper trapezius and levator scapulae","B":"Inhibited upper trapezius with compensatory firing of the rhomboids and pectoralis minor","C":"Weak sternocleidomastoid with compensatory firing of the anterior scalenes and suboccipitals","D":"Inhibition of the pectoralis minor with compensatory firing of the sternocleidomastoid","E":"Hyperactive upper trapezius and levator scapulae overcoming weak lower scapular anchors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The shrug reveals hyperactive compensation by the upper trapezius and levator scapulae overcoming weak lower scapular anchors. Winging of the medial border is a separate finding that reflects serratus anterior inhibition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight elevators substitute for weak depressors and stabilizers. COMLEX: shrug and winging are different push-up findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 1 — dynamic movement testing in the crossed syndrome examination","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step of the prioritized management plan for the crossed syndromes?","choices":{"A":"Release the hypertonic antagonists with OMT","B":"Reset the facilitated spinal segments with OMT","C":"Re-educate the inhibited agonists with sensorimotor retraining","D":"Integrate functional movement patterns and ergonomics","E":"Strengthen the inhibited agonists with isometric loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The sequence is to release hypertonic antagonists first, then reset facilitated spinal segments, then re-educate and strengthen inhibited agonists, and finally integrate functional movement patterns and ergonomics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: releasing the tight antagonist disarms reciprocal inhibition. COMLEX: strengthening comes after release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":1,"stem":"Which OMT approach treats the tight lower-body muscles in the plan?","choices":{"A":"Muscle energy or counterstrain to the gluteus maximus and the abdominal wall muscles","B":"High-velocity thrust to the lumbar spine followed by stretching of the hamstrings","C":"Suboccipital decompression followed by iliopsoas stretching with the hips flexed","D":"Counterstrain to the gluteus medius followed by sensorimotor retraining of the abdominals","E":"Muscle energy or counterstrain to the iliopsoas and the lumbar erector spinae"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the lower body the tight iliopsoas, rectus femoris, and thoracolumbar erector spinae are treated with muscle energy or counterstrain. The gluteals and abdominal wall are the inhibited muscles, so they are not the target of release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: treat the tight muscles and retrain the weak ones. COMLEX: muscle energy and counterstrain release hypertonic hip flexors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":1,"stem":"Which exercises re-establish motor drive to the gluteus maximus and gluteus medius?","choices":{"A":"Chin-tuck nods and scapular wall slides","B":"Y-to-T raises and chin-tuck nods","C":"Glute bridges and clamshells","D":"Glute stretches with MET to the erector spinae","E":"Scapular wall slides and Y-to-T raises"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glute bridges and clamshells use isometric and isotonic gluteal contractions to re-establish motor drive to the gluteus maximus and medius. Chin-tuck nods, wall slides, and Y-to-T raises retrain the upper body.","boardPrep":"Step 1: motor drive is restored after the antagonist is released. COMLEX: match the exercise to the inhibited muscle group.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has tight pectoral muscles with weak rhomboids and weak middle and lower trapezius muscles. Which neurophysiologic mechanism best accounts for the weakness?","choices":{"A":"Direct nociceptor input from the pectorals paralyzing the scapular retractors at the neuromuscular junction","B":"Loss of descending cortical drive to the scapular retractors from a lesion of the cervical spinal cord","C":"Chronic injury of the long thoracic nerve from repetitive overload of the shoulder girdle muscles","D":"Failure of Golgi tendon organ feedback in the pectorals that enhances firing of the rhomboids and trapezius","E":"Inhibitory signals from hypertonic pectorals relayed by spinal interneurons to antagonist motor neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By Sherrington's law, chronically hypertonic pectoral muscles send continuous Ia inhibitory signals through spinal interneurons to the motor neurons of their direct antagonists, shutting down and weakening the rhomboids, middle trapezius, and lower trapezius.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reciprocal inhibition runs through spinal interneurons. COMLEX: weak scapular retractors often follow tight pectorals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — reciprocal inhibition in a patient with an active mid-pectoral fascial lesion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a hypertonic hip flexor and a weak gluteal extensor with normal lumbar imaging. Which mechanism best explains the gluteal weakness?","choices":{"A":"Reciprocal inhibitory signals from a hypertonic gluteus medius suppressing iliopsoas recruitment","B":"Reciprocal facilitation from the hamstrings enhancing recruitment of the gluteus maximus muscle","C":"Reciprocal inhibitory signals from the hypertonic iliopsoas suppressing gluteus maximus firing","D":"Radiculopathy at L5 with a structural myotomal deficit in the gluteal muscles and hip","E":"Spinal facilitation of the gluteal motor pool causing exaggerated firing and early fatigue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Lower Crossed Syndrome a chronically hypertonic iliopsoas sends reciprocal inhibitory signals to the gluteus maximus motor pool, suppressing gluteal recruitment. Normal imaging fits a functional rather than structural cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hip flexor hypertonicity inhibits the hip extensors. COMLEX: weak glutes with tight hip flexors indicate LCS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — release of the hypertonic antagonist before strengthening the inhibited muscle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the pathophysiological and neurophysiological mechanisms underlying Vladimir Janda's Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes, specifically incorporating Sherrington’s Law of Reciprocal Inhibition and Korr’s model of spinal facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with years of poor desk posture develops marked paraspinal spasm after very light touch. Which mechanism explains the exaggerated response?","choices":{"A":"Subthreshold depolarization of dorsal horn interneurons that amplifies baseline afferent input","B":"Sustained hyperpolarization of dorsal horn interneurons that blocks baseline afferent input from the periphery","C":"Failure of neurotransmitter release at the dorsal horn with loss of segmental motor output","D":"Permanent loss of inhibitory motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord","E":"Demyelination of afferent fibers with slowed conduction of sensory impulses to the cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic nociceptive, proprioceptive, or postural input leaves dorsal horn interneurons in sustained subthreshold depolarization, the facilitated segment. Even minimal stimuli then trigger exaggerated motor efferent responses that maintain paraspinal spasm and sympathetic overactivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a facilitated segment responds out of proportion to its input. COMLEX: facilitation perpetuates tonic hypertonicity and phasic inhibition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — examination findings that reflect facilitation in the crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has chronic neck and shoulder pain with an altered movement pattern and reciprocal weakness. Which cause best fits?","choices":{"A":"Acute cervical disc herniation with nerve root compression","B":"Cervical ligament tear with segmental instability","C":"Dynamic neuro-fascial muscle imbalance","D":"Articular spondylosis with a fixed barrier","E":"Structural scoliosis of the cervicothoracic spine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Altered movement patterns with reciprocal weakness indicate a functional pathology from dynamic neuro-fascial imbalance. Disc herniation, ligament tear, spondylosis, and structural scoliosis are structural etiologies that produce fixed findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nondiagnostic imaging with abnormal movement suggests functional disease. COMLEX: use treatment response and movement testing to classify.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has focal sharp lumbar pain, a fixed articular barrier, and a focal myotomal deficit. Which cause is most consistent?","choices":{"A":"Postural collapse","B":"Repetitive motor strain","C":"Uncorrected crossed syndrome","D":"Acute disc herniation","E":"Spinal facilitation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fixed barriers, localized sharp pain, instability, and focal myotomal deficits indicate structural pathology, and acute disc herniation is a listed structural etiology. Postural collapse, repetitive motor strain, uncorrected crossed syndromes, and spinal facilitation cause functional pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: focal neurologic deficit suggests a structural lesion. COMLEX: structural causes include fractures, ligament tears, acute disc herniations, spondylosis, and structural scoliosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between structural and functional musculoskeletal pathologies based on clinical presentation, physical exam findings, and imaging modalities","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has chronic low back pain with an altered movement pattern. Her lumbar MRI is normal. Why does the normal MRI not exclude a musculoskeletal cause?","choices":{"A":"MRI cannot visualize bone and so misses small fractures","B":"Imaging does not capture dynamic motor firing dysfunction","C":"Reciprocal inhibition is visible on CT but not on MRI","D":"Functional pathology produces focal dermatomal deficits that MRI cannot localize","E":"A normal MRI confirms that no pathology exists in the muscles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Plain films, CT, and MRI are normal or nondiagnostic in functional pathology because they fail to capture dynamic motor firing dysfunction. The functional cause is found through movement testing and examination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: imaging evaluates structure but not neural control. COMLEX: do not let normal imaging end the examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old office worker has a head that sits ahead of the shoulders on lateral inspection. Which finding defines the head position in Upper Crossed Syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Neutral head posture with the ear directly over the acromion tip and the chin elevated","B":"Posterior head carriage with the ear more than 2.5 cm posterior to the acromion tip","C":"Cervical lordosis loss with the occiput resting behind the C7 spinous process","D":"Forward head posture with the ear more than 2.5 cm anterior to the acromion tip","E":"Shoulder protraction with the scapulae more than 3 inches from the midline"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Forward head posture in UCS is defined by the external auditory meatus sitting more than 2.5 cm anterior to the acromion tip. Shoulder protraction is a separate finding, in which the scapulae widen more than 3 inches from the thoracic midline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: forward head posture is defined by the ear sitting more than 2.5 cm anterior to the acromion tip. COMLEX: ergonomic correction aims to place the ear over the acromion tip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — structural examination findings in classic upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with forward head posture keeps his gaze level. Which compensation occurs at the head-neck junction?","choices":{"A":"Atlantoaxial flexion with a C1–C2 rotation","B":"Suboccipital hyperextension with a C0–C1 tilt","C":"Occipitoatlantal flexion with a C0–C1 translation","D":"Subaxial flexion with a C5–C6 anterior tilt","E":"Axial extension with a C2–C3 posterior shear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In UCS a compensatory C0–C1 tilt through suboccipital hyperextension maintains a horizontal eye gaze. The mid-cervical spine shows anterior shear at C4–C5, and a biomechanical fulcrum at C7–T1 forms a dowager's hump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the head-neck junction extends to keep the eyes level over a forward head. COMLEX: suboccipital hypertonicity accompanies forward head posture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — postural distortion in classic upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the specific hypertonic (tonic) and inhibited (phasic) muscle groups involved in both Upper and Lower Crossed Syndromes and predict their corresponding postural deformities","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with Lower Crossed Syndrome has a weak gluteal extensor and a hypertonic hip flexor. Which compensation is expected?","choices":{"A":"Hamstrings take over hip extension and become secondarily tight and strained","B":"Hip flexors lengthen to offset the weak abdominal wall and relieve pelvic tilt","C":"Erector spinae lengthen and weaken to offset the anterior pelvic tilt","D":"Gluteus maximus hypertrophies through reciprocal facilitation from the iliopsoas","E":"Quadriceps take over hip extension and become lengthened"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inhibited gluteals force the hamstrings to take over hip extension, which leads to secondary hamstring tightness and strain. The erector spinae and hip flexors are tight rather than lengthened in LCS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: synergists compensate for a weak prime mover. COMLEX: recurrent hamstring strain can signal gluteal inhibition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — therapy plan for lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"A patient lies supine and lifts the head toward the chest, and the jaw thrusts forward. What does this abnormal pattern confirm?","choices":{"A":"Weakness of the sternocleidomastoid with deep neck flexor overcompensation of the neck","B":"Facilitation of the deep neck flexors with inhibition of the suboccipital muscles","C":"Structural instability of the atlantoaxial articulation with ligamentous laxity of the neck","D":"Weakness of the anterior scalenes with upper trapezius overcompensation","E":"Deep neck flexor weakness and inhibition with sternocleidomastoid overcompensation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An abnormal Supine Active Neck Lift confirms weakness and reciprocal inhibition of the deep neck flexors, with SCM overcompensation that drives mid-cervical shear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overactive superficial flexors substitute for weak deep flexors. COMLEX: an abnormal pattern indicates deep neck flexor weakness and reciprocal inhibition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"During a push-up a patient shows medial scapular border lift and the shoulders rise toward the ears. Which interpretation fits?","choices":{"A":"Rhomboid hypertonicity with lower trapezius overcompensation of the scapular stabilizers","B":"Serratus anterior hypertonicity with pectoralis major inhibition at the shoulder girdle","C":"Serratus anterior inhibition with upper trapezius and levator scapulae overcompensation","D":"Upper trapezius inhibition with levator scapulae weakness and failure of elevation","E":"Pectoralis minor inhibition with anterior scalene overcompensation at the shoulder girdle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Scapular winging confirms inhibition and firing failure of the serratus anterior, and the shoulder shrug reveals hyperactive compensation by the upper trapezius and levator scapulae overcoming weak lower scapular anchors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight elevators dominate over weak stabilizers. COMLEX: winging indicates serratus inhibition and shrug indicates upper trapezius and levator overcompensation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — static and dynamic examination for upper and lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate patient movement patterns using clinical screening maneuvers (e.g., Supine Active Neck Lift, Push-Up Movement Pattern Test) to pinpoint specific motor control dysfunctions","batch":2,"stem":"A patient fails the Supine Active Neck Lift with early superficial flexor dominance. Which cervical consequence does this overcompensation drive?","choices":{"A":"Atlantoaxial rotation at C1–C2 from suboccipital inhibition and weak rotators","B":"Mid-cervical anterior shear at C4–C5 from unopposed sternocleidomastoid pull","C":"Cervicothoracic posterior shear at C7–T1 from deep flexor facilitation of the neck","D":"Upper cervical compression at C2–C3 from longus colli hypertonicity and shortening","E":"Lower cervical extension at C6–C7 from scalene weakness and anterior fatigue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The abnormal neck lift reflects deep neck flexor weakness with SCM overcompensation, and this driving force produces mid-cervical anterior shear at C4–C5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: unopposed superficial flexor pull shears the mid-cervical segments. COMLEX: the neck lift screens for this pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — cervical stress points in the upper crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with Lower Crossed Syndrome has done gluteal strengthening for six weeks without improvement. Which step was most likely skipped?","choices":{"A":"Workstation ergonomics before any exercise","B":"Counterstrain of the gluteus maximus before strengthening","C":"Isometric loading of the abdominal wall before strengthening","D":"Lumbar high-velocity thrust before gluteal exercises","E":"Release of the hypertonic iliopsoas before strengthening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Attempting to strengthen inhibited gluteals while the hypertonic iliopsoas remains unreleased fails because persistent Ia reciprocal inhibitory signals keep suppressing alpha motor neuron firing in the target muscle. Releasing the tight antagonist restores motor recruitment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: motor neuron firing is suppressed while the antagonist stays hypertonic. COMLEX: release before rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — therapy plan for lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with Upper Crossed Syndrome has restricted apices and C7–T4 dysfunction with signs of sympathetic facilitation. Which technique targets these segments in the plan?","choices":{"A":"Thoracic articulation and high-velocity thrust","B":"Suboccipital decompression with cephalad traction","C":"Muscle energy to the pectoralis minor and scalenes","D":"Counterstrain to the iliopsoas and rectus femoris","E":"Chin-tuck nods with sensorimotor retraining"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Step 2 of the plan resets facilitated spinal segments, and thoracic spine articulation with HVLA across C7–T4 and the restricted thoracic apices resets hyperactive muscle spindles, clears joint restrictions, and down-regulates sympathetic facilitation. Suboccipital decompression addresses C0–C1 shear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitated segments drive sympathetic overactivity. COMLEX: thrust technique is applied across C7–T4 and the restricted thoracic apices.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — OMT corrective techniques in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate a prioritized osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) and rehabilitation management plan utilizing Janda’s sequencing rule (releasing hypertonic antagonists prior to strengthening inhibited agonists)","batch":2,"stem":"A software developer has forward head posture at the desk. Which workstation change addresses it?","choices":{"A":"Tilt the screen forward so the head leans toward the text","B":"Move the keyboard farther from the trunk to lengthen the pectoral muscles","C":"Add a lumbar roll to flatten the thoracic kyphosis while seated","D":"Adjust the monitor height so the ear sits directly over the acromion tip","E":"Raise the chair so the hips sit below the level of the knees"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Step 4 includes workstation ergonomics: adjust monitor height so the external auditory meatus sits directly over the acromion tip, eliminating the forward ear shift of more than 2.5 cm, and take standing or movement breaks every 30 minutes to prevent recurrent crossed posture collapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: posture correction reduces chronic loading. COMLEX: ergonomic changes support the OMT and retraining.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — motor retraining and ergonomics in the upper crossed syndrome therapy plan","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"SDL 27 — Upper, Lower Crossed Syndrome (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"In plumb line analysis, where does the line fall relative to the knee in proper alignment?","choices":{"A":"Slightly anterior to the knee joint axis","B":"Slightly posterior to the knee joint axis","C":"Through the tibial tuberosity below the knee","D":"Through the head of the fibula laterally","E":"Through the posterior popliteal crease behind the joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Per the Week 9 rubric, proper alignment places the plumb line through the external auditory meatus and acromion process, through or slightly posterior to the greater trochanter, slightly anterior to the knee joint axis, and slightly anterior to the lateral malleolus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: plumb line alignment is a standing postural screen. COMLEX: the line starts at the external auditory meatus and passes the acromion process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — the screening maneuvers and the motor control faults they reveal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which instruction is correct for the Supine Active Neck Lift?","choices":{"A":"Lift the head and shoulders together while extending the chin upward","B":"Lift the head with the chin protruded and hold for 30 seconds","C":"Lift the head while rotating toward the symptomatic side","D":"Lift the head without the shoulders while keeping the chin tucked","E":"Lift the shoulders first and then flex the head forward"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The patient starts supine with the head on the table and the chin tucked, then lifts the head without lifting the shoulders while the chin stays tucked. The position is held for 5 seconds, and the head is lowered to the table slowly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the deep cervical flexors should initiate the lift. COMLEX: a chin that protrudes shows substitution by superficial flexors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — the screening maneuvers and the motor control faults they reveal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Per the Week 9 rubric, how is the patient positioned for the Push-Up Test?","choices":{"A":"Prone with the arms at the sides and the palms turned upward","B":"Standing facing a wall with the hands at shoulder height","C":"Push-up position with the knees allowed on the ground","D":"Supine with the shoulders flexed to 90 degrees overhead","E":"Quadruped with the elbows resting on the table surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient is in the push-up position, and the knees may rest on the ground. The patient then performs a slow push-up while the DO looks for the positive signs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the test observes shoulder girdle stabilization under load. COMLEX: the patient performs a slow push-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — the screening maneuvers and the motor control faults they reveal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"In pectoralis muscle energy, how is the patient's shoulder positioned?","choices":{"A":"Flexed to 90 degrees with the palm on the opposite shoulder","B":"Adducted across the chest with the elbow fully extended","C":"Extended behind the trunk with the palm facing upward","D":"Abducted with the back of the hand on the forehead","E":"Internally rotated with the hand resting at the lumbar spine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For pectoralis muscle energy the shoulder is moved into abduction with the back of the hand placed on the forehead, and the DO then takes up the barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectoralis is lengthened by abducting the arm with the hand on the forehead. COMLEX: the patient contracts for 3 to 5 seconds against the DO's resistance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — the prioritized OMT and rehabilitation sequence built on Janda’s rule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"Which patient and physician positions are used for pectoralis muscle energy?","choices":{"A":"Patient prone with the DO standing at the head of the table beside the neck","B":"Patient seated with the DO standing directly behind the shoulders of the patient","C":"Patient supine or lateral recumbent with the DO on the treated side","D":"Patient supine with the DO seated at the foot of the table","E":"Patient standing with the DO kneeling in front of the patient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient is supine or in lateral recumbent position and the DO stands on the side being treated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: positioning allows barrier engagement of the tight pectorals. COMLEX: the process is repeated at least 3 times.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — the prioritized OMT and rehabilitation sequence built on Janda’s rule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"Which sequence describes the chin tuck nod exercise?","choices":{"A":"Flex the cervical spine first and then tuck the chin at maximum flexion","B":"Flex the OA joint to tuck the chin and then flex the cervical spine","C":"Extend the OA joint and then extend the cervical spine while holding","D":"Protrude the chin and then flex the cervical spine against resistance","E":"Tuck the chin and then rotate the cervical spine while holding the tuck"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The patient flexes the OA joint to tuck the chin, flexes the cervical spine while holding the tuck, holds maximum flexion for 5 seconds, and returns to neutral with the chin still tucked, repeating 10 to 12 times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exercise targets deep cervical flexor activation. COMLEX: it can be done standing, seated, or supine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — the prioritized OMT and rehabilitation sequence built on Janda’s rule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"How is the patient placed for iliopsoas muscle energy?","choices":{"A":"Supine at the edge of the table in the Thomas position","B":"Prone with the hips positioned near the edge of the table","C":"Lateral recumbent with both hips flexed to the chest","D":"Seated with the feet supported on a stool","E":"Supine with the knees bent and the feet flat on the table"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Iliopsoas muscle energy is performed supine at the edge of the table in the Thomas position, with the DO standing next to the table on the side being treated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the iliopsoas is a tight hip flexor in lower crossed syndrome. COMLEX: the hip is positioned into full extension to find the barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the hypertonic and inhibited muscle groups of lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"In iliopsoas muscle energy, which contraction does the patient perform against the DO's resistance?","choices":{"A":"Hip extension for 3 to 5 seconds","B":"Hip flexion for 3 to 5 seconds","C":"Hip abduction for 3 to 5 seconds","D":"Knee extension for 3 to 5 seconds","E":"Hip adduction for 3 to 5 seconds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The DO positions the hip into full extension and the patient performs a flexion contraction for 3 to 5 seconds against the DO's resistance. The DO pauses 1 to 2 seconds before taking up the new barrier, and the process is repeated at least 3 times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscle energy uses a contraction by the shortened muscle against resistance. COMLEX: the contraction is made by the muscle being treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the hypertonic and inhibited muscle groups of lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":1,"stem":"In lumbar spine muscle energy, which contraction does the patient perform after the DO flexes the lumbar spine to the barrier?","choices":{"A":"Flexion for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance","B":"Rotation for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance","C":"Sidebending for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance","D":"Hip flexion for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance","E":"Extension for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The DO flexes the lumbar spine to the barrier, the patient performs an extension contraction for 3 to 5 seconds against resistance, the DO pauses 1 to 2 seconds before taking up the new barrier, and the process is repeated at least 3 times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the DO flexes the lumbar spine to the barrier and the patient contracts into extension. COMLEX: position is seated or lateral recumbent with the DO standing behind the patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 3 — the hypertonic and inhibited muscle groups of lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A physician performs the prone hip extension test with the patient prone, hips near the table edge, and a guarding hand over the buttock. At what point should the test stop?","choices":{"A":"When the knee reaches 90 degrees of flexion during the hip lift","B":"When the pelvis lifts or another compensatory pattern begins","C":"When the patient reports tenderness at the iliac crest region","D":"When the hip reaches 30 degrees beyond neutral extension range","E":"When the lumbar spine reaches full extension on the table surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The test stops when the pelvis lifts from the table or a compensatory pattern begins. The DO assesses whether the hip can extend to neutral or beyond.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the test stops at the first compensatory pattern. COMLEX: the DO assesses whether the hip extends to neutral or beyond.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — screening maneuvers for motor control faults","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A student sets up the static standing alignment examination for the sagittal alignment check. Which setup is correct per the Week 9 rubric?","choices":{"A":"Standing posterior view with the plumb line dropped from the occiput","B":"Seated lateral view with the plumb line placed behind the patient","C":"Prone with the plumb line suspended over the pelvis","D":"Standing lateral view with the plumb line placed at the patient's side","E":"Standing anterior view with the plumb line placed in front of the sternum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The plumb line analysis is done with the patient standing in lateral view and the plumb line placed at the side of the patient. Alignment is then checked from the external auditory meatus to the lateral malleolus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sagittal alignment is evaluated from the side. COMLEX: forward head and tilted pelvis show up on a lateral plumb line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — postural deformities predicted by the crossed syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate Static and Dynamic Examination for Upper and Lower Crossed Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"Which set lists all four positive signs recognized on the Push-Up Test in the Week 9 rubric?","choices":{"A":"Scapular winging, shoulder elevation, forward shoulder movement, and loss of spine alignment","B":"Scapular retraction, shoulder elevation, backward shoulder movement, and loss of spine alignment","C":"Scapular winging, shoulder depression, forward shoulder movement, and elbow flaring beyond the trunk","D":"Scapular winging, shoulder elevation, elbow hyperextension, and drop of the head toward the floor","E":"Scapular retraction, shoulder depression, forward shoulder movement, and pelvic sagging toward the floor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric lists scapular winging, shoulder elevation, the shoulder moving forward, and loss of spine alignment as positive signs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: each sign reflects a failure of shoulder girdle or trunk stabilization. COMLEX: winging points to serratus anterior weakness and shoulder elevation to upper trapezius and levator scapulae substitution.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — interpreting the push-up findings","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old desk worker has forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Which treatment plan fits the Week 9 rubric?","choices":{"A":"Iliopsoas muscle energy and lumbar erector spinae soft tissue followed by glute bridges and dead bugs","B":"Suboccipital release and pectoralis energy followed by chin tuck nods and wall slides","C":"Pectoralis muscle energy and suboccipital release followed by glute bridges and dead bugs","D":"Suboccipital release and iliopsoas muscle energy followed by chin tuck nods and wall slides","E":"Chin tuck nods and scapular wall slides followed by pectoralis muscle energy and suboccipital release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The upper crossed syndrome plan releases the tight structures first, with suboccipital release and pectoralis muscle energy, and then retrains the weak muscles with chin tuck nods and scapular wall slides. Iliopsoas work and glute bridges belong to the lower crossed plan, and retraining before release reverses Janda's rule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: release the tight muscles before strengthening the weak ones. COMLEX: match each technique to the syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Janda's sequencing rule in the management plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A student sets up scapular wall slides. Which setup is correct?","choices":{"A":"Standing 30 cm from the wall with the chin protruded and the hands resting on the hips","B":"Standing with the heels against the wall and the lumbar spine arched away from it","C":"Full spine and occiput contact with the wall with the chin tucked and the core engaged","D":"Seated with the occiput forward of the wall and the shoulders adducted at the sides of the trunk","E":"Standing with the hands pressed on the wall and the elbows fully extended overhead and locked"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient stands with the pelvis, lumbar spine, thoracic spine, and occiput in contact with the wall, tucks the chin, engages the core, abducts the shoulders to 90 degrees with the forearms on the wall, and slides into full abduction while breathing deeply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: wall contact gives feedback on posture. COMLEX: the exercise fires the serratus anterior and the middle and lower trapezius.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — re-educating inhibited agonists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Upper Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A student gives a patient a 10 second pectoral contraction with no pause and repeats the cycle once. Which correction fits the rubric?","choices":{"A":"Contract for 3 to 5 seconds then pause 1 to 2 seconds and repeat at least 3 times","B":"Contract for 10 seconds with a 5 second pause and repeat at least twice more","C":"Contract for 3 to 5 seconds with no pause and repeat 10 to 12 times","D":"Contract for 3 to 5 seconds and hold the new barrier for 30 seconds before releasing","E":"Contract for 20 seconds with a 10 second pause and stop after one repetition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In pectoralis muscle energy the patient contracts for 3 to 5 seconds while the DO resists, the DO pauses 1 to 2 seconds before taking up the new barrier, and the process is repeated at least 3 times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rubric timing is a 3 to 5 second contraction, a 1 to 2 second pause, and then a new barrier. COMLEX: the same timing applies to iliopsoas and lumbar muscle energy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — release of the hypertonic antagonists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A patient performing glute bridges arches the low back and fires the hamstrings strongly. What should the DO do?","choices":{"A":"Extend the hold to 30 seconds to fatigue the hamstrings","B":"Instruct the patient to tilt the pelvis anteriorly during the lift","C":"Stop the exercise and apply a lumbar thrust technique","D":"Move the patient to prone and repeat the extension","E":"Assess and correct the hyperextension and hamstring firing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For glute bridges the patient lies supine with knees bent and feet flat, tilts the pelvis posteriorly, extends the hips through glute contraction, and holds 5 seconds for 10 to 12 repetitions. The DO assesses and corrects hyperextension or hamstring firing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inhibited gluteals lead to hamstring overcompensation. COMLEX: coaching the pelvic tilt keeps the gluteals driving the movement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — hamstring overcompensation in lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A student treats the lumbar erector spinae with soft tissue and myofascial release. Which approach fits the rubric?","choices":{"A":"Supine positioning with sustained inhibition at the thoracolumbar junction and spinous processes","B":"Prone positioning with a high-velocity thrust through lumbar rotation to the barrier","C":"Prone with parallel or perpendicular kneading and treatment of the fascial bind","D":"Lateral recumbent positioning with muscle energy using a contraction at the lumbar barrier","E":"Seated positioning with rhythmic compression of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar region"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient is prone and the provider performs parallel or perpendicular kneading, then assesses the fascia for bind and ease and treats the bind with a direct or indirect approach.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the erector spinae are tight in lower crossed syndrome. COMLEX: assess the fascia for bind or ease and then treat the bind directly or indirectly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — releasing the hypertonic muscles before strengthening","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Demonstrate therapy plan for Lower Crossed Syndrome, including OMT corrective techniques and motor retraining exercises","batch":2,"stem":"A student teaches the dead bug exercise. Which instruction is correct?","choices":{"A":"Arch the lumbar spine, tighten the abdomen, and extend the opposite arm and leg","B":"Flatten the lumbar spine, tighten the abdomen, and extend the same-side arm and leg","C":"Tighten the abdomen and extend both arms and both legs together","D":"Flatten the lumbar spine, tighten the abdomen, and extend the opposite arm and leg","E":"Lift the pelvis off the table while extending the opposite arm and leg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The patient lies supine with knees bent, feet flat, and shoulders flexed to 90 degrees, flattens the lumbar spine onto the table, tightens the abdomen, and extends the opposite arm and leg before repeating on the other side, for 10 to 12 repetitions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exercise trains the weak abdominal wall. COMLEX: a flattened lumbar spine prevents hyperlordosis during the movement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — the inhibited abdominal wall in lower crossed syndrome","isHighYield":false}]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"SDL 28 — Osteopathic Reflexes","questions":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Whose pioneering animal experiments in 1907 established the neurophysiologic basis of both viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes?","choices":{"A":"Thomas Brown","B":"John Hilton","C":"Louisa Burns","D":"Frank Chapman","E":"Myron Beal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Louisa Burns, DO, conducted the pioneering animal experiments of 1907 that established the neurophysiologic basis for both reflex types. Thomas Brown described spinal irritation in 1838, John Hilton described segmentally related tender points in 1863, Frank Chapman introduced reflex points in the 1930s, and Myron Beal validated palpatory testing in the 1980s.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the experimental basis for viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes is segmental spinal cord integration. COMLEX: Burns established that the two reflex arcs run in opposite directions between viscera and soma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — the research basis for viscerosomatic reflexes and spinal facilitation, applied to cardiac disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Which findings did Nicholas and colleagues report in 1985 as highly prevalent in patients with acute myocardial infarction?","choices":{"A":"Left-sided T6–T9 dysfunction with reduced paraspinal skin temperature","B":"Right-sided T1–T5 dysfunction with unchanged paraspinal skin temperature","C":"Right-sided T10–L2 dysfunction with elevated paraspinal skin temperature","D":"Left-sided T1–T5 dysfunction with elevated paraspinal skin temperature","E":"Bilateral T6–T9 dysfunction with increased paraspinal skin dryness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nicholas et al. documented a high prevalence of left-sided T1–T5 somatic dysfunction and elevated paraspinal skin temperature in acute myocardial infarction. This matches the segmental sympathetic representation of the heart and the earlier findings of Burns and Beal that visceral disease produces segmentally related paraspinal tissue changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac sympathetic afferents enter the cord at T1–T5, so cardiac pain and tissue changes localize there. COMLEX: left-sided T1–T5 paraspinal findings with warmth support a cardiac visceral contribution to somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — the research basis for viscerosomatic reflexes and spinal facilitation, applied to cardiac disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"How did Frank Chapman and Charles Owens describe Chapman’s reflex points when they introduced them in the 1930s?","choices":{"A":"Painless bony nodules reflecting skeletal, vascular, and endocrine disturbance","B":"Tender nodular soft-tissue masses reflecting visceral, autonomic, and lymphatic disturbance","C":"Diffuse paraspinal muscle hypertonicity reflecting somatic, motor, and proprioceptive disturbance","D":"Transient skin blanching reflecting cutaneous, sudomotor, and vasomotor disturbance","E":"Fixed joint restrictions reflecting articular, ligamentous, and fascial disturbance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chapman and Owens established Chapman’s reflex points as localized, tender, nodular soft-tissue masses that reflect visceral, autonomic, and lymphatic disturbance. They are soft-tissue findings rather than bony nodules, and they are localized rather than diffuse muscle hypertonicity or fixed joint restrictions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chapman points are reflex tissue changes tied to a visceral organ. COMLEX: palpate for small tender nodular tissue changes and correlate their location with the associated viscus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — the research basis for viscerosomatic reflexes and spinal facilitation, applied to cardiac disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"After entering the spinal cord through the posterior root, on which neurons do primary visceral sensory axons make their first synapse?","choices":{"A":"Alpha motor neurons of the ventral horn","B":"Preganglionic neurons of the intermediolateral cell column","C":"Postganglionic neurons of the paravertebral ganglia","D":"Second-order neurons of the dorsal column nuclei","E":"Wide dynamic range interneurons of the dorsal horn"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary visceral sensory axons enter the dorsal root and synapse on wide dynamic range interneurons in laminae I to VII of the dorsal horn, where visceral and somatic fibers from the same segment converge. Alpha motor neurons and intermediolateral preganglionic neurons are the efferent targets of the facilitated interneurons, not the first synapse of the afferent limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: convergence of visceral and somatic afferents on shared dorsal horn neurons underlies referred pain. COMLEX: this shared pool explains why visceral disease produces segmentally related somatic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement best defines segmental facilitation?","choices":{"A":"Sustained suprathreshold firing of alpha motor neurons across several spinal segments","B":"Chronic hyperpolarization of dorsal horn interneurons that raises the segmental firing threshold","C":"Chronic subthreshold depolarization with hyperexcitability of interneurons in one spinal segment","D":"Transient loss of afferent traffic from the viscera after organ capsule stretch","E":"Permanent destruction of interneurons within a spinal segment after prolonged ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Segmental facilitation is a state of chronic subthreshold depolarization and hyperexcitability of spinal cord interneurons within a specific segment. Ongoing nociceptive bombardment holds the interneurons near their firing threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitation is a property of spinal cord interneurons rather than of the viscus. COMLEX: a facilitated segment is the neurologic basis for persistent paraspinal tissue texture change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":1,"stem":"In an acute viscerosomatic reflex, which palpable body wall findings result from autonomic efferent output through the intermediolateral cell column?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction and splinting of paraspinal muscles","B":"Paraspinal heat, moisture, and localized edema","C":"Paraspinal cooling, dryness, and tissue atrophy","D":"Hypotonicity of the intercostal and paraspinal muscles","E":"Hyperesthesia of dermatomes with weakness of myotomes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Facilitated interneurons drive two efferent pathways. Autonomic output through the intermediolateral cell column alters blood vessel tone and sweat gland activity, producing palpable paraspinal heat, moisture or skin drag, and edema. Somatic output through alpha motor neurons produces tonic contraction, splinting, and hypertonicity of paraspinal and intercostal muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the intermediolateral cell column holds sympathetic preganglionic neurons. COMLEX: tissue texture change in a segment reflects both somatic and autonomic efferent limbs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":1,"stem":"Which respiratory changes accompany sustained sympathetic hyperactivity?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilation with more viscous secretions","B":"Bronchoconstriction with thinner secretions","C":"Bronchodilation with greater mucus volume","D":"Bronchoconstriction with unchanged viscosity","E":"Bronchoconstriction with reduced mucus volume"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sustained sympathicotonia causes bronchodilation and decreases overall mucus volume but increases the viscosity of respiratory secretions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta-2 stimulation dilates bronchi. COMLEX: thickened secretions with sympathetic tone are one reason osteopathic care of respiratory disease also addresses autonomic balance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal effect of sustained sympathetic hyperactivity contributes to elevated blood volume and pressure?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal vascular resistance with greater urine output","B":"Increased urinary sodium excretion with falling blood volume","C":"Decreased tubular water reabsorption with greater diuresis","D":"Dilation of the renal arterioles with higher glomerular perfusion","E":"Raised renal vascular resistance and sodium retention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Increased sympathetic tone raises renal vascular resistance and enhances tubular sodium and water reabsorption, which elevates blood volume and pressure. The other choices describe renal vasodilation, or increased sodium and water loss, which would lower volume and pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal sympathetic activity stimulates tubular sodium reabsorption and constricts renal arterioles. COMLEX: chronic sympathicotonia contributes to hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":1,"stem":"Increased moisture and drag felt on the skin over a facilitated paraspinal segment reflects heightened activity of which sympathetic function?","choices":{"A":"Pilomotor inhibition","B":"Vasodilator activity","C":"Lacrimal secretion","D":"Sudomotor activity","E":"Salivary secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sustained sympathetic activity drives sudomotor activity, which produces sweating and increased skin drag, and pilomotor activity. It also stimulates adrenal medullary release of catecholamines and increases the basal metabolic rate. Vasodilator activity is not the function that produces sweating, and the source assigns the parasympathetic outflow to lacrimal and salivary glands to cranial nerves VII and IX.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic postganglionic fibers to sweat glands are cholinergic, but the output is part of sympathetic activity. COMLEX: increased moisture and skin drag over a segment suggest sympathetic facilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic dominant symptoms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Which spinal segments provide sympathetic innervation to the stomach through the celiac ganglion?","choices":{"A":"T5–T9","B":"T1–T5","C":"T9–T11","D":"T10–T11","E":"T12–L2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The stomach receives sympathetic innervation from T5 to T9 through the celiac ganglion. T1 to T5 supplies the heart, head, and neck, T9 to T11 supplies the small intestine, T10 to T11 supplies the kidneys, ureters, and ascending colon, and T12 to L2 supplies the distal colon and pelvic organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the celiac ganglion supplies the foregut. COMLEX: palpate T5–T9 paraspinal tissue for gastric viscerosomatic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — mapping viscerosomatic reflex arcs to the autonomic spinal segments and the vagus nerve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves carry the parasympathetic outflow to the ocular structures, lacrimal glands, and salivary glands?","choices":{"A":"CN V, VII, and XII","B":"CN IV, VI, and X","C":"CN III, VII, and IX","D":"CN IX, X, and XI","E":"CN III, IX, and XII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cranial division of the parasympathetic system travels with cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X. Cranial nerves III, VII, and IX innervate the ocular structures, the lacrimal glands, and the salivary and mucosal glands of the head and neck, and cranial nerve X supplies the thoracic and abdominal organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cranial parasympathetic ganglia relay fibers of cranial nerves III, VII, and IX. COMLEX: cranial and sacral parasympathetic outflow complements the thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — mapping viscerosomatic reflex arcs to the autonomic spinal segments and the vagus nerve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic outflow to the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum is relayed through which ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric ganglion","B":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","C":"Aorticorenal ganglion","D":"Superior cervical ganglion","E":"Celiac sympathetic ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The distal third of the transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum receive T12 to L2 sympathetic innervation through the inferior mesenteric ganglion. The superior mesenteric ganglion supplies the small intestine, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, and the celiac ganglion supplies the foregut organs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the prevertebral ganglia correspond to the three major abdominal arteries. COMLEX: lower thoracolumbar findings at T12–L2 accompany distal colon and pelvic visceral dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — mapping viscerosomatic reflex arcs to the autonomic spinal segments and the vagus nerve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Which reflex travels from a somatic structure through the spinal cord to alter the function of a viscus?","choices":{"A":"Viscerosomatic reflex","B":"Viscerovisceral reflex","C":"Somatosomatic reflex","D":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex","E":"Somatovisceral reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In a somatovisceral reflex the pathway runs from soma to the central nervous system to a viscus, so a somatic dysfunction alters organ function. A viscerosomatic reflex runs from viscus to soma, and a viscerovisceral reflex runs from one viscus to another.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the first word of the reflex name gives its origin and the second gives its target. COMLEX: name the reflex by its origin and its target before choosing where to direct treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — differentiating viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Irritation or expansion of one organ that alters the function of a different organ describes which reflex?","choices":{"A":"Viscerosomatic reflex","B":"Somatovisceral reflex","C":"Somatosomatic reflex","D":"Viscerovisceral reflex","E":"Cutaneous withdrawal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The viscerovisceral reflex runs from one viscus through the ganglia or spinal cord to another viscus and appears as altered organ blood flow, vital sign changes in heart rate, blood pressure, or respiratory rate, or altered smooth muscle tone in a distant organ.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerovisceral reflexes use autonomic ganglia and spinal pathways without a somatic target. COMLEX: look for a distant organ responding to a primary visceral problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — differentiating viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":1,"stem":"Which treatment approach applies to a viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Treat the primary visceral pathology and use OMT to break the positive feedback loop","B":"Treat the primary somatic dysfunction and expect OMT to resolve the visceral response","C":"Treat both involved organs and expect OMT to normalize their interaction","D":"Treat the paraspinal tissue changes and defer evaluation of the visceral pathology","E":"Treat the overlying skin findings and expect the visceral disease to resolve spontaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a viscerosomatic reflex the primary problem is visceral disease, so the underlying pathology is treated and OMT to the soma breaks the positive feedback loop and relieves symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerosomatic findings are a sign of visceral disease and do not replace its evaluation. COMLEX: OMT is adjunctive and relieves symptoms while the underlying pathology is treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — differentiating viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A research team stimulates the visceral pericardium and myocardium of an animal and records the muscular response of the body wall. Which finding would the team expect?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the second through sixth intercostal and paraspinal muscles","B":"Contraction of the lumbar paraspinal, gluteal, and psoas muscles","C":"Flaccid paralysis of the diaphragm and the scalene muscles of the neck","D":"Atrophy of the intercostal muscles with progressive loss of paraspinal muscle tone","E":"Spasm of the rectus abdominis and abdominal oblique muscles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Stimulating the visceral pericardium and myocardium elicited contraction of the second through sixth intercostal and paraspinal muscles, corresponding to T2–T5 segments. This viscerosomatic response was the experimental basis for the reflex that runs from viscus to soma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac afferents enter the cord at upper thoracic levels. COMLEX: cardiac disease should prompt a search for upper thoracic and rib findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — the neurophysiologic mechanism of viscerosomatic reflexes and facilitation for cardiac afferent input","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"In an experiment, the deep paravertebral muscles of an animal are stimulated while the abdominal and thoracic organs are observed. Which response would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Visceral vasodilation with reddening of color and unchanged heart rate and blood pressure","B":"Visceral vasoconstriction with lightening of color and unchanged heart rate and blood pressure","C":"Visceral vasoconstriction with lightening of color and altered heart rate and blood pressure","D":"Visceral vasodilation with reddening of color and altered heart rate and blood pressure","E":"Visceral engorgement with darkening of color and a lowered basal metabolic rate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deep paravertebral muscle stimulation caused visceral vasoconstriction, seen as lightening of organ color, along with altered heart rate and blood pressure. This somatovisceral response showed that somatic input can change visceral function through autonomic pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic afferent input can alter sympathetic outflow to organs. COMLEX: somatic dysfunction can alter visceral function, which is a rationale for OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — somatic restriction, facilitation, and increased sympathetic output altering cardiovascular function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the history of and research significance of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has had intermittent exertional chest pain for 3 months. Palpation shows thickened, tender paraspinal tissue from T2 through T5. Which property of segments like these was demonstrated by quantitative studies of human motoneuron pools in 1947?","choices":{"A":"Acute reflex suppression with preserved sudomotor and vasomotor activity","B":"Chronic motoneuron fatigue with absent sudomotor and vasomotor activity","C":"Intermittent reflex hyperexcitability with unchanged sudomotor and vasomotor activity","D":"Permanent motoneuron loss with exaggerated sudomotor and vasomotor activity","E":"Chronic reflex hyperexcitability with altered sudomotor and vasomotor activity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Denslow, Korr, and Krems showed that facilitated spinal segments display chronic reflex hyperexcitability together with altered sudomotor and vasomotor activity, which accounts for palpable tissue and skin changes. Myron Beal later validated palpatory testing for somatic dysfunction in cardiovascular and pulmonary disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sudomotor and vasomotor changes reflect autonomic efferent output from the facilitated segment. COMLEX: heat, moisture, and texture change over a segment are objective signs of facilitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with acute pancreatitis has severe epigastric pain. On examination, the paraspinal muscles at T6 through T10 are hypertonic, and the overlying skin is moist and warm. Which mechanism best explains the finding in the paraspinal muscles?","choices":{"A":"Direct spread of pancreatic inflammation into the paraspinal muscles","B":"Retrograde conduction of the visceral pain signal along the sympathetic chain","C":"Reduced afferent traffic from the paraspinal muscles to the dorsal horn","D":"Facilitated dorsal horn interneurons driving alpha motor neuron output","E":"Primary muscle injury from sustained postural strain of the thoracic spine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral inflammation drives visceral afferents onto wide dynamic range interneurons that also receive somatic input from the same segment. The facilitated interneurons fire through alpha motor neurons, causing tonic contraction and hypertonicity of the paraspinal muscles, and through the intermediolateral cell column, causing heat and moisture. The other choices do not describe the segmental reflex pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: convergence at the dorsal horn explains referred pain and reflex muscle splinting. COMLEX: segmental paraspinal hypertonicity with skin changes points toward a visceral source.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with a chronic gastric ulcer has had recurrent thoracic paraspinal spasm at T5 through T8 for months. Minor postural strain and emotional stress now trigger severe spasm at the same level. Which concept best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Permanent damage to the segment abolishes its normal inhibitory control from higher centers","B":"Direct spread of the ulcer’s inflammation into the paraspinal muscles","C":"A facilitated segment responds to any afferent input with an exaggerated efferent response","D":"Persistent muscle fiber hypertrophy increases the contractile force of the muscle","E":"Chronic suppression of the sympathetic outflow lowers the muscle’s activation threshold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once a segment is facilitated, interneurons held near threshold by ongoing visceral or somatic nociceptive bombardment respond to additional afferent input from any source, including minor postural strain, distant spinous process pressure, or emotional stress, with an exaggerated low-threshold efferent response. Korr described this as a neurologic lens that amplifies baseline afferent input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ongoing nociceptive bombardment keeps interneurons near threshold. COMLEX: treat the facilitated segment because it perpetuates somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — facilitation and the neurologic lens concept","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the neurologic origins of viscerosomatic reflexes and the concept of segmental facilitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has chronic visceral disease with persistent upper thoracic paraspinal spasm. Her symptoms persist and escalate over weeks despite no change in the disease. Which process best explains the progression?","choices":{"A":"A self-perpetuating loop of somatic nociceptive input returning to the facilitated segment","B":"Progressive failure of the dorsal horn neurons to relay visceral afferent signals to higher centers","C":"Direct compression of the dorsal root by the spastic paraspinal muscles","D":"Spread of the visceral disease along the paravertebral ganglia into the muscles","E":"Declining sensitivity of facilitated interneurons as nociceptive input continues"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Visceral disease produces spinal facilitation, which causes paraspinal spasm and tissue texture change. These somatic findings activate nociceptors and send further nociceptive signals back to the same facilitated segment, which increases sympathetic output to both organ and soma in a self-sustaining positive feedback loop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: positive feedback loops maintain facilitation. COMLEX: OMT to the soma is intended to interrupt the loop.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has chronic upper thoracic somatic dysfunction with sustained sympathicotonia. Which combination of cardiovascular changes would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Bradycardia with slowed AV nodal conduction and decreased afterload","B":"Tachycardia with accelerated AV nodal conduction and increased afterload","C":"Tachycardia with prolonged AV nodal conduction and decreased contractility","D":"Unchanged heart rate with arteriolar vasodilation and lower peripheral resistance","E":"Bradycardia with increased contractility and systemic arteriolar vasodilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic sympathetic hyperactivity raises heart rate, accelerates SA and AV nodal conduction, increases myocardial contractility, constricts systemic arterioles, and increases total peripheral resistance, blood pressure, and cardiac afterload.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction raises afterload and beta-1 stimulation raises rate and contractility. COMLEX: sympathetic hypertonicity of the upper thoracic segments is relevant to cardiac patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — increased sympathetic output and impaired cardiovascular function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic low thoracic somatic dysfunction reports difficulty starting urination and incomplete emptying. A urologic workup excludes outlet obstruction. Which pairing of sympathetic effects predisposes him to retention?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-mediated trigone contraction with muscarinic detrusor relaxation","B":"Trigone relaxation with detrusor contraction through muscarinic receptors","C":"Alpha-mediated trigone contraction with beta-mediated detrusor relaxation","D":"Trigone and detrusor contraction through alpha adrenergic receptors","E":"Trigone and detrusor relaxation through beta adrenergic receptors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sustained sympathetic activity contracts the bladder trigone through alpha adrenoceptors and relaxes the detrusor through beta adrenoceptors. This combination keeps the outlet closed while the bladder wall is relaxed and contracts poorly, predisposing to urinary retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic tone favors urine storage. COMLEX: T12–L2 viscerosomatic findings accompany bladder dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — sympathetic dominant symptoms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the effects of increased sympathetic tone on an organ","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has chronic stress and sustained sympathicotonia. She reports constipation and abdominal bloating after meals. Which gastrointestinal changes best explain her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated peristalsis, contracted sphincters, and reduced digestive secretions","B":"Inhibited peristalsis, relaxed sphincters, and increased digestive secretions","C":"Accelerated peristalsis, relaxed sphincters, and splanchnic vasodilation","D":"Inhibited peristalsis, contracted sphincters, and accelerated gastric emptying","E":"Inhibited peristalsis, contracted sphincters, and reduced digestive secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic hyperactivity inhibits smooth muscle peristalsis and motility, contracts sphincters, decreases digestive gland secretions, and constricts splanchnic vessels. Together these slow transit and cause constipation and bloating.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic tone slows gastrointestinal transit. COMLEX: abdominal ganglion inhibition aims to reduce sympathetic outflow to the gut.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — sympathetic dominant symptoms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has sudden severe right flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a 5 mm stone at the right ureteropelvic junction. Which prevertebral ganglia carry the sympathetic innervation of the kidney and upper ureter?","choices":{"A":"Stellate and inferior mesenteric ganglia","B":"Superior mesenteric and aorticorenal ganglia","C":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion and inferior hypogastric plexus","D":"Superior and middle cervical ganglia","E":"Stellate ganglion and the sacral splanchnic nerves"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The kidneys, ureters, and gonads receive sympathetic innervation at T10 to T11 through the superior mesenteric and aorticorenal ganglia, so upper urinary tract disease produces viscerosomatic findings at those levels. The celiac ganglion supplies the foregut organs, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion and inferior hypogastric plexus supply the distal colon and the bladder and other pelvic organs at T12 to L2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal and ureteral sympathetic fibers relay through the superior mesenteric and aorticorenal ganglia. COMLEX: examine T10–T11 paraspinal tissue in suspected renal or ureteral disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis has tissue texture changes at the occipitoatlantal joint, C1, and C2. Which pathway most likely mediates these reflected findings?","choices":{"A":"Vagus nerve afferents","B":"Greater splanchnic afferents","C":"Pelvic splanchnic afferents","D":"Hypogastric nerve afferents","E":"Phrenic nerve afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vagal afferent irritation presents as tissue texture abnormalities at OA, C1, and C2. The vagus supplies parasympathetic outflow to the pancreas and many other abdominal organs, whereas the splanchnic and hypogastric nerves are sympathetic or sacral pathways that reflect to thoracolumbar or sacral levels, and phrenic afferents localize to C3 to C5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the vagus nerve carries visceral afferents and parasympathetic efferents. COMLEX: include the upper cervical region in the screen when vagal-mediated visceral disease is suspected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Map out both the sympathetic and parasympathetic visceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has chronic constipation from sigmoid colon dysfunction. Palpation shows tissue changes in the paraspinal tissues around the sacrum. Which pathway most likely mediates these reflected findings?","choices":{"A":"Greater splanchnic nerves","B":"Lumbar splanchnic nerves","C":"Vagus nerve branches","D":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","E":"Phrenic nerve branches"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sacral parasympathetic division, S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves or nervi erigentes, supplies the distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon, the rectum, and the bladder. Irritation presents as palpable tissue changes around the sacrum and S2–S4 paraspinal and pelvic tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers to the hindgut and pelvis. COMLEX: examine the sacrum when distal colon or pelvic organ dysfunction is suspected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — correlating specific visceral pathology with target autonomic spinal segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has acute myocardial infarction. Palpation shows multi-segmental paraspinal muscle splinting with heat and moisture, and the intercostal muscles are also involved. Which reflex best describes these findings?","choices":{"A":"Somatovisceral reflex","B":"Viscerovisceral reflex","C":"Somatosomatic reflex","D":"Crossed extensor reflex","E":"Viscerosomatic reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Internal organ ischemia produces a viscerosomatic reflex with multi-segmental paraspinal tissue changes over 2 to 5 levels, muscle splinting, heat, moisture, Chapman points, and costal linkage that involves both paraspinal and intercostal muscles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerosomatic reflexes begin with visceral nociception. COMLEX: a multi-segmental pattern with heat and moisture suggests a visceral source.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — cardiac-induced somatic dysfunction versus noncardiac musculoskeletal pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has recurrent episodes of palpitations. A cardiac workup, including ECG and ambulatory rhythm monitoring, shows no structural or electrical disease. Examination shows a restricted left second rib. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Treat the primary cardiac pathology and expect the rib restriction to resolve with it","B":"Treat the primary somatic dysfunction and expect the cardiac response to resolve","C":"Treat the heart and the stomach together as the two involved organs","D":"Treat the autonomic ganglia directly and expect the rib restriction to follow","E":"Treat the cranial base and expect the rib restriction to follow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A somatic dysfunction that alters organ function is a somatovisceral reflex, as in a second rib dysfunction associated with cardiac arrhythmia. Treatment is directed at the primary responsible somatic dysfunction, and OMT resolves the visceral response. Treating the underlying pathology is the focus for a viscerosomatic reflex, and treating both organs is the focus for a viscerovisceral reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic input can alter sympathetic output to the viscera. COMLEX: treat the somatic dysfunction when no visceral disease is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — the treatment target depends on the primary lesion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate between viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has marked gallbladder distension and an abrupt fall in heart rate and blood pressure. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Treat the primary somatic dysfunction and expect both organs to normalize","B":"Treat the paraspinal tissue changes and expect the organ interaction to resolve","C":"Treat the cranial base and expect both organs to normalize","D":"Treat both involved organs and expect their interaction to normalize","E":"Treat the heart as the primary lesion and defer attention to the gallbladder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Irritation or expansion of one organ that alters the function of another organ, shown by changes in organ blood flow, vital signs, or smooth muscle tone, is a viscerovisceral reflex. Its treatment focus is both involved visceral organs. Treating the primary somatic dysfunction is the focus for a somatovisceral reflex, and treating the underlying visceral pathology with OMT to the soma is the focus for a viscerosomatic reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: viscerovisceral reflexes pass through autonomic ganglia and the spinal cord. COMLEX: both organs need evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — differentiating viscerosomatic, somatovisceral, and viscerovisceral reflexes","isHighYield":false}]}]}]};